window.LMS_LOAD && window.LMS_LOAD("llmops-platform", {"topics":[{"key":"llmops-platform/ci-cd-for-prompts-and-models","sources":["Humble & Farley, 2010 — Continuous Delivery","Breck et al., 2017 — The ML Test Score: A Rubric for ML Production Readiness and Technical Debt Reduction","Sculley et al., 2015 — Hidden Technical Debt in Machine Learning Systems"],"why":"A conventional pipeline rests on three assumptions: the behaviour of the system is determined by the\ncode, tests are deterministic, and the bits that passed are the bits that run in production. An LLM\napplication violates all three. Behaviour is a function of code, prompt text, the model behind an API\nalias, decoding parameters and the retrieval index, and outputs are sampled rather than computed. The\nmodel can also change underneath you: a provider updates the model behind `-latest`, a training job\nproduces a new adapter, an index is re-embedded.\n\nTeams usually try one of three shortcuts first. Keeping prompts as string constants in the codebase\nputs them under review but gives no evidence: a PR that fixes the one complaint in the ticket regresses\nten other cases, and the regression is discovered by users. Letting people edit prompts live in a\ndashboard makes iteration fast but leaves no review, no rollback, and no record of which prompt produced\nwhich answer. Running the entire eval suite on every commit is the correct instinct done badly: thousands\nof model calls per push cost real money and take tens of minutes, so within a month the step is marked\noptional and skipped.\n\nThe practice that works treats prompts and models as first-class release artifacts: the pipeline knows\nwhich artifact changed, runs an eval of depth proportional to the risk of that stage, gates on aggregate\nquality rather than exact strings, and promotes the exact bundle it tested.","how":"### Three artifacts, three cadences\nApplication code changes daily and is covered by deterministic tests. Prompts and configuration change\nhourly to daily, are cheap to evaluate, and are often edited by product managers or forward-deployed\nengineers. Models change weekly to quarterly, are expensive to evaluate, and have capacity and cost\nconsequences. A release is therefore defined as a *bundle*: `{code sha, prompt versions, model id or\nweights revision, decoding params, index snapshot}`. The pipeline tests and promotes bundles, because\ntesting a prompt against one model and deploying it with another tests nothing.\n\n### The test ladder\nDepth grows with the blast radius of the stage, because cost and wall-clock time scale with model calls:\n\n1. **Static and unit tests (seconds, no model calls).** Every template renders with every declared\n   variable; output parsers run against recorded model outputs; tool JSON schemas validate; guardrail\n   rules compile; the rendered prompt stays under its token budget.\n2. **Contract tests with recorded responses.** Replay fixtures through the real code path to check\n   plumbing (retries, streaming, parsing). They prove the wiring, not the quality.\n3. **Smoke eval on every PR (minutes, a few dollars).** A stratified subset of the golden set, 50 to\n   200 items as an illustrative size, scored with deterministic checks where possible and an LLM judge\n   where necessary, compared against the baseline score recorded by the last green build of main.\n4. **Full eval on merge, nightly and on release candidates.** The whole golden set plus slices, cost\n   and latency, with every output stored as a build artifact so reviewers can diff behaviour.\n5. **Deploy with a canary** and online metrics, and an automatic rollback trigger.\n\n### Gating on distributions, not strings\nBecause outputs are sampled, exact-match assertions on free text flake. The pipeline compares score\ndistributions: a mean with a confidence interval against the champion, absolute floors for safety and\nschema validity, and per-slice floors. The stored outputs are the review artifact; the diff between the\ncandidate's and the champion's answers on the same items is what a reviewer actually reads. The\n`evaluation-gates-for-production-releases` lesson covers the statistics.\n\n### Content-addressed caching\nEval cost is dominated by model calls, so the cache sits at the model-client boundary and every call is\nkeyed by `hash(model id, decoding params, exact rendered request)`, where the rendered request is the full\nmessage list (prompt text, retrieved context, tool definitions) the application actually sends. Keying on\nthe rendered request rather than on `(prompt version, item input)` matters: a code or index change that\nalters what gets retrieved or how the prompt is assembled changes the request and therefore misses the\ncache, whereas a key built only from the item's input would serve the old output and hide the change. A\nPR that touches one prompt re-runs only the items whose requests that prompt affects; a code-only PR that\nleaves every request byte-identical re-runs nothing and just re-scores. This is what makes a per-PR eval\naffordable, and it is also why the cache key must include every pin: a key that omits the model id will\nhappily serve stale outputs after a model upgrade and hide the change. One consequence to accept\nknowingly: with temperature above zero, a cache hit replays one frozen sample, so the cached smoke eval\nmeasures that sample, not the sampling variance.\n\n### Promote, do not rebuild\nThe bundle that passed the release eval is promoted dev → staging → production by moving a pointer in\nthe prompt and model registries, never by rebuilding at deploy time, because a rebuild re-resolves\nfloating aliases (`-latest`, `main`) and can ship something that was never tested.\n\n### Prompts as code versus prompts as config\nWith prompts in the repository, prompt changes ride the normal PR pipeline; simple, reviewable, but\nnon-engineers are blocked and prompt deploys are coupled to code deploys. With a prompt registry, the\nregistry is the source of truth, a label change is the deploy, and the pipeline is triggered by a\nregistry webhook to run the same eval gate before the `production` label moves. Most mature teams land\non the hybrid: registry as source of truth, pipeline as the only thing allowed to move production labels.\n\n### When the yardstick changes\nA baseline is only meaningful relative to the dataset version and scorer version it was measured with.\nAdding twenty items to the golden set, relabeling a slice, or upgrading the judge model changes every\nscore without any change to the bundle, so the pipeline treats those as their own kind of change: it\nre-runs the current champion under the new yardstick, writes a new baseline tagged with the dataset hash\nand scorer version, and only then compares candidates. A baseline file that records its own dataset hash\nand scorer version lets the job refuse a comparison that would otherwise blame a prompt for a judge\nupgrade. For the same reason, changes to `data/golden/**` or to scorer code should trigger the eval job\ntoo.\n\n### Model changes are releases too\nPin the model snapshot in the bundle. Run a scheduled \"model upgrade\" job that opens a PR with the new\nsnapshot and the full eval when the provider announces a new version or a deprecation; treat a retrained\nadapter arriving in the model registry the same way. The trigger differs; the ladder is identical.","example":"A PR-time smoke eval that sits as one job in the pipeline. The YAML fragment shows where it runs; the\nscript shows the content-addressed cache wrapped around the model client and the comparison against the\nbaseline written by the last green build of `main`, refused if that baseline used a different dataset or\nscorer. `call_app`, `llm_call`, `score` and `SCORER_VERSION` are your application's entrypoint (accepting\nan injected model client), your raw provider call, your scorer and its version string. With a 100-item\nsmoke set and a warm cache, a PR that edits one prompt used by 30 items pays for 30 application model calls\n(and 30 judge calls, if a judge scorer goes through the same cache), not 100 of each.\n\n```yaml\n# .github/workflows/llm-ci.yml (fragment): trigger on anything that changes the bundle\non:\n  pull_request:\n    paths: [\"app/**\", \"prompts/**\", \"release.json\", \"data/golden/**\", \"evals/scorers/**\"]\njobs:\n  smoke-eval:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v4\n      - run: python scripts/smoke_eval.py --subset smoke   # nightly job runs --subset full\n```\n\n```python\n# scripts/smoke_eval.py\nimport hashlib, json, pathlib, sys\n\nbundle = json.load(open(\"release.json\"))   # {\"model\": \"vendor-model-2025-04-14\", \"prompt\": \"support_answer@v12\", \"params\": {...}}\nsubset = sys.argv[sys.argv.index(\"--subset\") + 1]\ndata = pathlib.Path(f\"data/golden/{subset}.jsonl\").read_bytes()\nitems = [json.loads(l) for l in data.splitlines()]\nyardstick = {\"dataset\": hashlib.sha256(data).hexdigest()[:12], \"scorer\": SCORER_VERSION}\ncache = pathlib.Path(\".evalcache\"); cache.mkdir(exist_ok=True)\n\ndef cached_llm(messages):   # key = model + params + the exact rendered request, so any change that alters it misses\n    blob = json.dumps([bundle[\"model\"], bundle[\"params\"], messages], sort_keys=True)\n    p = cache / hashlib.sha256(blob.encode()).hexdigest()\n    if p.exists():\n        return json.loads(p.read_text())\n    out = llm_call(bundle[\"model\"], bundle[\"params\"], messages)\n    p.write_text(json.dumps(out)); return out\n\ndef run_item(item):\n    out = call_app(bundle, item[\"input\"], llm=cached_llm)   # the production code path; only its model calls are cached\n    return {\"id\": item[\"id\"], \"output\": out, \"score\": score(item, out)}\n\nresults = [run_item(i) for i in items]\nmean = sum(r[\"score\"] for r in results) / len(results)\nbase = json.load(open(f\"eval_baselines/main-{subset}.json\"))   # written by the last green build of main\nif base[\"yardstick\"] != yardstick:                              # new items or a new judge: scores are not comparable\n    sys.exit(\"baseline was measured with a different dataset or scorer; re-baseline the champion first\")\njson.dump({\"bundle\": bundle, \"yardstick\": yardstick, \"mean\": mean, \"items\": results}, open(\"eval_out.json\", \"w\"))\nprint(f\"candidate={mean:.3f} baseline={base['mean']:.3f} n={len(results)}\")\nsys.exit(0 if mean >= base[\"mean\"] - 0.02 else 1)   # smoke tolerance; the release gate uses a paired CI instead\n```","tradeoffs":["Per-PR smoke evals catch gross regressions in minutes, but a 100-item subset cannot resolve a one- or two-point change; the moment you need that resolution you must pay for the full set or a canary.","Caching makes evals cheap and fast, yet every omitted pin in the cache key becomes a way to hide a real change; the more aggressive the cache, the more disciplined the key must be.","Prompts-as-config decouples prompt deploys from code deploys and unblocks non-engineers, at the cost of a second deploy path that must be gated as strictly as the first or it becomes the bypass everyone uses.","Gating on aggregate scores tolerates sampling noise, but it also tolerates an individual catastrophic answer; safety and schema checks therefore need absolute per-item floors, not averages.","Pinning model snapshots gives reproducible builds, but snapshots get deprecated; without a scheduled upgrade job, pinning turns into an emergency migration on the provider's timetable."],"pitfalls":["Running evals against a copy of the prompt chain inside the test harness instead of the production entrypoint; the symptom is a green pipeline and a production regression caused by code the eval never executed.","Comparing the candidate against a fixed threshold rather than the current champion; quality erodes a point per release while every release passes, and nobody notices until a customer does.","Retrying a flaky eval until it goes green; the symptom is a gate whose pass rate is uncorrelated with quality and a team that has stopped reading the numbers.","Letting the `production` label in the registry be moved from the UI without the pipeline; the symptom is an incident whose timeline contains a prompt change with no PR, no eval and no author.","Using `-latest` model aliases in the bundle; the symptom is a score change on a day with no commits, traced eventually to a provider update.","Comparing against a baseline measured on an older golden-set or judge version; the symptom is every PR failing (or passing) by the same few points the day the dataset grew, with no prompt to blame."],"when":"Introduce this the moment more than one person edits prompts or a prompt is deployed to real users:\nthat is when \"we changed something and it got worse\" becomes unanswerable. Start with the cheapest\nrungs, unit tests on templates and parsers plus a 50-item smoke eval against a stored baseline, and add\nthe full suite and canary when release frequency or user impact justifies them. Measure pipeline cost\nper PR, eval wall-clock time, cache hit rate and, most importantly, the number of production regressions\nthat the pipeline would have caught. In interviews the expected answer to \"how do you deploy a prompt\nchange safely\" is this ladder: unit, recorded-response contract, smoke eval versus baseline, full eval\nwith gates, canary with rollback, with the bundle, not the prompt, as the unit of release.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds the smoke-eval job, the content-addressed cache and the baseline file, and keeps the harness calling the real application entrypoint rather than a reimplementation.","architect":"Decides that the bundle is the unit of release, which registry owns each artifact, and that the pipeline is the only path allowed to move a production label.","fde":"Reproduces a lightweight version of the ladder inside the customer's CI, often with the customer's own golden set, so their change-management process has evidence rather than a demo."},"check":{"q":"A product manager edits a system prompt in the registry UI at 4 pm and moves the `production`\nlabel; latency is unchanged but support tickets about wrong refund amounts double overnight. The code\npipeline is green. What went wrong structurally, and how would you change the pipeline so it cannot\nhappen again?","a":"Structurally, the prompt had a deploy path that bypassed every test: the code pipeline only runs on\ncommits, and the registry label move was an unreviewed, un-evaluated production change. The fix is not\nto forbid non-engineers from editing prompts but to make the label move itself the thing the pipeline\ngates. Registry edits create a new immutable version; a webhook or scheduled job runs the smoke eval and,\nfor `production`, the full eval and gates against the champion on the same golden set, including a\nrefund-intent slice with a floor; only the pipeline's service account may move the production label,\nand it writes the eval evidence next to the version. Rollback is moving the label back. Finally, add the\nincident's cases to the golden set so the regression is a permanent test."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does the PR stage run a stratified subset of the golden set rather than the full suite?","options":["Subsets are statistically more reliable than the full set","Eval cost and time scale with model calls, so depth is matched to the blast radius of each stage","Full suites can only be run against production models","GitHub Actions cannot run jobs longer than ten minutes"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Every item is a model call that costs money and time; a per-PR job must stay fast and cheap or it will be skipped. The full suite runs on merge, nightly and on release candidates where the stakes justify it."},{"q":"Why should the tested bundle be promoted by pointer rather than rebuilt at deploy time?","options":["Rebuilding is slower because weights must be downloaded again","Registries cannot store more than one version at a time","A rebuild re-resolves floating aliases and can deploy a model or prompt that was never evaluated","Promotion avoids the need for a canary stage"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The whole point of the gate is that the exact bits that passed are the bits that run. A rebuild that resolves `-latest` or `main` again may pick up a provider update or a prompt edit the eval never saw."},{"q":"What is the strongest reason to put the model snapshot id inside the eval cache key?","options":["It makes the cache directory easier to browse","Providers require it for billing attribution","Without it, a model upgrade would be served stale cached outputs and the change would be invisible","Hashing is cheaper when the key is longer"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The cache exists to skip calls whose inputs have not changed. If the model id is not part of the key, swapping the model counts as \"unchanged\" and the eval silently measures the old model's cached outputs."}]},{"key":"llmops-platform/prompt-registry","sources":["Official docs: Langfuse","Official docs: MLflow"],"why":"Prompts start life as string constants. That is fine for one engineer and one prompt. It stops being\nfine the first time a product manager wants to change the tone of the answer, a forward-deployed\nengineer needs a customer-specific variant, or an incident review asks \"which prompt produced this\nresponse at 02:14 on Tuesday\". With constants, every tweak is a code deploy, variants become nested\nif-statements, and the trace holds the rendered text at best and nothing at worst.\n\nThe naive upgrade, a `prompts` table edited from an admin page, solves the deploy problem and creates a\nworse one: a mutable row has no history, so a bad edit is indistinguishable from the previous good one,\nthere is no review step, no eval evidence attached to a change, and rollback means remembering what the\ntext used to be. Teams that go this route tend to discover the problem at 3 am.\n\nThe registry pattern separates the two things people conflate: the *content* of a prompt, which must be\nimmutable once created so that every past trace can be resolved forever, and the *pointer* that says\nwhich content a given environment uses, which must be mutable so that deploy and rollback are a pointer\nmove. It is the same split a model registry makes between versions and aliases, applied to text.","how":"### Data model\nA **prompt** is a named entity (`support_answer`). Each **version** is immutable and holds the template\nbody, usually a list of chat messages with roles rather than a single string, the declared variables and\ntheir types, the model settings it was tuned against (model id, temperature, max tokens, output schema),\na content digest, the author, a change note and a created timestamp. Versions are append-only because\na trace that recorded `support_answer@v12` must resolve to the same text in a year.\n\nA **label** is a mutable pointer from `(prompt, label)` to a version: `production`, `staging`, `canary`,\nor tenant-scoped labels such as `tenant:acme`. Deploy is a label move; rollback is the same operation in\nreverse; an A/B test is two labels resolved by a routing rule. Labels are mutable because the\nalternative, deploying code to change which version runs, is exactly what the registry exists to avoid.\n\nAttached to a version, not a label, are the **evidence** records: eval run fingerprints and scores on a\nnamed golden-set version, and links to the review that approved it. Evidence belongs to content, not to\npointers, because the question \"was this text tested\" has one answer regardless of where it is deployed.\n\nLabel moves are themselves recorded as an append-only log (`who`, `from`, `to`, `when`, `why`), *because*\nrollback needs to know what the label pointed at before, and an audit needs to know what was live at\n02:14 on Tuesday, which is a question about the label's history, not about any version. Moves should be\ncompare-and-set (\"move `production` from v12 to v13, only if it is still v12\") so that two people\npromoting at once cannot silently overwrite each other.\n\n### Prompt and model are a pair\nA prompt is tuned to a model. The same text that works on one model family may over-refuse, ignore the\nschema or change tone on another, so a version stores model id and decoding parameters alongside the\ntext. Swapping the model under an unchanged prompt is a new bundle and must be re-evaluated; the registry\nmakes that visible by refusing to pretend the text alone is the artifact.\n\n### Runtime fetch\nThe application fetches by name and label at request time through an SDK that keeps an in-memory copy\nwith a short TTL, refreshes in the background, and serves the stale copy if the registry is unreachable.\nA bundled copy of the production versions ships with the application as a last-resort fallback. The\nregistry is therefore never on the hot path in the sense that its outage cannot take the product down,\nwhich is the condition under which it is acceptable to let it control behaviour at all.\n\nEvery model call logs the prompt name, version and digest into the trace. Observability platforms that\ninclude prompt management, Langfuse being the common open-source example, link a trace to the prompt\nversion automatically, which turns \"find every answer produced by v12\" into a filter.\n\nThe TTL sets the propagation delay of every label move, rollback included: with a 60-second TTL and\nfifty replicas, for up to a minute after a move some replicas serve the old version and some the new.\nThat is fine for a planned deploy and too slow for an emergency rollback of a harmful prompt, which is why\nsome SDKs and home-grown registries add a push invalidation (a webhook or pub/sub message that evicts the\ncached entry) on top of the TTL.\n\n### Change workflow\nCreate a draft version, run the eval suite against it (the registry or the CI pipeline triggers this),\nattach the results, get a review, move the label. The declared variables are an interface between the\nprompt and the code that renders it: a version that adds `{{customer_tier}}` can only be labeled\n`production` once the deployed code supplies that variable, so the label move should validate the\nversion's variable set against what the running application declares it provides, and rendering should\nfail loudly on a missing variable rather than send the literal placeholder to the model. The pipeline's service account should be the only\nprincipal allowed to move `production`, so that an edit in the UI can create a version but cannot\ndeploy it. A diff view between two versions, both text and settings, is the review artifact.\n\n### Where to host it\nPrompts in a git directory with a build step give review and history for free but couple prompt\ndeploys to code deploys unless the application loads them from an object store at runtime. Hosted\nregistries in observability or eval platforms (Langfuse, LangSmith, Braintrust; MLflow added a prompt\nregistry in 2025, first as an experimental feature in the 2.x line and expanded in 3.x) give labels, a UI for non-engineers and trace linkage. A home-grown table with\nversions and labels is a few hundred lines and is often the right answer for a small platform team that\nalready owns a config service. What matters is the split between immutable versions and mutable labels,\nnot the vendor.","example":"A minimal registry showing the three operations that matter: register an immutable version (identical\ncontent maps to the existing version, so re-registering is idempotent), move a label with compare-and-set\nand a move log, and fetch with a TTL cache that serves stale on failure. A real deployment backs `store`\nwith a database and adds the bundled-file fallback for a cold start while the registry is down.\n\n```python\nimport hashlib, json, time\n\nclass PromptRegistry:\n    def __init__(self, store):                 # store: {\"versions\": {name: [...]}, \"labels\": {(name, label): {...}}}\n        self.store, self._cache = store, {}\n\n    def register(self, name, messages, model, params, author, note):\n        body = json.dumps({\"messages\": messages, \"model\": model, \"params\": params}, sort_keys=True)\n        digest = hashlib.sha256(body.encode()).hexdigest()[:12]\n        versions = self.store[\"versions\"].setdefault(name, [])\n        for v in versions:\n            if v[\"digest\"] == digest:\n                return v[\"version\"]            # same content, same version: registering is idempotent\n        v = {\"version\": len(versions) + 1, \"digest\": digest, \"messages\": messages, \"model\": model,\n             \"params\": params, \"author\": author, \"note\": note, \"created\": time.time(), \"evals\": {}}\n        versions.append(v)                     # append-only: a version is never edited or deleted\n        return v[\"version\"]\n\n    def set_label(self, name, label, version, by, expected):   # deploy and rollback are both this one call\n        cur = self.store[\"labels\"].get((name, label), {}).get(\"version\")\n        if cur != expected:                    # compare-and-set: a concurrent move must not be clobbered\n            raise RuntimeError(f\"{name}:{label} is v{cur}, not v{expected}\")\n        self.store[\"labels\"][(name, label)] = {\"version\": version, \"by\": by, \"at\": time.time()}\n        self.store.setdefault(\"moves\", []).append((name, label, cur, version, by, time.time()))  # rollback target\n\n    def get(self, name, label=\"production\", ttl=60):\n        now, hit = time.time(), self._cache.get((name, label))\n        if hit and now - hit[\"at\"] < ttl:\n            return hit[\"v\"]\n        try:\n            ver = self.store[\"labels\"][(name, label)][\"version\"]\n            v = self.store[\"versions\"][name][ver - 1]\n            self._cache[(name, label)] = {\"v\": v, \"at\": now}\n            return v\n        except Exception:\n            if hit:\n                return hit[\"v\"]                # registry down: serve stale rather than fail the request\n            raise\n\nreg = PromptRegistry({\"versions\": {}, \"labels\": {}})\nv1 = reg.register(\"support_answer\",\n                  [{\"role\": \"system\", \"content\": \"Answer using only the provided context: {{context}}\"},\n                   {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"{{question}}\"}],\n                  model=\"vendor-model-2025-04-14\",        # a dated snapshot id, never a floating alias\n                  params={\"temperature\": 0.2, \"max_tokens\": 600}, author=\"ana\", note=\"initial\")\nreg.set_label(\"support_answer\", \"production\", v1, by=\"ci:release-42\", expected=None)\np = reg.get(\"support_answer\")\n# Log p[\"version\"] and p[\"digest\"] on every trace so any answer resolves to its exact prompt.\n```","tradeoffs":["Decoupling prompt deploys from code deploys lets non-engineers iterate in minutes, but it creates a second production change path that must be gated as strictly as code or it becomes the bypass.","Immutable versions guarantee every trace resolves forever, at the cost of a growing store and a retention policy; for most teams prompts are kilobytes and this never matters, for multi-tenant platforms with thousands of variants it does.","Runtime fetching keeps behaviour changeable without redeploy, but it adds a dependency; the TTL cache and bundled fallback remove the availability risk while introducing a propagation delay of one TTL.","Storing model settings with the prompt makes the pair explicit and re-evaluation mandatory on model change; it also means a platform-wide model migration touches every prompt version rather than one config line.","A hosted registry gives UI, labels and trace linkage out of the box; a git directory gives review and blame for free. The hosted option wins once people outside engineering own prompts."],"pitfalls":["Editing a version in place \"because it is just a typo\"; the symptom is two traces with the same version id and different rendered text, which destroys every post-incident analysis that relies on the id.","Resolving labels at deploy time and baking the text into the image; the symptom is a label move that changes nothing until the next deploy, and an engineer who concludes the registry is broken.","Logging only the prompt name, not the version and digest; the symptom is an investigation that knows which prompt family failed and cannot tell whether it was before or after Tuesday's change.","Treating the registry as prompt-only and leaving temperature and model id in application config; the symptom is a prompt that passed its eval behaving differently in production because the two were tuned apart.","Allowing the `production` label to be moved from the UI by anyone with edit rights; the symptom is a behaviour change with no pipeline run and no evidence attached.","Promoting a version whose new template variable the deployed code does not yet supply; the symptom is model inputs containing a literal `{{customer_tier}}` or an empty slot, and answers that ignore the tier."],"when":"Reach for a registry as soon as a second person edits prompts, a prompt needs per-tenant or per-locale\nvariants, or an audit or incident process needs to tie an answer to the exact text that produced it.\nBefore that point a prompts directory in the repository with versioned file names is enough. Measure the\nthings the registry is supposed to deliver: time from prompt edit to production, number of versions with\nattached eval evidence, and whether every production trace resolves to a version. In interviews, the\nexpected depth is the immutable-version versus mutable-label split, why evidence attaches to versions,\nhow the runtime fetch avoids becoming a single point of failure, and why the prompt and its model\nsettings are versioned together.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Integrates the SDK with TTL caching and fallback, logs version and digest on every trace, and makes the eval pipeline run on new versions automatically.","architect":"Decides where the registry lives, who may move which labels, how tenant variants are scoped, and that prompt plus model settings is the versioned unit.","fde":"Uses tenant labels to ship customer-specific prompt variants without forking the product, and shows the customer's change-control team the version history and attached evidence."},"check":{"q":"Your registry stores only prompt text, and application config holds `temperature=0.7`. A platform\nteam migrates the whole fleet to a new model snapshot by changing one config line, and a legal-summary\nprompt that passed its evals last month starts omitting mandatory disclaimers. Explain what the registry\ndesign got wrong and what the version record should have contained.","a":"The registry treated text as the artifact when the artifact is the pair of text and the model\nsettings it was evaluated against. Because the model id lived outside the version, the migration did not\ncreate a new version, triggered no eval, and left every trace pointing at a version id that now means\nsomething different in practice. The version record should hold the model snapshot id, temperature,\nmax tokens and output schema alongside the messages, with a digest over all of it; a model migration\nthen becomes a new version per prompt, each of which must pass the eval suite and be promoted by label.\nThe migration gets slower by exactly the amount of evidence it was skipping, and the disclaimer\nregression would have been caught by the legal-summary golden set before the label moved."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why must prompt versions be immutable while labels are mutable?","options":["Immutable versions compress better in storage","Traces must resolve to the exact text forever, while deploy and rollback need to be a pointer move","Labels are immutable too; only drafts can change","Mutable versions would break template rendering"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Immutability of content keeps past traces meaningful and evidence attached to the right text; mutability of the pointer is what lets a deploy or rollback happen without shipping code."},{"q":"An application fetches prompts from the registry on every request with no cache. What is the main risk?","options":["Prompts will be rendered with stale variables","Versions will be created on every request","A registry outage or slowdown takes the product down with it","Labels cannot be resolved without a cache"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Runtime fetching is acceptable only when the registry cannot become a single point of failure, which is what the TTL cache, background refresh and bundled fallback provide."},{"q":"Where should eval results for a prompt be attached, and why?","options":["To the label, because that is what production uses","To the version, because evidence describes the content regardless of where it is deployed","To the trace, because that is where the answer appears","To the application release, because prompts ship with code"],"answer":1,"explanation":"\"Was this text tested\" has a single answer per version. Attaching it to a label would lose the evidence every time the label moved and would let an untested version inherit a tested label's history."}]},{"key":"llmops-platform/model-registry","sources":["Official docs: MLflow","Official docs: Hugging Face Hub","Mitchell et al., 2019 — Model Cards for Model Reporting"],"why":"Without a registry, models live as files in a bucket with names like `adapter_final_v2_really.safetensors`.\nNobody can say with confidence which file is serving, a retraining job produces new weights that someone\ncopies into place by hand, rolling back means finding the old file and hoping it is the right one, and\nthe first compliance question, \"which data was this trained on\", has no answer. For LLM applications the\nproblem is sharper because the thing you deploy is rarely one file: it is a base model identity, a LoRA\nadapter, a quantization choice, a tokenizer revision and a serving configuration, and each of these can\ndrift independently while all of them still look like \"the model\".\n\nThe naive alternatives fail in predictable ways. \"Put it in git\" does not survive multi-gigabyte weights\neven with LFS, and git has no notion of lineage to a training run or an evaluation. \"Deploy the newest\nfile in the bucket\" makes deploys non-deterministic: two replicas started an hour apart can serve\ndifferent models. \"Keep a spreadsheet\" is the registry pattern with no enforcement, and it is wrong\nwithin a week.\n\nThe registry fixes this by borrowing the two ideas every package manager already uses: immutable\nversions with content hashes, and mutable named pointers that environments resolve. It then adds the\nthings models need that packages do not: lineage, evaluation evidence, a model card and an approval\nstate.","how":"### Entities\nA **registered model** is a name, such as `support-classifier`. Each **version** is immutable and\nrecords the artifact URI and content hash of the weights, the format or flavour (a Transformers\ncheckpoint, a LoRA adapter, an ONNX graph, a pyfunc wrapper), an input and output signature, the\nparameters and metrics of the run that produced it, lineage (tracking run id, training dataset version,\ncode commit, parent base model and its revision), free-form tags, a description or model card, and an\napproval state. Versions are immutable because a deployment that resolved version 7 must mean the same\nbytes next year, and because evaluation evidence attached to a version is meaningless if the bytes can\nchange underneath it.\n\nAn **alias** is a mutable pointer from a name to a version: `champion`, `challenger`, `staging`,\n`tenant-acme`. MLflow's registry originally used a fixed set of stages (Staging, Production, Archived);\nnewer versions replaced them with free-form aliases plus tags, because fixed stages could not express\n\"two production models for two tenants\" or \"the canary candidate\". Promotion is setting an alias;\nrollback is setting it back to the previous version.\n\n### What counts as a model in an LLM application\nThe registry should hold every artifact whose change alters model behaviour: fine-tuned weights and\nLoRA adapters, quantized variants (a version per quantization, because they are evaluated separately),\nembedding models (whose version must match the vector index built with them), reward or judge models,\nand provider-hosted models. The last case matters: a \"virtual\" version that records only a provider\nsnapshot id and decoding config gives the API-based model the same lifecycle, evidence and rollback\nstory as self-hosted weights. A release bundle then references a model version and a prompt version;\nsome teams register the bundle itself as a model so that one alias move changes both.\n\n### Lifecycle\nRegister from a tracking run, so lineage is automatic. Attach the eval run fingerprint and scores on a\nnamed golden-set version. Request review; the reviewer reads the model card, the diff against the\nchampion's metrics and the slice results. Promote by alias, deploy, observe the canary, promote to\n`champion`. Later, deprecate: mark the version archived, keep the bytes for the retention period the\nlifecycle policy specifies, and refuse new deployments of it.\n\n### Serving integration\nA serving configuration references `models:/support-classifier@champion`. At process start the server\nresolves the alias to a version, downloads the artifact by hash, verifies it, and logs the resolved\nversion into every trace and response header. Resolving once at startup rather than on every request\nis deliberate: a running replica never changes model mid-flight, and a rollout happens by starting new\nreplicas against the new alias target, which is what a canary needs. For embedding models the same\nmechanism prevents the classic bug of querying an index built with embedder v2 using embedder v3: the\nindex build records the embedder version and the query path refuses a mismatch.\n\nRollback is a pointer move in the registry, but in serving it is a new rollout, so its real duration is\nthe time to start replicas on the previous version: download, verify and load. For an API model that is\nseconds; for tens of gigabytes of self-hosted weights it can be many minutes, which is why teams keep the\nprevious champion's artifact cached on serving nodes (or keep a few previous-version replicas warm)\nuntil the new champion has survived its canary window. Measure rollback time end to end; the alias move\nis the fast part.\n\n### Hugging Face Hub as a registry\nFor open weights the Hub is a git-backed registry: each repository has a commit history, a model card in\nthe README, and `from_pretrained(..., revision=\"<commit sha>\")` pins an exact version. Pinning to a\nbranch name such as `main` is the same mistake as a floating `-latest` alias. Many teams mirror the pinned\nrevision into their own registry so that an upstream deletion or license change cannot break a deploy.\n\n### Governance\nAccess control on alias moves, signed or hash-verified artifacts, a model card per version, and a record\nof who approved what are the parts auditors and security reviewers ask for. They are cheap when designed\nin and expensive to retrofit.","example":"The registry lifecycle with MLflow's client API: register a version from a tracked run, attach the\nevidence a reviewer needs, promote by alias, and have the server resolve the alias once at startup.\nTag values are illustrative; the lineage fields are what matter.\n\n```python\nimport mlflow\nfrom mlflow import MlflowClient\n\nclient = MlflowClient()\nNAME = \"support-classifier\"\n\n# 1. Register the artifact a tracked run produced; the run id is the lineage link to params, data and code\nrun_id = \"3f1c0a9e2b7d\"                                          # the run that trained the LoRA adapter\nmv = mlflow.register_model(f\"runs:/{run_id}/model\", NAME)        # returns a new immutable version number\n\n# 2. Attach the evidence a reviewer and, later, an auditor will ask for\nclient.set_model_version_tag(NAME, mv.version, \"base_model\", \"meta-llama/Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct\")\nclient.set_model_version_tag(NAME, mv.version, \"base_revision\", \"<hf commit sha>\")   # pinned, not 'main'\nclient.set_model_version_tag(NAME, mv.version, \"golden_set\", \"golden-v8@5c2e91\")\nclient.set_model_version_tag(NAME, mv.version, \"eval_fingerprint\", \"run:9a71cc\")\nclient.set_model_version_tag(NAME, mv.version, \"eval_accuracy\", \"0.912\")\nclient.update_model_version(NAME, mv.version,\n                            description=\"Retrained on Q3 tickets; fixes refund-intent confusion\")\n\n# 3. Promote by moving a pointer; rollback is this same call with the previous version number\nclient.set_registered_model_alias(NAME, \"challenger\", mv.version)\n# ... canary healthy for the agreed window ...\nclient.set_registered_model_alias(NAME, \"champion\", mv.version)\n\n# 4. The serving process resolves the alias once at startup and records what it got\nresolved = client.get_model_version_by_alias(NAME, \"champion\")\nprint(\"serving\", NAME, \"version\", resolved.version, \"from\", resolved.source)   # log into every trace\nmodel = mlflow.pyfunc.load_model(f\"models:/{NAME}/{resolved.version}\")   # load the version just resolved, not the\n                                                                          # alias again: it may move in between\n```","tradeoffs":["Immutable versions plus aliases give deterministic deploys and one-call rollback, at the cost of storage for every version ever promoted; a retention policy matters once weights are tens of gigabytes.","Resolving the alias at startup keeps replicas stable and rollouts explicit, but a hot swap without restart is impossible by design; teams that want instant global switches must accept replicas that disagree for a while.","Registering provider-hosted models as virtual versions gives API models the same lifecycle, but it only helps if application code reads the model id from the registry rather than from its own config.","A full registry with approvals and signing satisfies auditors and security; for a two-person team with one model it is ceremony, and a pinned artifact URI in a config file is the honest minimum.","Registering the whole bundle (model plus prompt) as one versioned unit makes promotion atomic, but it multiplies versions whenever either half changes and blurs which half caused a metric move."],"pitfalls":["Pointing serving config at a branch or `latest` tag instead of a version or alias; the symptom is two replicas returning different answers for the same input after a rolling restart.","Registering weights without the tokenizer and serving config; the symptom is a version that scores well in the eval harness and produces garbage in production because the tokenizer revision differs.","Letting the embedding model version advance without rebuilding the index; the symptom is retrieval quality collapsing after a \"routine\" model upgrade while every service reports healthy.","Attaching eval results to the alias rather than the version; the symptom is a rolled-back champion that inherits the evidence of the version that replaced it.","Resolving the alias and then loading by alias again in a second call; the symptom is a replica whose logged version differs from the weights it actually loaded, because a promotion landed between the calls.","Mirroring nothing from the Hub and pinning only to a repository name; the symptom is a broken deploy the day the upstream author force-pushes, gates the repository or changes the license."],"when":"Introduce a registry the first time a model is retrained, quantized or swapped after launch, or the\nfirst time two environments need different models; before then a pinned artifact URI with a hash in\nconfig is sufficient and honest. Include provider-hosted models as virtual versions once an API model\nswap would be a user-visible change, which is nearly always. Measure whether every serving replica logs\na resolved version, how long a rollback takes end to end, and whether every promoted version carries\neval evidence and a lineage link. In interviews, expect to explain immutable versions versus aliases,\nwhy aliases are resolved at startup, how the registry prevents an embedder-index mismatch, and how\nprovenance flows from a tracking run into a registered version and out into a trace.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Registers versions from training runs, wires the server to resolve aliases at startup and log the resolved version, and refuses index queries whose embedder version mismatches.","architect":"Defines what counts as a model artifact, the alias vocabulary per environment and tenant, the approval and retention policy, and whether bundles or components are the unit of promotion.","fde":"Keeps a customer-scoped registry or alias set so that a fine-tuned adapter trained on one client's data can never be promoted into another tenant's serving path."},"check":{"q":"A team serves a RAG system with an open embedding model loaded from a Hub repository's `main`\nbranch. One morning retrieval quality drops sharply, no deploy happened, and every health check is\ngreen. Walk through the likely cause and how a registry would have prevented it.","a":"The likely cause is that the upstream repository received a new commit on `main`, so replicas that\nrestarted overnight loaded a different embedding model than the one that built the index; query vectors\nand index vectors now live in different spaces, nearest neighbours are nonsense, and nothing errors\nbecause the shapes still match. A registry prevents this in three places: the embedder is a registered\nversion pinned to a commit hash and mirrored, so a restart loads identical bytes; the index build\nrecords the embedder version it used; and the query path checks that the resolved embedder version\nequals the index's recorded version and refuses to serve, or alerts, on mismatch. The fix is to pin the\nrevision, rebuild or roll back, and add the mismatch check so the failure becomes loud."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does a serving process resolve the `champion` alias at startup rather than on each request?","options":["Alias lookups are too slow for request latency budgets","A replica should never change model mid-flight; rollouts happen by starting new replicas, which is what a canary needs","Registries only allow one resolution per client","Per-request resolution would bypass the model cache"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Resolving once makes each replica's behaviour stable and attributable; a change in the alias target becomes a controlled rollout instead of an instantaneous, unobservable fleet-wide switch."},{"q":"What does registering a provider-hosted model as a \"virtual version\" buy you?","options":["Lower API cost through registry-level caching","The ability to fine-tune the provider's weights locally","The same lifecycle, evidence and rollback story for API models as for self-hosted weights","Automatic migration when the provider deprecates a snapshot"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The version holds a snapshot id and decoding config, so swapping the API model is a promoted, evaluated change with a rollback, not a config edit nobody can trace."},{"q":"Which lineage field is most important for an auditor asking \"what was this model trained on\"?","options":["The alias the model currently holds","The artifact storage region","The link from the version to the tracking run and its dataset version","The model's parameter count"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Lineage flows from the training run, which recorded the dataset version, code commit and params, into the registered version; without that link the question has no verifiable answer."}]},{"key":"llmops-platform/experiment-tracking","sources":["Official docs: MLflow","Official docs: Weights & Biases","Pineau et al., 2021 — Improving Reproducibility in Machine Learning Research"],"why":"Any team that trains, fine-tunes or evaluates models produces runs faster than it can remember them.\nThe first week it is a spreadsheet of learning rates and scores; by the third week the spreadsheet is\nmissing the run everyone is talking about, nobody knows which checkpoint produced the 0.91, and the\nconfig that got it was edited in place since. Fine-tuning makes this combinatorial: LoRA rank, learning\nrate, data mix, epochs and base model revision interact, and a result without its full configuration\ncannot be reproduced or trusted.\n\nLLM application work has the same shape without the training loop. Every evaluation run is an\nexperiment: a prompt version, a model snapshot, a dataset version and a judge configuration go in, and\nscores come out. Teams that keep those results in notebook cells and Slack screenshots cannot answer\n\"did the last three prompt changes help on the refund slice\" without re-running everything.\n\nThe naive alternative, printing to logs and encoding settings in file names, fails because comparison\nand lineage are the whole job: you need to filter a thousand runs by parameter, overlay metric curves,\nand follow a model version back to the run, data and commit that produced it. That is a database with\na specific schema and a UI on top, which is what a tracker is.","how":"### The data model both tools share\nAn **experiment** (MLflow) or **project** (W&B) groups **runs**. A run has **parameters** fixed at start\n(treated as immutable, because changing them mid-run would make comparisons meaningless; MLflow rejects\nre-logging a param with a different value, while W&B lets `config` be updated but that is an escape hatch,\nnot the model), **metrics** logged as time\nseries against a step or epoch (so you can see divergence or over-fitting at step *k*, not just the\nfinal number), **artifacts** which are files (checkpoints, per-item eval outputs, plots, configs),\n**tags** for free-form filtering, and automatically captured context: git commit, command line, Python\nenvironment and, where enabled, system metrics such as GPU utilisation (W&B records them by default;\nMLflow does once system-metrics logging is switched on). Runs can be nested for hyperparameter sweeps.\nEverything is queryable, so \"all runs where `lora_r=16` sorted by `eval_acc`\" is one call.\n\n### What happens when you call `log`\nBoth clients are thin writers to a store, and their transport explains several sharp edges. MLflow's\nclient sends each logging call to the tracking server over REST (or writes to a local `mlruns`\ndirectory with no server), and the server writes rows into its SQL backend: params as key/value\n*strings*, metrics as `(key, value, timestamp, step)` rows, plus a \"latest value\" per metric that the\nrun table and search use. Batch calls (`log_metrics`, `log_params`) exist *because* one HTTP round trip\nper scalar at every training step is slow; recent versions also offer asynchronous logging. W&B's\n`init` starts a background process that buffers everything to a local run directory and streams it to\nthe server, *because* a training loop must never block on the network; the same design gives offline\nmode (`WANDB_MODE=offline`, uploaded later with `wandb sync`) for air-gapped clusters. Artifacts take a\nseparate path: large files go to the artifact store (bucket or W&B storage) and only a reference lands\nin the run.\n\nThree consequences follow. MLflow params are strings, so a filter such as `params.lr > 0.001` is not a\nnumeric comparison; put numbers you will want to range-query into metrics or tags you control. MLflow's\nsearch and run table sort on a metric's *last* logged value, not its best, so log a separate\n`best_eval_acc` if that is what you will rank by. W&B requires the `step` passed to `log` to increase\nmonotonically; a call with an earlier step is dropped with a warning, which bites when resuming a run or\nlogging evaluation results after the training loop has advanced.\n\n### MLflow\nMLflow is Apache-licensed open source that started at Databricks. Its tracking server has a backend\nstore (a SQL database for runs, params and metrics) and an artifact store (local disk, S3, GCS or Azure\nBlob for files), which is why self-hosting is a container plus a database plus a bucket. Around tracking\nit adds a model registry with versions and aliases, a packaging format with \"flavours\" (`pyfunc`,\n`transformers`, `sklearn` and others) that lets any registered model be loaded with one call, and\nautologging integrations that capture params and metrics from common training frameworks without\nexplicit calls. Through the later 2.x releases and the 3.x line it added GenAI features — tracing, LLM\nevaluation APIs and a prompt registry — so an LLM team can keep evals and prompts in the same store as\ntraining runs. Databricks offers a managed version, and some cloud ML platforms (Amazon SageMaker, for\nexample) offer managed MLflow tracking servers; otherwise you self-host.\n\n### Weights & Biases\nW&B is SaaS-first with a self-hosted server option for data-residency requirements. The API is\n`wandb.init(project=..., config=...)`, `wandb.log({...}, step=...)` and `run.finish()`. Its distinguishing\npieces are **Artifacts**, versioned datasets and models with an automatic lineage graph (which run\nconsumed which artifact version and produced which), **Sweeps**, a built-in hyperparameter search\norchestrator that schedules agents across machines, **Tables** for per-example analysis inside the UI,\n**Reports** for sharing, and **Weave**, its toolkit for tracing and evaluating LLM applications. The UI\nis the usual reason teams pick it; the usual reasons they do not are cost per seat and the fact that\nmetrics and artifacts leave the network unless you self-host.\n\n### How an LLM team uses either\nA fine-tuning run logs `base_model`, `base_revision`, `lora_r`, `lr`, `train_set` version hash and\n`git_sha` as params, train and eval loss per step as metrics, the adapter directory and the per-item\neval outputs as artifacts, then registers the adapter as a model version so lineage flows into the\nregistry. An evaluation run logs prompt version, model snapshot, dataset hash and judge config as\nparams, aggregate and per-slice scores as metrics, and the per-item outputs file as an artifact so error\nanalysis can start from the run page. The tracker becomes the place where \"what did we try and what\nhappened\" is answered.\n\n### Choosing\nData residency or regulated environments push toward self-hosted MLflow or a W&B server. A team already\non Databricks gets MLflow for free. A research-heavy team that runs sweeps daily usually prefers W&B's\nUI and sweep orchestration. LLM application teams increasingly keep traces and evals in an observability\nplatform such as Langfuse or LangSmith and use the tracker only for training; the important decision is\none system of record per artifact class, not one tool for everything.","example":"The same LoRA fine-tuning run logged two ways. Parameters are fixed at the start, metrics are\nstep-indexed, artifacts are files, and the dataset and code versions are logged as params so the run is\nreproducible. `train_and_eval` is your loop, yielding per-step metrics; the two halves are alternatives,\nshown together only for comparison (as written, the loop would train twice).\n\n```python\nimport mlflow, wandb, subprocess\n\ncfg = {\"base\": \"meta-llama/Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct\", \"base_revision\": \"<hf commit sha>\",\n       \"lora_r\": 16, \"lr\": 2e-4, \"epochs\": 2,\n       \"train_set\": \"tickets-v3@9c1e04\", \"golden_set\": \"golden-v8@5c2e91\"}\ncfg[\"git_sha\"] = subprocess.check_output([\"git\", \"rev-parse\", \"HEAD\"]).decode().strip()\n\n# --- MLflow: self-hosted tracking server; runs grouped by experiment -------------------------\nmlflow.set_tracking_uri(\"http://mlflow.internal:5000\")\nmlflow.set_experiment(\"support-classifier\")\nwith mlflow.start_run(run_name=\"lora-r16-lr2e-4\") as run:\n    mlflow.log_params(cfg)                                            # immutable for the run's lifetime\n    for step, (train_loss, eval_acc) in enumerate(train_and_eval(cfg)):\n        mlflow.log_metrics({\"train_loss\": train_loss, \"eval_acc\": eval_acc}, step=step)\n    mlflow.log_artifact(\"outputs/eval_items.jsonl\")                   # per-item outputs for error analysis\n    mlflow.log_artifacts(\"outputs/adapter\", artifact_path=\"model\")    # raw files; use a flavour's log_model for pyfunc loading\n    print(\"register with run id:\", run.info.run_id)\n\n# Comparison is the point: params are strings (quote them); ordering uses each metric's LAST value\nbest = mlflow.search_runs(experiment_names=[\"support-classifier\"], filter_string=\"params.lora_r = '16'\",\n                          order_by=[\"metrics.eval_acc DESC\"], max_results=5)\n\n# --- W&B: SaaS or self-hosted server; config + log + a versioned Artifact with lineage --------\nrun = wandb.init(project=\"support-classifier\", name=\"lora-r16-lr2e-4\", config=cfg)\nfor step, (train_loss, eval_acc) in enumerate(train_and_eval(cfg)):\n    wandb.log({\"train_loss\": train_loss, \"eval_acc\": eval_acc}, step=step)\nart = wandb.Artifact(\"support-classifier-adapter\", type=\"model\", metadata=cfg)\nart.add_dir(\"outputs/adapter\")\nart.add_file(\"outputs/eval_items.jsonl\")\nrun.log_artifact(art)                                                 # becomes v0, v1, ... in the lineage graph\nrun.finish()\n```","tradeoffs":["Self-hosted MLflow keeps data in your network and costs nothing in licences, but you operate a server, a database and an artifact store, and the UI is thinner than W&B's.","W&B's hosted UI, sweeps and lineage graph save engineering time, but per-seat pricing and data leaving the network flip the decision in regulated environments unless you run the self-hosted server.","Logging every per-item output as an artifact makes error analysis one click away, at the cost of storage that grows with runs times dataset size; sample or compress once eval sets reach six figures.","Autologging captures params and metrics with zero code, but it also captures whatever the framework decides is interesting, which can bury the three numbers you care about under three hundred.","Keeping LLM eval runs in the training tracker unifies history, whereas keeping them in the observability platform links them to traces; the split that wins depends on whether your evals start from production traces or from training jobs."],"pitfalls":["Logging the final metric only; the symptom is a run that looks best on its last value while its curve shows it diverged and recovered by luck, which the next seed will not repeat.","Forgetting the data version and git commit in params; the symptom is a top run that nobody can reproduce because the training set was regenerated since.","Mutating a config dict after `log_params` and assuming the tracker noticed; the symptom is runs whose recorded learning rate differs from the one actually used.","Treating the tracker as a backup for weights; the symptom is a bucket bill that doubles monthly and a cleanup that deletes the one checkpoint the registry still points to.","Ranking runs by a metric whose final value is not its best, such as eval accuracy after over-fitting; the symptom is the run table hiding the checkpoint that actually peaked, because sorting uses the last value.","Resuming a W&B run or logging post-training evals with a step lower than the last one logged; the symptom is eval points that silently never appear on the chart, with only a warning in the console.","Running evals outside the tracker \"because it is just a prompt change\"; the symptom is a team arguing from memory about whether version 11 or 12 did better on the hard slice."],"when":"Use a tracker from the first fine-tuning run or the first eval you intend to compare against a later\none, which is essentially always. Choose MLflow when you need self-hosting, already run Databricks, or\nwant the registry and model packaging in the same system; choose W&B when sweep orchestration, the UI\nand artifact lineage are worth the subscription and data can leave your network or you will run the\nself-hosted server. Measure whether every registered model version links to a run, whether every run\nhas a data version and commit, and how long it takes a new team member to find \"the best run on the\nrefund slice\". In interviews, be ready to describe the run data model, how lineage flows from a run into\nthe registry, and the hosting and ecosystem differences between the two tools.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Instruments training and eval scripts with params, step metrics and artifacts, and registers the resulting model version from the run so lineage is automatic.","architect":"Chooses the system of record per artifact class, decides self-hosted versus SaaS from residency and cost, and sets the minimum metadata every run must carry.","fde":"Stands up a tracker inside the customer's environment when their data cannot leave, so customer-specific fine-tuning and evals have the same history and reproducibility as internal work."},"check":{"q":"A colleague reports that run 42 reached 0.91 accuracy and asks you to promote its adapter. The\nrun page shows only `lr` and `epochs` as params and a single final metric. What questions do you ask\nbefore promoting, and what should the run have logged to make them unnecessary?","a":"First, which base model revision, which training set version and which code commit produced it;\nwithout those the adapter cannot be retrained or audited. Second, what the evaluation set was and\nwhether it was the current golden version, because 0.91 on last month's set is not comparable to this\nweek's baseline. Third, how the metric evolved over steps, since a single final number can hide a\ndivergent run that landed on a good value by chance. Fourth, where the per-item outputs are, so the\nrefund-intent slice can be checked. The run should have logged base revision, dataset hashes, git sha\nand LoRA settings as params, train and eval metrics per step, and the eval outputs and adapter as\nartifacts, with the adapter registered from the run so the registry version carries that lineage."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why are a run's parameters fixed at the start while metrics are logged over time?","options":["Parameters are stored in a different database table for speed","Parameters define the experiment and must stay constant for comparison, while metrics describe how it unfolded","Metrics cannot be stored as strings","Parameters are only used by sweeps"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A run is an answer to \"what happens with this configuration\"; if the configuration changes mid-run the question changes. Step-indexed metrics let you see divergence and over-fitting instead of a final number."},{"q":"Which difference most often decides between MLflow and W&B in a regulated enterprise?","options":["W&B cannot log artifacts","MLflow has no model registry","Self-hosting and data residency: MLflow is self-hosted by default, W&B is SaaS-first with a self-hosted option","MLflow only supports PyTorch"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Both tools cover the core data model. Where metrics, configs and artifacts are allowed to live is usually the constraint that decides, with W&B's UI and sweeps as the counterweight when residency is not binding."},{"q":"What links a registered model version back to the data it was trained on?","options":["The alias assigned at promotion time","The run that produced it, which logged the dataset version as a parameter","The artifact file's modification timestamp","The model card's free-text description"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Lineage is captured at training time in the run; registering from the run carries that link into the version. An alias says where a model is deployed, not where it came from."}]},{"key":"llmops-platform/evaluation-frameworks","sources":["Liang et al., 2022 — Holistic Evaluation of Language Models","Zheng et al., 2023 — Judging LLM-as-a-Judge with MT-Bench and Chatbot Arena","Official docs: Promptfoo","Official docs: lm-evaluation-harness"],"why":"The `evals/eval-harness` lesson explains the engine: dataset in, pinned configuration, raw outputs,\ngrades, report. The problem this lesson addresses appears the moment a second team builds its own engine.\nTeam A stores items as CSV with a `label` column and reports \"87% pass\"; team B keeps JSONL with a\n`reference` field and reports \"judge score 0.71\". When the platform group asks whether the company can\nmove to a new model snapshot, every team re-runs something different, the results cannot be added up, and\nthe decision is made on anecdotes. Worse, the numbers are not even stable within one team: the scoring\nrubric lives in a notebook, the dataset was edited last Tuesday, and the \"baseline\" was measured against\na model alias that has since been updated.\n\nThe naive alternatives each fail in a specific way. Evaluating in notebooks is not repeatable, so\nFriday's number cannot be compared with Tuesday's. A spreadsheet of hand-checked outputs is honest but\nstale within a sprint and cannot gate a pipeline. Public benchmarks (MMLU, HumanEval and friends) measure\nthe base model, not your prompt, retrieval, tools and guardrails, and the popular ones are widely\nsuspected to have leaked into frontier-model training data. Buying a dashboard and declaring the problem solved fails more quietly: without dataset\ndiscipline, scorers calibrated to your task and a runner that pins every version, the dashboard renders\nnoise with good typography.\n\nWhat the framework actually contributes is standardisation of the interfaces, not cleverness in any one\nof them. When datasets, targets, scorers, runs and results have one schema, pieces become swappable (a\nnew judge model, a new dataset version), runs become comparable across teams and weeks, caching makes\nper-PR evaluation affordable, and every score links back to the trace that produced it.","how":"### The five interfaces\n1. **Dataset.** Items of `{id, input, expected or reference, slice metadata}`, stored as immutable,\n   content-hashed versions. Immutable *because* a score only means something relative to a dataset\n   version; an item relabeled in place silently moves every baseline. Strategy for which datasets exist\n   and how items get in is the `golden-dataset-strategy` lesson.\n2. **Target.** An adapter that calls the production code path with a pinned bundle (model snapshot,\n   prompt version, decoding parameters, index snapshot). It calls the real application *because*\n   evaluating a copy of the prompt evaluates the copy: the app's template rendering, post-processing and\n   retrieval are part of what ships.\n3. **Scorers.** Three families, each with a name and a version. *Deterministic* scorers (exact match,\n   regex, JSON-schema validity, unit tests that execute generated code) are cheap and unambiguous but only\n   apply to structured outputs. *Model-graded* scorers (an LLM judging against a rubric, against a\n   reference answer, or pairwise between two outputs) cover free text, cost a model call per item, and are\n   themselves a model that drifts, so they must be calibrated against human labels and pinned to a\n   snapshot. *Human* scorers are the ground truth, used to calibrate judges and to sign off high-risk\n   changes. Every score is stored as `{item_id, scorer, scorer_version, value, rationale}`, the version\n   *because* a judge upgrade changes scores without any change to the application.\n4. **Runner.** Executes dataset × target with concurrency, retries and timeouts, caches model calls keyed\n   by the exact request sent (model id, decoding params, rendered messages), writes raw outputs *before* scoring, and records a\n   manifest of every pin. Raw outputs first *because* scorers change: re-scoring stored outputs with a new\n   judge should cost judge calls only, never a re-run of the target. Timeouts are recorded as errors, not\n   as zero scores, so a provider incident does not masquerade as a regression.\n5. **Results store and comparison.** Per-run aggregates per slice with confidence intervals, and a\n   per-item diff between any two runs that share a dataset version. The item-level diff is the artifact a\n   reviewer reads; the aggregate is what the gate reads.\n\n### Three categories of framework, chosen by the question\n- **Capability benchmark harnesses** (EleutherAI's lm-evaluation-harness, HELM) run standard academic\n  tasks against a model. They answer \"which base model\" and nothing about your application.\n- **Application eval frameworks** (Promptfoo's config of prompts × providers × test cases with\n  assertions; DeepEval's pytest-style tests; RAGAS's retrieval metrics such as faithfulness and context\n  precision) run *your* dataset through *your* pipeline. They answer \"did this prompt or pipeline change\n  help\".\n- **Platform-integrated tools** (Braintrust, LangSmith, Langfuse) hold datasets, experiments and\n  production traces in one store, so a bad trace becomes a dataset item with one click and an eval score\n  attaches to the trace that produced it. They answer \"how do we close the loop with production\".\nThe `evals/tooling` lesson compares the tools; the architectural point is that a team usually needs one\nfrom the second category wired to one from the third, and the first only when choosing a base model.\n\n### Where it plugs into the platform\nThe prompt registry and model registry store a version's eval results as metadata, and promotion reads\nthem. CI calls the framework through a CLI or SDK and applies the gate policy (the\n`evaluation-gates-for-production-releases` lesson); the framework reports, the pipeline decides. The\nobservability stack runs the *same* scorers on sampled production traces, so offline and online numbers\nshare a definition. The dataset pipeline flows the other way: failures mined from traces and error\nanalysis become candidate items.\n\n### Build or adopt\nThe differentiating assets are the dataset and the scorers, never the runner or the results store. Adopt\na framework when the task shape is common (chat, RAG, classification) and write only the target adapter\nand custom scorers. Build a thin layer of your own when the target does not fit the framework's data model\n(long multi-turn agents with tool side effects, simulated users), when data residency forbids a SaaS\nstore, or when scorers need to run inside your network; even then, keep the five interfaces and reuse an\nopen-source store rather than inventing a results format.","example":"A minimal framework skeleton showing the five interfaces as code. The point is the shape: scorers carry a\nversion, the target is the production entrypoint, and raw output is stored before any scoring under a\n*generation id* that hashes only what determines the outputs (dataset version and bundle). The run id\nadds the scorer versions on top. So an interrupted run resumes without paying again for finished items,\nand swapping `judge@rubric-v3` for `judge@rubric-v4` on a 1,000-item set yields a new run id but reuses\nall 1,000 stored outputs: 1,000 judge calls, zero target calls. A changed prompt changes the bundle, hence\nthe generation id, hence fresh outputs. Repeats for a noise-floor measurement need a repeat index in the\ngeneration id; otherwise every \"repeat\" replays the same stored sample and the measured noise is zero.\n\n```python\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass, asdict\nfrom typing import Any, Callable\nimport hashlib, json, pathlib, time\n\n@dataclass\nclass Item:                      # one row of a content-hashed dataset version\n    id: str; input: dict; expected: Any = None; slice: str = \"default\"\n\n@dataclass\nclass Scorer:                    # name + version travel with every score it produces\n    name: str; version: str; fn: Callable[[Item, str], tuple[float, str]]\n\nTarget = Callable[[dict], str]   # the production code path, with the pinned bundle inside\n\ndef json_field_equals(field: str) -> Scorer:            # deterministic family\n    def fn(item, out):\n        try:\n            return float(json.loads(out).get(field) == item.expected), \"\"\n        except json.JSONDecodeError:\n            return 0.0, \"invalid JSON\"\n    return Scorer(f\"json_eq:{field}\", \"1\", fn)\n\ndef rubric_judge(judge: Target, rubric_version: str) -> Scorer:   # model-graded family\n    def fn(item, out):\n        v = json.loads(judge({\"input\": item.input, \"output\": out, \"reference\": item.expected}))\n        return float(v[\"score\"]), v[\"reason\"]          # judge prompt asks for {\"score\": 0..1, \"reason\": \"...\"}\n    return Scorer(\"judge\", rubric_version, fn)\n\ndef run(dataset_version: str, items: list[Item], target: Target, scorers: list[Scorer], bundle: dict) -> dict:\n    h = lambda o: hashlib.sha256(json.dumps(o, sort_keys=True).encode()).hexdigest()[:12]\n    gen_id = h({\"dataset\": dataset_version, \"bundle\": bundle})          # what determines the outputs\n    run_id = h({\"gen\": gen_id, \"scorers\": [f\"{s.name}@{s.version}\" for s in scorers]})\n    path = pathlib.Path(f\"outputs/{gen_id}.jsonl\"); path.parent.mkdir(exist_ok=True)\n    done = {r[\"id\"]: r[\"output\"] for r in map(json.loads, path.open())} if path.exists() else {}\n    rows = []\n    with path.open(\"a\") as raw:\n        for it in items:\n            if it.id not in done:                       # a new judge reuses these: re-scoring costs judge calls only\n                done[it.id] = target(it.input)\n                raw.write(json.dumps({\"id\": it.id, \"output\": done[it.id]}) + \"\\n\"); raw.flush()\n            out = done[it.id]                           # persisted before any scorer runs\n            scores = [dict(zip((\"value\", \"rationale\"), s.fn(it, out)), scorer=f\"{s.name}@{s.version}\")\n                      for s in scorers]                 # one call per scorer: a judge call costs money\n            rows.append({\"item\": asdict(it), \"output\": out, \"scores\": scores})\n    return {\"run_id\": run_id, \"gen_id\": gen_id, \"started\": time.time(), \"rows\": rows}\n```","tradeoffs":["One framework across teams makes numbers comparable, but forces every task into its data model; it flips when a team's target (a long tool-using agent, a simulated user) does not fit and they fork.","Model-graded scorers cover free text that deterministic checks cannot, at the price of a judge call per item and a second model's drift; prefer deterministic scorers whenever the output is structured.","SaaS platforms give datasets, traces and experiments in one place with no operations burden, but production traces (customer data) leave your boundary; self-host or build when residency or PII rules apply.","Running the eval on every PR gives fast feedback, and the cache keeps it cheap only while most items are unaffected by the change; a prompt touched by every item invalidates the cache, so tiered datasets exist."],"pitfalls":["Evaluating a copy of the prompt inside the framework instead of the production code path; the symptom is a green eval followed by a production regression caused by template or post-processing differences.","Using a judge scorer without calibrating it against human labels; the symptom is \"improvements\" that turn out to be longer answers, or scores that jump when the judge model is upgraded.","Storing aggregates only; the symptom is a two-point drop that nobody can explain because there are no raw outputs to diff against the champion.","Letting the dataset schema drift as teams add fields ad hoc; the symptom is two runs \"on the same dataset\" with different item counts, which the comparison tool should refuse to compare.","Reading a capability benchmark as application quality; the symptom is a model with the higher public score losing on your golden set because your task depends on retrieval and formatting, not trivia."],"when":"Reach for a framework the moment a second person needs to run or read an eval, or the moment an eval\ngates anything. Pick the category by the question: base-model choice means a capability harness,\nprompt or pipeline change means an application framework over your golden set, closing the loop with\nproduction means a platform-integrated store. Measure the framework itself: time from PR to eval result,\ncost per run and cache hit rate, judge-to-human agreement, and the fraction of production incidents that\nalready had a dataset item. In interviews, expect to draw the five interfaces and explain why raw outputs\nare stored before scoring and why a scorer carries a version.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes the target adapter against the production entrypoint and the custom scorers, and wires the framework's CLI into the PR pipeline with content-addressed caching.","architect":"Decides which category of framework the organisation standardises on, where results and traces are stored given data-residency rules, and which interfaces every team must conform to.","fde":"Stands up the framework inside a customer's environment with their golden set, so acceptance is argued from item-level diffs rather than from demos of hand-picked prompts."},"check":{"q":"Four product teams each have their own eval script, and leadership wants a company-wide decision on\nmoving to a new model snapshot next month. What do you build, and what do you refuse to build?","a":"Build the shared interfaces, not a new harness per team: a dataset schema with content-hashed\nversions, a target adapter contract that calls each team's production entrypoint with a pinned bundle,\na scorer library with names and versions (deterministic where outputs are structured, a calibrated judge\nelsewhere), one runner that stores raw outputs and a manifest, and a results store that diffs runs item by\nitem. Then run champion and candidate snapshots through every team's golden set on identical items and\nreport per-slice deltas with confidence intervals. Refuse to decide from public benchmark scores, from\naggregate numbers computed by four different rubrics, or from runs whose judge versions differ."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does a well-designed framework persist raw outputs before any scoring happens?","options":["Because scoring is too slow to run inline with generation","Because scorers change and re-scoring stored outputs avoids paying for the target's model calls again","Because raw outputs are needed to compute the cache key","Because judges cannot score streamed responses"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Scorers are versioned and get replaced; with raw outputs stored, a new judge or rubric re-scores at judge cost only, and reviewers can diff candidate against champion. The cache key is built from the input and pins, not from the output."},{"q":"Which framework category answers \"should our RAG support bot move from model X to model Y\"?","options":["A capability benchmark harness such as lm-evaluation-harness or HELM","An application eval framework running your golden set through your pipeline with your scorers","A public leaderboard comparing the two models' chat ratings","The provider's own published evaluation of model Y"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The decision depends on how the model behaves inside your prompt, retrieval and formatting; only your dataset and scorers measure that. Benchmarks and leaderboards measure the base model on other tasks."},{"q":"All scores rise five points overnight with no change to code, prompts, model or dataset version. What is the most likely cause?","options":["The cache served stale outputs from a previous run","The golden set gained easier items","The judge scorer's model or rubric changed, which is why scorers carry a version","Production traffic shifted toward easier queries"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A model-graded scorer is itself a model; if its snapshot or rubric moves, every candidate and champion shifts together. Pinning the judge version and re-baselining the champion under the same scorer is the defence; a cache would reproduce old scores, not new ones, and an unchanged dataset version rules out new items."}]},{"key":"llmops-platform/golden-dataset-strategy","sources":["Gebru et al., 2021 — Datasheets for Datasets","Ribeiro et al., 2020 — Beyond Accuracy: Behavioral Testing of NLP Models with CheckList","Breck et al., 2017 — The ML Test Score: A Rubric for ML Production Readiness and Technical Debt Reduction"],"why":"Most teams start with one file of fifty examples the first engineer wrote in an afternoon. It works for a\nquarter, then fails in four predictable ways. It goes *stale*: the product adds a refunds flow and a\nsecond language, traffic shifts, and the set still measures last quarter's product, so the eval stays\ngreen while users complain. It *leaks*: someone copies the best examples into the few-shot prompt, then\ninto the fine-tuning data, and from then on the set measures memorisation; the score climbs toward 100% and\nstops carrying information. It has the *wrong size*: fifty items cannot distinguish a two-point regression\nfrom noise, so engineers learn that the number wobbles and ignore it; the fix, growing it to five thousand\nitems, makes a full run cost real money and an hour of wall-clock, so the run is skipped on busy days,\nwhich are the days it matters. And it has *no owner*: either nobody is allowed to say \"this expected\nanswer is wrong\", so stale labels block legitimate changes, or everyone edits it, so the baseline moves\nsilently and no two runs are comparable.\n\nThe naive alternatives do not solve this. \"Just use production logs\" gives inputs without agreed outputs,\nskewed toward the common case, with customer PII inside. \"Just use a public benchmark\" measures the model\nrather than your prompt, retrieval and tools. \"Just make it bigger\" trades noise for cost without fixing\nstaleness or leakage. The strategy treats the golden data as a managed platform asset with tiers,\nversions, owners and a refresh loop, the same way a mature team treats its test suite rather than a\nfolder of scripts.","how":"### A portfolio, not a file\nEach tier has a purpose, a size and a cadence, and the tiers exist *because* cost multiplied by run\nfrequency must stay affordable while the release gate stays powerful (sizes below are illustrative):\n- **Smoke set** (tens to a couple of hundred items, stratified): runs on every PR; catches catastrophic\n  breakage such as a broken template or a parser that no longer matches, not two-point drifts.\n- **Full set** (hundreds to low thousands, per-slice quotas): runs on merge, nightly and on release\n  candidates; this is the set gates are computed on.\n- **Slice sets**: per intent, language, tenant or document type; small, but each carries its own floor\n  because the aggregate hides a collapsed slice.\n- **Regression set**: one item per fixed production incident, never pruned, expected at 100% pass; \"every\n  bug becomes a test\" is the cheapest coverage you will ever buy.\n- **Adversarial and safety set**: prompt injection, jailbreak attempts, PII extraction; gated by absolute\n  floors, not relative to the champion.\n- **Sealed holdout**: a subset prompt authors never see and that never enters few-shot or training data,\n  run only at release; its job is to detect overfitting to the development set. The gap between full-set\n  and holdout scores is the overfitting signal.\n\n### The statistics that set the sizes\nFor a pass-rate metric with true rate *p*, one run's standard error is √(p(1−p)/n). With *p* = 0.8 the\n95% half-width is about ±11 points at n = 50, ±5.5 at n = 200 and ±2.5 at n = 1,000. So a smoke set\ncan only detect large breaks, and anyone claiming a two-point improvement on a hundred items is reading\nnoise. Pairing tightens this considerably: when candidate and champion run on the *identical* items,\nitem difficulty cancels in the per-item difference, which is why both must run on the same dataset\nversion (the `evaluation-gates-for-production-releases` lesson does the arithmetic). Size each slice from\nthe smallest regression you must be able to detect there, not from a round number.\n\nThe sizing rule follows directly. For a target 95% half-width *h* on the candidate-minus-champion\ndifference, an unpaired comparison of two pass rates needs n ≈ 1.96² · 2p(1−p) / h² items, while a paired\none needs n ≈ 1.96² · f / h², where *f* is the fraction of items whose verdict differs between the two\nbundles. At p = 0.8 and h = 2 points, unpaired needs about 3,070 items; paired with f = 10% needs about\n960. Detecting a regression of size *h* reliably rather than just bounding it costs more again (roughly\ndouble the items for 80% power), and *f* is unknown until you have run the champion against a few\ncandidates, so measure it and resize.\n\n### The sourcing pipeline\nItems come from four places, and the mix matters. *Production traces*, sampled with per-slice quotas and\nstratified by outcome (thumbs-down, escalation, abandoned session) rather than uniformly, *because*\nuniform sampling drowns rare intents in the head of the distribution. *Failure mining* from error\nanalysis of incidents. *Synthetic generation* for slices with too little real traffic, admitted only after\na human labels and filters them, *because* a generator shares the model's blind spots and tends to write\neasier cases than users do. *Expert-written* items for policy-critical behaviour that traffic rarely\nexercises. What an item stores as \"expected\" depends on the task: a label for\nclassification, executable tests for code, and for free text usually a short list of must-include facts\nand must-not behaviours rather than one reference phrasing, *because* a judge grading against criteria\npenalises legitimate rewording less, and a policy change then edits a named criterion instead of a\nparagraph nobody can diff. Every item passes an admission workflow: proposed, labeled by the task owner, checked by a\nsecond reviewer (measure inter-annotator agreement; a slice with persistent disagreement has an\nunder-specified task, not bad reviewers), then merged into the *next* version. PII is scrubbed or\nreplaced with synthetic values before storage, *because* a golden set lives for years and gets copied to\nlaptops.\n\n### Versioning and ownership\nA version is immutable and identified by a content hash, with a changelog recording items added, removed\nor relabeled and why. Relabeling is a version bump, never an edit, *because* a baseline recorded against\nversion A is meaningless once A's labels change. The owner of \"what counts as correct\" is the role that\nowns the task definition (product or domain), while engineering owns the pipeline; the person who wrote\nthe prompt under test does not label the holdout. A datasheet (purpose, sources, slices, known gaps)\ntravels with each set so a new team can judge what a score on it means.\n\n### Lifecycle\nRefresh is triggered, not calendar-driven: when production slice mix shifts (monitoring tells you), when\na policy changes an expected answer, when a slice has sat at 100% for every candidate for months (move\nthose items to smoke or regression, they no longer discriminate), and after every incident. Contamination\ndefence is mechanical: the registry checks that no golden input's hash or near-duplicate appears in prompt\ntemplates, few-shot banks or fine-tuning corpora, and the sealed holdout is excluded from any export that\nengineers can read.","example":"A support assistant with six intents whose production traffic splits roughly 60/20/10/5/3/2 percent.\nUniform sampling of 300 traces yields about 180/60/30/15/9/6 items; the rarest intent, with six items,\ncannot be measured at all. Drawing a quota of 40 per slice from the full trace pool instead gives 240\nitems where every slice is measurable to roughly ±12 points at p = 0.8, and the aggregate is reported both\ntraffic-weighted and as per-slice floors. The code shows the size arithmetic, the quota sampler and how a\nversion is cut.\n\n```python\nimport hashlib, json, math, random\nfrom collections import defaultdict\n\ndef half_width_points(p, n):            # 95% half-width in percentage points for a pass-rate metric\n    return 100 * 1.96 * math.sqrt(p * (1 - p) / n)\n\nfor n in (40, 50, 200, 1000):\n    print(f\"n={n:5d}: ±{half_width_points(0.8, n):.1f} points\")   # ±12.4, ±11.1, ±5.5, ±2.5\n\ndef items_for_paired_gate(h, flip_rate):  # n so the paired 95% half-width on the delta is h (fractions)\n    return math.ceil(1.96**2 * flip_rate / h**2)          # items_for_paired_gate(0.02, 0.10) -> 961\n\ndef stratified_sample(candidates, quota_per_slice, seed=7):\n    \"\"\"candidates: labeled production traces; a fixed quota per slice keeps rare intents measurable.\"\"\"\n    by_slice = defaultdict(list)\n    for c in candidates:\n        by_slice[c[\"slice\"]].append(c)\n    rng = random.Random(seed)\n    chosen = []\n    for slice_name, items in sorted(by_slice.items()):\n        rng.shuffle(items)\n        chosen += items[:quota_per_slice]           # a slice with fewer than quota items is a coverage gap to log\n    return chosen\n\ndef cut_version(items, parent, changelog, tier=\"full\", sealed=False):\n    body = json.dumps(sorted(items, key=lambda i: i[\"id\"]), sort_keys=True).encode()\n    slices = {i[\"slice\"] for i in items}\n    return {\"version\": hashlib.sha256(body).hexdigest()[:12],   # content hash: an edit is a new version\n            \"parent\": parent, \"n\": len(items), \"tier\": tier, \"sealed\": sealed,\n            \"per_slice\": {s: sum(i[\"slice\"] == s for i in items) for s in sorted(slices)},\n            \"changelog\": changelog}                            # e.g. \"relabeled 15 refund items after policy change 2025-06\"\n```","tradeoffs":["Per-slice quotas make rare cases measurable, but the aggregate no longer reflects traffic; report both a traffic-weighted score and per-slice floors, and drop the quota only for slices nobody cares about.","A sealed holdout detects overfitting to the development set, but you cannot error-analyse what you are not allowed to read; a small team without capacity for two sets should rotate items into the holdout instead.","Mining failures from production keeps the set current, but a set made only of past failures makes every candidate look worse than traffic would; keep a randomly sampled, stratified core alongside it.","Synthetic items cover rare slices cheaply, but share the generator's blind spots and run easier than real users; the trade flips in their favour only once humans have labeled and filtered them.","Larger sets tighten confidence intervals and raise cost per run; tiering and content-addressed caching are what reconcile the two, and without caching a large full set gets skipped."],"pitfalls":["Golden items copied into few-shot prompts or fine-tuning data; the symptom is a development-set score near 100% while the sealed holdout and production CSAT do not move.","Editing expected outputs in place; the symptom is a champion baseline that shifts without any deploy, so last month's runs are no longer comparable.","Uniform sampling from logs; the symptom is a flat aggregate while a rare but high-value intent regresses all the way to production.","A set that is only a museum of past failures; the symptom is every candidate \"regressing\" ten points relative to what users actually experience.","Nobody owning correctness; the symptom is label disputes settled by whoever ran the eval and an inter-annotator agreement that has never been measured.","Items stale after a policy change; the symptom is the gate blocking a legitimately correct new behaviour, after which engineers start overriding the gate and it stops meaning anything."],"when":"Adopt the portfolio the moment an eval gates anything or more than one person edits the set; before that,\none stratified file with a content hash is enough. Measure the asset itself: coverage and confidence-interval\nwidth per slice, median item age, the fraction of production incidents that have a regression item, agreement\non relabels, and cost per run per tier. Day to day, every bug-fix PR adds a regression item and every\nproduct change triggers a version bump with a changelog. In interviews, expect to be asked how big a set\nmust be to detect a given regression, how you keep it out of the prompt, and who is allowed to change a\nlabel.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes the quota sampler and the contamination check, adds a regression item with every incident fix, and cuts content-hashed versions rather than editing files in place.","architect":"Defines the tiers, the ownership model and the holdout policy, and decides which slices carry floors in the release gate and what cost per run the platform will fund.","fde":"Builds the customer's first golden set from their real tickets with their domain experts labeling, because acceptance criteria argued from the customer's own data survive procurement reviews."},"check":{"q":"A team's 120-item golden set has read 94% for six months while production satisfaction has fallen.\nWhat has most likely gone wrong, and what is your plan for the next two sprints?","a":"Three candidates, checked in order. Leakage: grep prompts, few-shot banks and fine-tuning data for\ngolden inputs; if found, the 94% measures memorisation. Staleness: compare the set's slice mix with current\ntraffic and the product's recent changes; a set built before the refunds flow cannot see refunds failing.\nSize: at n = 120 and p ≈ 0.94 the 95% half-width is roughly ±4 points (it would be about ±7 near p = 0.8),\nand each slice holds only a fraction of the 120 items, so a real drop on one slice can hide in the noise. Plan: cut a new version\nwith per-slice quotas sampled from recent traces stratified by negative outcomes, add regression items\nfrom the complaints, carve out a sealed holdout the prompt authors never see, record the new baseline\nfor the champion on that version, and put slice floors into the gate."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why keep a sealed holdout that prompt authors are never allowed to read?","options":["To reduce the cost of the per-PR smoke run","To detect overfitting of prompts and few-shot examples to the development set","To satisfy data-residency rules for customer traces","To measure inter-annotator agreement on hard items"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Anything authors can read eventually shapes the prompt, so development-set scores drift upward without real improvement. A gap between full-set and holdout scores is the overfitting signal; cost and residency are solved by other mechanisms."},{"q":"On a 100-item set a candidate scores 84% against the champion's 82%. What should you conclude?","options":["The candidate is better and can be promoted","The candidate is worse because two points is within rounding","Nothing yet: at n = 100 the noise is several points, so run a paired comparison on a larger set","The golden set is contaminated"],"answer":2,"explanation":"With p around 0.8 and n = 100 the 95% half-width is roughly ±8 points, far wider than the observed difference. A paired run on the same, larger dataset version is what can distinguish signal from noise."},{"q":"A policy change makes 15 expected answers wrong. What is the correct way to update the golden set?","options":["Edit the 15 expected outputs in place so the next run is correct","Delete the 15 items and shrink the set","Cut a new version with the relabels in its changelog and re-baseline the champion on that version","Move the 15 items into the sealed holdout"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Baselines are recorded against a dataset version; editing in place silently invalidates every earlier comparison. A new content-hashed version with a changelog keeps history comparable, and re-running the champion gives the gate a valid reference."}]},{"key":"llmops-platform/reproducible-evaluations","sources":["Pineau et al., 2021 — Improving Reproducibility in Machine Learning Research","Chen, Zaharia & Zou, 2023 — How Is ChatGPT's Behavior Changing over Time?","Sculley et al., 2015 — Hidden Technical Debt in Machine Learning Systems"],"why":"\"Did the prompt change help?\" is only answerable if everything else held still, and in an LLM system\nalmost nothing does by default. The provider updates the model behind an alias such as `-latest`, and\npublished studies have documented large behaviour shifts between dated versions of the same hosted model.\nSampling at temperature above zero produces different outputs on every run, and even at temperature zero\noutputs are generally not bit-identical across calls, because batching and floating-point reduction order\non GPUs are not deterministic. The judge used to score free text is itself a model and drifts for the same\nreasons. The dataset gets edited in place. The retrieval index is re-embedded. The prompt contains today's\ndate or a live tool result. A dependency bump changes a tokenizer or an SDK default. A warm cache serves\nlast month's outputs for a model that has since changed.\n\nThe naive answers each cover one source. \"Set temperature to 0 and a seed\" addresses sampling only, and\nmany APIs treat the seed as best-effort. \"Compare with last week's number\" compares two systems. \"Run it\ntwice and see\" is the right instinct without the bookkeeping to act on it. The cost of getting this wrong\nis not academic: a team ships a prompt that \"improved three points\" when the provider changed the model\nunderneath, or rolls back a genuine improvement because the judge was upgraded the same day. Regulated\ncustomers add a second reason: they ask for the evidence behind a release, and \"we ran it and it looked\nfine\" is not evidence. Reproducibility is the discipline of pinning what can be pinned, measuring the\nnoise in what cannot, and recording both.","how":"### Three levels, and which one you actually need\n*Bit-exact* reproduction (identical outputs) is achievable with locally served open-weight models at\ntemperature zero with fixed batching and deterministic kernels (at some throughput cost), and rarely\nthrough a hosted API. *Statistical* reproduction (the\nsame score distribution within a measured noise band) is the practical target for every decision eval.\n*Re-derivable* (anyone can reconstruct the run from its manifest, even if the provider has since changed)\nis the minimum bar for any eval that is cited in a release.\n\n### The eval lockfile\nEvery run records a manifest, and each field is there *because* a specific drift has burned someone:\n- **Dataset**: the content hash of the items, not a file name, because files get edited in place.\n- **Bundle**: model snapshot id (or weights digest), prompt versions, decoding parameters, seed where\n  supported, tool and retrieval index snapshots. A dated snapshot rather than an alias, because the alias\n  is the single most common cause of unexplained score changes.\n- **Scorers**: judge model snapshot, judge prompt and rubric version, scorer code version; the judge is\n  pinned because it is a model.\n- **Environment**: git SHA, dependency lockfile hash, container image digest; because tokenizers, SDK\n  defaults and retry behaviour live in dependencies.\n- **Runtime inputs**: anything the application reads at request time that is not in the bundle, such as\n  the current date, a live tool or API result, or a feature-flag value. These are frozen by injecting a\n  fixed clock and replaying recorded tool responses (an agent eval that calls a live search API is not\n  reproducible next week whatever else you pin), and the recording's hash goes into the manifest.\n- **Run policy**: concurrency, timeout and retry settings, cache mode; because timeouts recorded as zero\n  scores make a provider incident look like a regression, and a cache that ignores a pin hides a change.\nThe run id is a hash of the manifest, so two runs with identical pins are recognisably the same\nexperiment, and the manifest is stored next to the raw outputs in the experiment-tracking or evaluation\nstore.\n\n### Measure the noise you cannot pin\nRun the *same* bundle several times (three to five repeats is a common illustrative choice) and compute\nthe spread of the aggregate; that spread is the noise floor, and no delta smaller than it may be believed.\nReport every metric as a mean with a confidence interval, compare candidate against champion on\nidentical items so item difficulty cancels, and evaluate at the temperature production uses rather than\nforcing zero, *because* an eval at temperature zero of a system that ships at 0.7 measures a different\nsystem; repeats, not determinism, are how you tame the variance. Deterministic scorers on structured\noutputs from a locally pinned model can show a noise floor near zero, at which point one run suffices;\nmeasure it before assuming it.\n\n### Two kinds of noise, two different tools\nA score on a golden set is uncertain for two separate reasons, and they need different remedies.\n*Generation noise* is the run-to-run variation on the same items, from sampling, non-deterministic\nserving and a model-graded scorer; repeats measure it, and averaging each item's score over its repeats\nshrinks it. *Item-sampling noise* is the uncertainty from having evaluated these few hundred items rather\nthan the whole population of requests; repeats do nothing for it, only more items do, and the confidence\ninterval computed over items (bootstrap over per-item scores) is what quantifies it. A delta must clear\nboth: a perfectly deterministic run on 100 items still has an item-level interval of several points. The\npractical recipe is to average repeats per item first, then compute the paired interval over items.\n\n### Detect drift you cannot pin\nEven a dated snapshot is a promise you cannot verify from outside. A *sentinel run* re-executes a frozen\nreference bundle on a fixed subset on a schedule; if its score moves beyond the noise floor while\nnothing in the manifest changed, something outside the manifest did (the provider, the network, the\nenvironment), and the alert fires before a candidate is blamed. The same sentinel catches a silently\nupdated judge.\n\n### Comparability as a rule, not a hope\nTwo runs are comparable only if their dataset version and scorer versions match; the tooling should refuse\nto diff them otherwise, or label the comparison as cross-version. Re-scoring is cheap when raw outputs are\nstored: a new judge version re-scores champion and candidate from stored outputs without re-calling the\ntarget, which is how you re-baseline after a judge upgrade.","example":"A manifest builder, a noise-floor measurement, and a comparability check. The run id is derived from the\nmanifest, so re-running with the same pins produces the same id; a changed pin produces a new one. The\nnoise floor is measured on the champion before any candidate is judged against it.\n\n```python\nimport hashlib, json, statistics, subprocess, pathlib\n\ndef sha(path: str) -> str:\n    return hashlib.sha256(pathlib.Path(path).read_bytes()).hexdigest()[:12]\n\ndef build_manifest(bundle: dict, dataset_version: str, scorers: dict, image_digest: str) -> tuple[str, dict]:\n    git_sha = subprocess.check_output([\"git\", \"rev-parse\", \"HEAD\"]).decode().strip()\n    m = {\"dataset\": dataset_version,                       # content hash of the items, never a file name\n         \"bundle\": bundle,                                  # {\"model\": \"vendor-model-2025-04-14\", \"prompt\": \"support@v12\",\n                                                            #  \"params\": {\"temperature\": 0.7, \"max_tokens\": 400}, \"index\": \"idx-2025-06-01\"}\n         \"scorers\": scorers,                                # {\"judge_model\": \"judge-2025-01-15\", \"rubric\": \"helpfulness@v3\"}\n         \"env\": {\"git\": git_sha, \"lock\": sha(\"uv.lock\"), \"image\": image_digest},\n         \"policy\": {\"concurrency\": 8, \"timeout_s\": 60, \"retries\": 2, \"cache\": \"read-write\"}}\n    run_id = hashlib.sha256(json.dumps(m, sort_keys=True).encode()).hexdigest()[:12]\n    return run_id, m\n\ndef noise_floor(run_once, k: int = 3) -> tuple[float, float]:\n    \"\"\"run_once() executes the SAME manifest with cache disabled and returns per-item scores.\n    The spread between repeats is the smallest delta anyone is allowed to believe.\"\"\"\n    means = [statistics.mean(run_once()) for _ in range(k)]\n    return statistics.mean(means), max(means) - min(means)\n\ndef comparable(a: dict, b: dict) -> tuple[bool, dict]:\n    checks = {k: a[k] == b[k] for k in (\"dataset\", \"scorers\")}\n    checks[\"same_model\"] = a[\"bundle\"][\"model\"] == b[\"bundle\"][\"model\"]   # false is fine for a model-upgrade eval, but say so\n    return all(checks[k] for k in (\"dataset\", \"scorers\")), checks\n\n# champion_mean, floor = noise_floor(lambda: run_eval(champion_manifest, cache=False))\n# delta = candidate_mean - champion_mean\n# if abs(delta) <= floor: print(\"within noise: not a result\")  # generation noise only; the paired interval\n#                                                               # over items (gate lesson) must also exclude it\n```","tradeoffs":["Pinning dated snapshots gives stable comparisons, but every deprecation forces a re-baseline; when the provider deprecates faster than you re-baseline, a scheduled model-upgrade job with a full eval is the only sustainable answer.","Repeated runs quantify noise at a multiple of the cost; once a deterministic scorer on a locally pinned model shows a near-zero floor, a single run is enough, but only after the floor has been measured.","Evaluating at production temperature measures what ships but is noisier; evaluating at zero is cleaner and may measure a system you do not ship, so the choice follows what production actually does.","Content-addressed caching makes re-runs nearly free, yet a key that omits any pin hides a change; the sentinel run must bypass the cache, which is exactly the run you cannot make cheap."],"pitfalls":["A model alias instead of a dated snapshot in the manifest; the symptom is scores jumping on a day when nobody deployed anything.","A silently upgraded judge; the symptom is champion and candidate shifting together, which preserves the delta but breaks every absolute floor in the gate.","Datasets referenced by file name; the symptom is two runs \"on golden.jsonl\" disagreeing on the item count.","Treating \"three identical repeats\" as proof the result is solid; the symptom is a deterministic eval on 80 items whose two-point win vanishes on the next dataset version, because repeats never measured item-sampling noise.","Timeouts counted as zero scores; the symptom is eval scores that correlate with the provider's status page.","Assuming temperature zero means deterministic; the symptom is a bug report that some outputs differ between identical runs (how many depends on the provider, output length and load), filed as a regression.","Comparing runs scored by different judge versions; the symptom is a \"regression\" that vanishes when both runs are re-scored from stored outputs with the same judge."],"when":"Every eval that informs a decision gets a manifest; the rigour scales with the stakes. In the development\nloop, re-derivable is enough and the framework captures the manifest automatically. At a release gate,\nstatistical reproduction is required: repeats to establish the noise floor, identical dataset and scorer\nversions, paired comparison. For regulated or customer-facing commitments, store the full evidence\n(manifest, raw outputs, scores, approvals) with the promoted bundle. Measure the practice itself: the\nfraction of runs with a complete manifest, the noise floor per metric, sentinel drift events per quarter,\nand how often the comparability check refuses a diff. In interviews, the question is usually \"how would\nyou know the model changed underneath you?\"; the sentinel run is the answer.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Makes the harness refuse to start without a complete manifest, pins judge and model snapshots, stores raw outputs, and schedules the sentinel run with caching disabled.","architect":"Sets the reproducibility level each stage requires, decides what evidence is retained for how long, and owns the policy that aliases never appear in a promoted bundle.","fde":"Hands a customer the manifest and raw outputs behind an acceptance number, so their team can re-run it in their own environment and get the same conclusion."},"check":{"q":"A colleague reports that their prompt change raised the judge score from 0.78 to 0.83 on Friday,\nagainst a baseline measured three weeks ago. What do you ask before believing it, and what do you run?","a":"Ask whether both runs used the same dataset version, the same model snapshot rather than an alias,\nthe same judge model and rubric version, the same decoding parameters and index, and whether the baseline's\nnoise floor was ever measured. Three weeks is long enough for the provider, the judge or the dataset to\nhave moved, so the 0.05 may belong to any of them. Then run champion and candidate side by side now, with\nmanifests identical except for the prompt version, cache disabled, three repeats each, paired on identical\nitems, and report the mean difference with a confidence interval. If the interval excludes zero and the\ndelta exceeds the measured floor, treat the improvement as real (and confirm it on the slices that matter);\notherwise it is not yet a result."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why must the judge model's snapshot be part of the eval manifest?","options":["Because judge calls are the most expensive part of the run","Because the judge is a model whose drift changes scores without any change to the application","Because judges only work at temperature zero","Because the cache key is derived from the judge's output"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A model-graded scorer is subject to the same provider updates as the target; if it moves, every score moves. Pinning it, and re-scoring stored outputs when it changes, keeps comparisons honest. Cost and cache keys are unrelated to the reason."},{"q":"Setting temperature to zero guarantees that two identical eval runs produce identical outputs. True or false, and why?","options":["True, because greedy decoding always selects the same token","True, as long as the seed parameter is also set","False, because batching and non-deterministic GPU arithmetic can change logits, so the noise floor must be measured","False, because the tokenizer is randomised per request"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Greedy decoding is deterministic given identical logits, but logits can differ slightly across runs on hosted infrastructure, and seeds are often best-effort. The practical answer is repeats and a measured noise floor, not an assumption of determinism."},{"q":"What does a scheduled sentinel run of a frozen reference bundle detect?","options":["Regressions introduced by a candidate prompt","Contamination of the golden set","Drift outside the manifest, such as a provider changing the model behind a dated snapshot or an environment change","Whether the cache hit rate is high enough"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Nothing in the sentinel's manifest changes between runs, so any movement beyond the noise floor must come from something the manifest cannot pin. Candidate regressions are caught by the gate, not the sentinel."}]},{"key":"llmops-platform/evaluation-gates-for-production-releases","sources":["Efron & Tibshirani, 1993 — An Introduction to the Bootstrap","Kohavi, Tang & Xu, 2020 — Trustworthy Online Controlled Experiments","Humble & Farley, 2010 — Continuous Delivery","Breck et al., 2017 — The ML Test Score: A Rubric for ML Production Readiness and Technical Debt Reduction"],"why":"Without a gate, the release test for an LLM change is either \"someone looked at a few outputs\" or \"the\naggregate went up\". Both fail in ways that reach users. The aggregate rises one point while the refunds\nslice collapses, because the slice is 3% of the set. An exact-string regression test flakes on a\nrephrased but correct answer, so it is marked flaky and ignored. A \"small prompt tweak\" ships without any\neval because prompts are config, and a provider model upgrade moves every behaviour at once with no\ntest in the path at all.\n\nThe naive gate designs fail too, and knowing why shapes the real one. \"Block if the score is below the\nlast score\" blocks on noise: at a few hundred items a one-point drop is well inside run-to-run variance,\nso engineers learn to re-run until green, which destroys the gate's meaning in a month. \"A human reviews\nevery release\" does not scale and degrades to rubber-stamping within a quarter. \"Only test online with an\nA/B\" is slow, exposes users to the regression before it is detected, and cannot enforce a safety floor,\nsince the point of a floor is that no user ever sees the failure. A gate that works makes the decision\nrule explicit, statistical and auditable: it says which metrics must not fall, by how much, with what\nconfidence, on which items, and who may override it.","how":"### Four kinds of criteria\n1. **Absolute floors.** The safety and adversarial set at or near 100% pass, schema validity above a\n   threshold, zero PII leakage, the regression set at 100%. These are not relative to the champion\n   *because* \"no worse than before\" is not acceptable for a safety property: a flawed champion must not\n   license a flawed candidate. With sampled outputs a floor is checked over repeats: run each safety item\n   *k* times and require every repeat to pass, *because* an answer that is unsafe one time in ten is unsafe\n   for the user who draws it, and a single run would let it through nine times out of ten.\n2. **Non-inferiority against the champion.** The candidate's primary metric must not be worse than the\n   champion's by more than a margin δ, with confidence, on the same dataset version and scorer version,\n   paired by item. δ is set per metric from product risk (one or two points on a pass rate is a common\n   illustrative choice), and it can never be tighter than the measured noise floor.\n3. **Per-slice floors.** The same test per slice with a wider margin (smaller n), or an absolute minimum\n   per slice, *because* the aggregate is a traffic-weighted average that hides rare, high-value cases.\n4. **Operational budgets.** p95 latency, cost per request and output tokens measured in the same run,\n   *because* a prompt that scores two points higher at three times the tokens is a cost regression that\n   the quality metric cannot see.\n\n### The statistics of the relative gate\nFor each item *i*, compute the paired difference *dᵢ* = candidate score − champion score. Pairing matters\n*because* item difficulty is the largest source of variance and it cancels in *dᵢ*; a paired test on 400\nitems is far tighter than comparing two independently measured averages. Estimate the confidence interval\nof the mean difference with a bootstrap: resample the *dᵢ* with replacement a few thousand times,\ncompute the mean each time, and take the 2.5th and 97.5th percentiles. The bootstrap is used *because*\nscores are often bounded, bimodal or judge-graded on a few discrete levels, where a normal approximation\nis shaky and the bootstrap needs no distributional assumption.\n\nReading only the lower end of a two-sided 95% interval makes this a one-sided test at 97.5% confidence,\nthe conventional non-inferiority setup; the question asked is \"could it be worse by more than δ\", not\n\"is it different\".\n\nThe decision has three outcomes, not two. If the interval's lower bound is above −δ the candidate is\nnon-inferior: **pass** (and if the lower bound is above zero it is an improvement you may claim). If the\nupper bound is below −δ the candidate is confidently worse: **block**. If the interval spans −δ the\nevidence is inconclusive: **needs review**, which routes to a larger run or a human, *because* treating\ninconclusive as pass lets regressions through and treating it as fail teaches people to re-run until\nlucky. With many slices, false alarms multiply, so slice verdicts default to review rather than block,\nor margins are widened.\n\nA worked instance (illustrative numbers): 400 items, champion pass rate 0.82, candidate 0.84, mean paired\ndifference +2.0 points, bootstrap 95% interval [−0.5, +4.5]. The interval's width depends on how many\nitems changed verdict between the two bundles: for pass/fail scores the per-item differences are −1, 0\nor +1, so their variance is roughly the discordant fraction, and a half-width of 2.5 points at n = 400\ncorresponds to about one item in fifteen flipping. With δ = 2, the lower bound −0.5 is above −2, so the gate\npasses, but the interval includes zero, so the release note may not claim an improvement. Had the interval\nbeen [−3.5, +0.5], the lower bound would fall below −δ while the upper bound sits above it: inconclusive,\nneeds review, probably a larger paired run.\n\n### Where the gates sit\nDepth follows blast radius, the ladder from the CI/CD lesson. PR: smoke set, hard floors on a small safety\nsubset block, everything else warns. Merge and nightly: full set, all four criteria, block. Release\ncandidate: full set plus sealed holdout, repeats to establish the noise floor, and a human sign-off for\nhigh-risk applications. Canary: online metrics against the champion's live traffic with an automatic\nrollback trigger, *because* offline evals cannot see distribution shift in real traffic. Full rollout\nonly after the canary window closes clean.\n\n### Overrides, configuration and gate fatigue\nAn override is allowed, with a named approver, a reason, an expiry and a record in the registry next to\nthe promotion, *because* unrecorded overrides are how gates die quietly. Gate policy lives as versioned\nconfiguration in the repository, per application, so threshold changes are themselves reviewed. Track\nthe override rate and the \"gate was right\" rate (blocked candidates later confirmed bad); a high override\nrate means the margin is tighter than the noise floor, the golden items are stale, or a scorer is flaky,\nand the fix is to recalibrate the gate rather than to keep overriding it.","example":"A gate that applies absolute floors first, then a paired bootstrap non-inferiority test per slice, then\nthe operational budgets, and returns one of three outcomes. Inputs are per-item scores for candidate and\nchampion aligned by item id on the same dataset and scorer versions.\n\n```python\nimport random, statistics\n\ndef paired_bootstrap_ci(cand: list[float], champ: list[float], iters: int = 2000, seed: int = 0):\n    \"\"\"cand and champ are aligned by item id, same dataset version, same scorer version.\"\"\"\n    d = [c - h for c, h in zip(cand, champ)]\n    rng, n = random.Random(seed), len(d)\n    means = sorted(statistics.mean(rng.choice(d) for _ in range(n)) for _ in range(iters))\n    return statistics.mean(d), means[int(0.025 * iters)], means[int(0.975 * iters)]\n\ndef gate(cand: dict, champ: dict, policy: dict):\n    # 1. absolute floors: never relative to the champion\n    failed = [k for k, floor in policy[\"floors\"].items() if cand[\"floors\"][k] < floor]\n    if failed:\n        return \"block\", {\"failed_floors\": failed}\n    # 2. paired non-inferiority, aggregate and per slice\n    verdicts = {}\n    for name, margin in policy[\"margins\"].items():            # {\"all\": 0.02, \"refunds\": 0.04, ...}\n        mean, lo, hi = paired_bootstrap_ci(cand[\"scores\"][name], champ[\"scores\"][name])\n        if lo > -margin:\n            verdicts[name] = (\"pass\", mean, lo, hi)             # improvement claimable only if lo > 0\n        elif hi < -margin:\n            verdicts[name] = (\"block\", mean, lo, hi)\n        else:\n            verdicts[name] = (\"review\", mean, lo, hi)           # inconclusive is neither pass nor fail\n    # 3. operational budgets measured in the same run\n    for k in (\"p95_latency_ms\", \"cost_per_request\"):\n        if cand[\"ops\"][k] > policy[\"budgets\"][k]:\n            verdicts[k] = (\"block\", cand[\"ops\"][k])\n    states = {v[0] for v in verdicts.values()}\n    outcome = \"block\" if \"block\" in states else (\"review\" if \"review\" in states else \"pass\")\n    return outcome, verdicts\n\npolicy = {\"floors\": {\"safety\": 1.0, \"schema_valid\": 0.995, \"regression\": 1.0},\n          \"margins\": {\"all\": 0.02, \"refunds\": 0.04},\n          \"budgets\": {\"p95_latency_ms\": 2500, \"cost_per_request\": 0.012}}\n```","tradeoffs":["A statistical non-inferiority gate stops flaking on noise, but needs enough items to be decisive; with a small set it can only catch large breaks, so either grow the set or accept a wider margin.","Absolute safety floors are unambiguous, but one mislabeled item blocks every release until fixed, which is acceptable only if the item-review path is fast.","Three outcomes keep humans for the ambiguous cases, yet if most verdicts land in review the queue becomes the bottleneck; that is the signal to raise n or loosen the margin, not to skip review.","Per-slice gates catch hidden regressions, and every added slice adds a chance of a false alarm; past a handful of slices, make slice verdicts review-only or correct for multiple comparisons.","Gating on cost and latency prevents expensive \"improvements\", but ties quality releases to infrastructure variance; measure both bundles in the same run on the same infrastructure."],"pitfalls":["Comparing the candidate against a stored champion score instead of re-running the champion on the same dataset and scorer version; the symptom is every candidate failing after a judge or dataset update.","Treating an inconclusive interval as a pass, or as a fail; the symptom is either regressions reaching canary or engineers re-running the gate until the dice land right.","A margin tighter than the noise floor; the symptom is an unchanged bundle failing the gate against itself.","Gating on the aggregate only; the symptom is a slice dropping fifteen points while the headline metric rises one.","Checking a 100% safety floor with one sample per item at production temperature; the symptom is a floor that passed in CI and a jailbreak that reproduces in production on the second or third attempt.","Overrides with no record; the symptom is a post-incident review that cannot find who approved the release the gate had blocked.","Gating only on the smoke set; the symptom is the nightly full run discovering what the PR gate missed, after the merge."],"when":"Install a gate wherever a change to code, prompt, model, parameters or index can reach users, and start\nsmall: hard floors plus one paired non-inferiority test on the primary metric, with slices added as error\nanalysis reveals them and block-versus-warn configured per stage. Measure the gate: pass, block and\nreview rates, override rate, time to verdict, the \"gate caught it\" rate and escaped regressions found by\nthe canary. Day to day it is the thing that turns \"the eval looks fine\" into a verdict with a confidence\ninterval attached; in interviews, expect to explain why the test is paired, what the margin means, why\nthere are three outcomes, and which criteria must be absolute.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements the paired bootstrap and the policy file, makes the pipeline re-run the champion alongside the candidate, and wires the verdict into the registry promotion step.","architect":"Owns the gate policy per application: which metrics are absolute, the margins and their relation to the noise floor, which stages block, and who may override with what record.","fde":"Translates a customer's acceptance criteria into floors and margins on their golden set, so go-live is a reproducible verdict rather than a negotiation over a demo."},"check":{"q":"A candidate prompt scores 85.0% against the champion's 83.5% on 300 items, and the product manager\nwants to ship it as an improvement. What does your gate say, and what do you tell the PM?","a":"First confirm both runs share the dataset and scorer versions and that the champion was re-run,\nnot read from history. Then compute the paired bootstrap interval on the 300 per-item differences; at\nthis size a 1.5-point mean difference will very likely have an interval that includes zero, so the gate\ncannot certify an improvement. Whether it can even report non-inferior for a two-point margin depends on\nhow many items flipped: the lower bound is about 1.5 minus the half-width, so it clears −2 only if the\nhalf-width is under 3.5 points, which at n = 300 means roughly fewer than one item in ten changing\nverdict; with more churn than that the verdict is needs-review, not pass. Check the absolute\nfloors, the per-slice verdicts and the cost and latency budgets, since a longer prompt often buys its\npoints with tokens. Tell the PM the change is safe to ship if the gate passes and everything else does too, that \"improvement\"\nneeds either a larger paired run or a canary that shows it in live metrics, and that the release note\nshould say \"no regression\" until then."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does the gate compare candidate and champion on identical items with a paired test rather than against last week's champion score?","options":["Because last week's run is too expensive to look up","Because item difficulty cancels in paired differences and the pins (dataset, scorer, model) are guaranteed equal","Because the champion's score changes every day due to caching","Because a paired test does not require a confidence interval"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Pairing removes the dominant source of variance and forces both bundles through the same dataset and scorer versions. A historical score may have been produced by a different judge, dataset or model snapshot, so a difference against it has no clear cause."},{"q":"The bootstrap interval for candidate minus champion is [−3.5, +0.5] points and the margin is 2 points. What is the verdict?","options":["Pass, because the interval includes positive values","Block, because the mean difference is negative","Needs review, because the interval spans the margin and the evidence is inconclusive","Pass, because the upper bound is above zero"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The lower bound is below −2 and the upper bound is above it, so the data neither establishes non-inferiority nor confirms a regression. The honest outcome routes to a larger paired run or a human, not to a coin flip."},{"q":"Which criterion should be an absolute floor rather than a relative test against the champion?","options":["Average answer length","The judge's helpfulness score on the full set","The pass rate on the prompt-injection and safety set","p50 latency"],"answer":2,"explanation":"\"No worse than the champion\" is the wrong standard for a safety property, since a flawed champion would license a flawed candidate. Helpfulness is legitimately relative, and latency belongs to the operational budget rather than to a safety floor."}]},{"key":"llmops-platform/canary-shadow-deployments","sources":["Kohavi, Tang & Xu, 2020 — Trustworthy Online Controlled Experiments","Humble & Farley, 2010 — Continuous Delivery"],"why":"A change to an AI system is non-local. Swapping the model, editing three lines of the system prompt or\ntightening a tool schema changes behaviour on *every* request, including the long tail your golden set\nnever sampled: odd phrasings, a language you did not test, tool outputs that are longer than expected,\na tenant whose documents are all scanned PDFs. A conventional service change touches one code path and\na unit test proves it; an LLM change touches a probability distribution and only traffic reveals it.\n\nThe naive approach is \"the eval gate passed, deploy to 100%\". It fails for three reasons. The golden\nset is small and ages: it reflects what users asked when it was built. Judge-based scores are noisy, so\na two-point gain on 300 cases is often within noise. And some regressions are invisible offline because\nthey depend on production conditions: cold prompt caches, rate limits on the new model, latency under\nreal concurrency, or a tool that behaves differently with real credentials. A big-bang deploy puts the\nwhole user base inside the blast radius for the hours it takes a quality regression to show up in\nfeedback.\n\nShadow and canary deployments exist to buy information about live behaviour at a bounded cost. They\ndiffer in what they buy: shadow buys a like-for-like comparison with no exposure but no user feedback;\ncanary buys real user outcomes at the price of real exposure.","how":"### What a \"version\" is\nThe thing you roll out is a bundle, not a model: model id (pinned snapshot), prompt template version,\ndecoding parameters, tool schemas, retrieval index version and guardrail configuration. Both patterns\nneed the bundle to be addressable as one immutable unit, because a canary that mixes a new prompt with\nan old index is testing a combination you never evaluated and cannot roll back to.\n\n### Shadow (traffic mirroring)\n1. The gateway receives a request and serves it with the incumbent as usual.\n2. It forks a copy to the candidate asynchronously, after the user response is on its way, so the\n   user never waits on the candidate.\n3. Both outputs are logged under the same trace id along with latency, tokens, cost, schema validity,\n   refusal flags and tool calls.\n4. An offline comparison job scores pairs: deterministic checks on 100% of pairs, LLM-judge pairwise\n   preference on a sample, and disagreement analysis (where do the two differ most, by intent or tenant).\n\nShadow is safe *because* nothing the candidate produces leaves the system. That same property is its\nlimit. Side-effecting tools must be stubbed or sandboxed, because an agent in shadow must not send the\nemail twice. Multi-turn effects are invisible, because the user's next message responds to the\nincumbent's answer, not the candidate's. And there is no user feedback at all, so shadow answers \"is it\ndifferent and is it cheaper or slower\", not \"do users prefer it\". The mirrored slice also doubles\ninference cost on that slice, so most teams shadow 5–20% of traffic (illustrative) or shadow 100% for a\nshort window.\n\nOne more trap is specific to LLMs: the incumbent disagrees *with itself*. At non-zero temperature, and\noften even at temperature zero on hosted APIs, running the same request twice yields different text. So\n\"B's answer differs from A's on 30% of requests\" is meaningless until you know A-vs-A differs on, say,\n25%. Mirror a small slice to a second copy of the *incumbent* as well, and read the candidate's\ndisagreement rate as the excess over that self-disagreement floor, broken down by intent.\n\n### Canary (partial live exposure)\n1. The router assigns each request to a bucket by hashing a stable key, usually user or session id, and\n   routes buckets below the canary percentage to the candidate. Hashing *because* a conversation must not\n   flip models mid-thread; random per-request assignment produces incoherent sessions and contaminates\n   the comparison.\n2. Guard metrics are watched on both arms over a bake window: error rate, p95 latency, schema-failure\n   rate, refusal rate, cost per request, tool-failure rate, and whatever quality proxy you trust\n   (thumbs-down rate, escalation rate, judge score on a sample).\n3. The percentage ramps in steps, for example 1% → 5% → 25% → 50% → 100%, each step held long enough to\n   collect a minimum sample. Small first steps *because* the point of the first step is to catch\n   catastrophic breakage (wrong schema, 429s from the new model's quota) with the fewest users exposed.\n4. Any guard breach triggers automatic rollback of the pointer to the incumbent; promotion to 100%\n   requires that the success metrics are not worse, not merely that nothing exploded.\n\n### What the numbers can and cannot tell you\nDetecting a shift of size δ in a metric with standard deviation σ needs, per arm, roughly\n\n```\nn ≈ 16 · σ² / δ²        (rule of thumb: two-sided α = 0.05, 80% power)\nσ² = p(1 − p) for a rate p\n```\n\nso halving the effect you want to see quadruples the traffic you need. A 1% canary on a service doing\n20,000 requests a day gives 200 canary requests a day. A schema-failure rate jumping from 0.5% to 5%\n(σ² ≈ 0.027 at the midpoint, δ = 0.045) needs about 16 × 0.027 / 0.002 ≈ 210 samples: about a day, and\nin practice faster because the incumbent's baseline is known precisely. A judge score moving from 4.1 to\n4.0 with σ ≈ 0.8 needs about 16 × 0.64 / 0.01 ≈ 1,000 samples per arm: a week at 1%, an afternoon at 25%.\nSo the early steps are for catastrophic signals, and the later, larger steps are where quality\ncomparisons become meaningful. Treat the canary as a controlled experiment only when the two\narms are assigned randomly and compared on the same window; comparing this week's canary to last\nweek's baseline confounds the version with time.\n\n### Canary vs A/B test vs blue-green\nThese are often conflated. A **canary** is a safety mechanism: the question is \"is anything broken?\",\nthe decision is asymmetric (roll back on harm, promote on *no harm*), and it ends at 100%. An **A/B\ntest** is a decision mechanism: the question is \"which is better on the success metric?\", it holds a\nfixed split until the pre-registered sample is reached, and either arm may win. A large canary step can\ndouble as an A/B test only if you pre-commit to the metric and sample size. **Blue-green** keeps two full\nenvironments and flips all traffic at once; it gives instant rollback but no gradual exposure, so it\nsuits infrastructure changes better than behaviour changes.\n\n### Where it lives\nRouting, hashing, mirroring and the rollout percentage belong in the AI gateway or control plane as\nconfiguration, not in application code, so a rollout or rollback is a config change with no deploy.","example":"A minimal gateway router: sticky canary assignment by user hash, an asynchronous shadow fork for a\nseparate candidate, and paired logging. The shadow call is scheduled as a background task so the user's\nresponse never waits on it, only incumbent-served requests are mirrored (so every shadow pair is\ncandidate-vs-incumbent), and the shadow candidate runs with side-effecting tools disabled. Each\nexperiment salts the hash, so the same 5% of users are not the guinea pigs for every rollout.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio, hashlib, json, time\n\nROLLOUT = {\"incumbent\": \"bundle-v12\", \"canary\": \"bundle-v13\", \"canary_pct\": 5,\n           \"shadow\": \"bundle-v14\", \"shadow_pct\": 20, \"salt\": \"exp-2026-10-v13\"}\n_background: set[asyncio.Task] = set()            # keep refs so tasks are not GC'd mid-run\n\ndef bucket(key: str, salt: str) -> int:           # stable 0..99 bucket per user per experiment\n    return int(hashlib.sha256(f\"{salt}:{key}\".encode()).hexdigest(), 16) % 100\n\nasync def call_bundle(bundle: str, req: dict, allow_side_effects=True) -> dict:\n    t = time.perf_counter()\n    out = await llm_app.run(bundle, req, allow_side_effects=allow_side_effects)\n    return {\"bundle\": bundle, \"text\": out.text, \"schema_ok\": out.schema_ok,\n            \"refused\": out.refused, \"cost\": out.cost, \"ms\": (time.perf_counter() - t) * 1e3}\n\nasync def shadow(req: dict, served: dict, trace_id: str):\n    cand = await call_bundle(ROLLOUT[\"shadow\"], req, allow_side_effects=False)\n    log.info(json.dumps({\"trace\": trace_id, \"served\": served, \"shadow\": cand}))\n\nasync def handle(req: dict, user_id: str, trace_id: str) -> dict:\n    b = bucket(user_id, ROLLOUT[\"salt\"])\n    bundle = ROLLOUT[\"canary\"] if b < ROLLOUT[\"canary_pct\"] else ROLLOUT[\"incumbent\"]\n    served = await call_bundle(bundle, req)\n    metrics.observe(bundle=bundle, ms=served[\"ms\"], schema_ok=served[\"schema_ok\"],\n                    refused=served[\"refused\"], cost=served[\"cost\"])\n    if bundle == ROLLOUT[\"incumbent\"] and bucket(user_id, \"shadow\") < ROLLOUT[\"shadow_pct\"]:\n        task = asyncio.create_task(shadow(req, served, trace_id))  # off the critical path\n        _background.add(task); task.add_done_callback(_background.discard)\n    return served\n# ROLLOUT is loaded from the control plane; promoting or rolling back is a config write, not a deploy.\n```","tradeoffs":["Shadow gives a like-for-like comparison with zero user exposure, but you pay roughly double inference on the mirrored slice and learn nothing about user preference or multi-turn behaviour.","Canary gives real outcomes, but every percent of exposure is real users seeing a possibly worse system; once the change is low-risk and well-evaluated offline, the extra caution costs more than it saves.","Sticky (hashed) assignment keeps conversations coherent and the experiment clean, but it means a bad canary hits the same users repeatedly, so a 5% canary is a 100% regression for those users.","Small early steps limit blast radius but slow the rollout; for a change with a hard deadline (a vendor deprecation date) you may have to accept larger steps and lean on shadow results instead.","Automatic rollback on guard metrics removes humans from the loop at 3 a.m., but with noisy quality metrics and small samples it will roll back good releases unless thresholds carry a minimum sample."],"pitfalls":["Shadowing an agent with live tools; the symptom is duplicate emails, tickets or payments appearing with no user action behind them.","Random per-request routing instead of per-session hashing; the symptom is users reporting that the assistant \"changed personality\" mid-conversation and metrics that look fine in aggregate.","Comparing the canary to a historical baseline rather than a concurrent control; the symptom is a \"regression\" that disappears when you notice the canary window included a product launch.","Judging the canary on a quality score after 150 samples; the symptom is rollbacks and promotions that flip from day to day with no change in the system.","Ignoring warm-up effects: the candidate model has a cold prompt cache and a fresh rate-limit bucket; the symptom is a latency and 429 spike in the first minutes that disappears, then gets blamed on the version.","Canarying only internal users; the symptom is a clean canary followed by a wave of complaints from the customer segment that writes in a different language or uploads different documents.","Reading the shadow disagreement rate without an incumbent-vs-incumbent baseline; the symptom is a \"40% disagreement\" alarm that turns out to be ordinary sampling variation in both versions."],"when":"Shadow first when the change is large or structurally different: a new model family, a new provider, a\nrewritten agent loop, a new retrieval pipeline. Its output tells you whether the candidate is safe\nenough to put in front of anyone and where it disagrees with the incumbent, which is where to look. Move\nto a canary when you need user outcomes to decide, or when the change is incremental and offline evals\nalready pass. Skip both for trivial, fully reversible changes with strong offline coverage, where the\ncost of the process exceeds the risk. Measure guard metrics on both arms, demand a minimum sample before\nany quality verdict, and automate the rollback path before the first ramp. In interviews, the expected\ndepth is to explain why sticky assignment matters, why shadow cannot test multi-turn agents, and how\nsample size limits what a 1% canary can detect.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements the hashed router and asynchronous mirroring in the gateway, tags every trace with the bundle id, and builds the paired-comparison job that scores shadow output.","architect":"Decides what the deployable bundle is, where rollout state lives (control plane, not code) and which guard metrics gate each ramp step for which risk class of change.","fde":"Runs shadow mode against a customer's live traffic before cutover so the customer sees a disagreement report instead of a surprise, and negotiates which users form the first canary cohort."},"check":{"q":"You are migrating a customer-support agent from model A to model B. The agent can issue refunds.\nOffline evals show B slightly ahead. Design the rollout and justify each step.","a":"Start with shadow at a meaningful slice of traffic with the refund tool disabled for the shadow\narm, because a side-effecting tool cannot be mirrored safely. Score pairs on deterministic checks\n(schema validity, refusal rate, tool-call mix, latency, cost) at 100% and judge preference on a sample;\nanalyse where B disagrees most with A by intent. If nothing is alarming, canary B to about 1% of\nsessions by user hash, with automatic rollback on error rate, schema failures, latency and\nrefund-rate anomalies; hold until a minimum sample accrues, then ramp through 5, 25 and 50%. Use\nthumbs-down and escalation rate as success metrics at the larger steps, where samples allow it. Keep\nA's bundle warm and the routing in control-plane config so rollback is a pointer flip, and note that\nB's refund behaviour in canary is the one thing shadow could not test, so it gets the tightest alert."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why is a shadow deployment unable to evaluate a multi-turn conversational agent properly?","options":["Because shadow traffic cannot be logged alongside the served response","Because the user's next turn responds to the incumbent's answer, so the candidate never gets the conversation it would have produced","Because shadow deployments only work for stateless classification models","Because the candidate's latency makes the mirrored copy time out"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Shadow forks the same input to both versions, but a conversation diverges after the first reply. The candidate sees follow-ups shaped by the incumbent, so its multi-turn behaviour is never observed."},{"q":"A team runs a 1% canary on a service with 10,000 requests per day and wants to know within a day whether the judge score moved from 4.2 to 4.1. What is the right assessment?","options":["Sufficient: 100 samples is enough for any metric","Sufficient, provided the judge runs at temperature zero","Insufficient: detecting a small shift in a noisy score needs far more samples; the 1% step is for catching catastrophic failures","Insufficient only because the canary should run for a week regardless of traffic"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Sample size needed grows roughly with the inverse square of the effect size. A 100-request slice can reveal a schema-failure spike but cannot resolve a 0.1-point judge difference; use larger ramp steps for quality verdicts."},{"q":"Why should canary assignment hash on user or session id rather than pick randomly per request?","options":["Hashing is faster than random number generation at gateway scale","Random assignment would exceed the canary percentage over time","Per-session stickiness keeps conversations coherent and prevents the arms from contaminating each other's results","Hashing lets the canary cohort be chosen from internal users only"],"answer":2,"explanation":"If consecutive turns of one conversation hit different versions, users see inconsistent behaviour and each arm's metrics include context produced by the other arm, which invalidates the comparison."}]},{"key":"llmops-platform/rollback","sources":["Beyer et al., 2016 — Site Reliability Engineering","Humble & Farley, 2010 — Continuous Delivery"],"why":"For a stateless web service, rollback is a solved problem: redeploy the previous container image. AI\nsystems break that model in three ways.\n\nFirst, the deployable unit is a bundle of artefacts with different lifecycles and different stores. The\nprompt lives in a registry, the model behind a provider API or a GPU fleet, the retrieval index in a\nvector database, the tool schemas in code, the guardrail policy in a config service. Rolling back one\nof them without the others yields a combination that was never evaluated: yesterday's prompt against\ntoday's model is a new version, not the old one.\n\nSecond, some changes destroy their own undo path. Re-embedding a corpus in place with a new embedding\nmodel overwrites the vectors the old prompt was tuned against, and the old query-embedding model\ncannot be used against the new vectors at all, because the two models' vector spaces are not\ncomparable. Overwriting a fine-tuned checkpoint or adapter without retaining the previous one leaves\nnothing to return to. A vendor retiring the previous\nsnapshot removes the old model entirely.\n\nThird, the failures that justify a rollback are usually quality regressions, visible only through\nlagging signals (thumbs-down rate, escalations, judge scores), not through a crash. By the time a human\nnotices, the bad version has been serving for hours. The naive alternative, \"just tweak the prompt to\nfix it\", compounds the problem: every edit under pressure is another unevaluated version, and the\noriginal cause is now buried under three patches. Rollback exists so that the first move in an incident\nis a known-good state, after which diagnosis can happen calmly.","how":"### Make versions immutable and deployment a pointer\nEvery release is a manifest that pins each component: model id with snapshot date, prompt version hash,\ndecoding parameters, tool schema version, index id, guardrail policy version, and the feature flags\nthat were on. Manifests are immutable and stored in the registry. The environment, say `prod`, is an\nalias pointing at one manifest. Deploying is moving the alias forward; rolling back is moving it to the\nprevious manifest. This is O(1) *because* nothing is rebuilt, re-embedded or re-trained; only a pointer\nchanges, and the data plane picks up the new pointer on its next config refresh.\n\nThe rule that follows: you may only roll back to a manifest that has previously served production (or\npassed the same gates). Hand-assembling \"old prompt + new model\" during an incident is a forward change\nin disguise.\n\n### Keep the previous version reachable\nA pointer is only useful if its target still exists and is warm.\n- Self-hosted models: keep the previous weights loaded or at least on local disk; loading tens of\n  gigabytes from object storage can take minutes (illustrative), which is the whole rollback budget.\n- Provider models: pin snapshots, and track the vendor's deprecation calendar so the \"previous\" model is\n  not one that was retired last month.\n- Indexes: build the new index alongside the old (blue/green), switch the alias, keep the old index\n  until the new one has baked. Never re-embed in place.\n- Fine-tunes: keep the previous adapter or checkpoint and the training data that produced it.\n\n### Decide triggers before the release\nRollback should be a decision made in advance and executed by a rule, not debated in the incident\nchannel. Guard metrics with thresholds and windows (error rate, p95 latency, schema-failure rate,\nrefusal rate, cost per request, tool-failure rate) trigger automatically; quality signals (judge score\non a sample, thumbs-down rate, escalation rate) usually trigger a human decision because they are\nnoisier and slower. Each trigger carries a minimum sample size, *because* a threshold evaluated on\ntwenty requests produces flapping.\n\nAutomatic triggers should also be scoped to a **bake window** after a promotion (hours to a few days).\nInside the window a guard breach is most likely caused by the release, so reverting it is the right\nbet. Outside it, a breach is more often external, such as a provider incident, a traffic shift or a\nbroken upstream tool, and rolling back to a version that depends on the same provider fixes nothing\nwhile discarding a good release. After the window, breaches page a human instead.\n\n### Handle what a pointer flip does not restore\nRollback restores behaviour, not data. Things to check after the flip:\n- Caches keyed without the version: a semantic or response cache filled by the bad version will keep\n  serving its answers. Key caches by manifest id, or purge on rollback.\n- Conversations in flight: a session whose earlier turns and compaction summaries were produced by the\n  bad version continues under the old one; usually acceptable, but worth knowing when reading feedback.\n- Agent memory and side effects: notes the bad version wrote to long-term memory, tickets it filed,\n  emails it sent. Rollback does not undo them; the audit log identifies them for cleanup.\n- Downstream consumers: if another team cached embeddings or outputs from the bad version, tell them.\n- State written in a new shape: if the new version stored tool results, memory records or structured\n  outputs in a new schema, the old version may fail to read them after the flip. The fix is the same\n  expand-then-contract discipline used for database migrations: a release must write data the previous\n  manifest can still read, and drop the old shape only once the release is past its bake window.\n\n### Rehearse it\nA rollback that has never been executed is a hypothesis. Exercise it in CI (deploy v2, roll back to\nv1, run a smoke eval) and in periodic game days, and time it against the MTTR target in the runbook.","example":"A registry with immutable manifests, an environment alias, and a guard loop that rolls back when a\nmetric breaches its threshold with enough samples. Promotion records history so rollback has a\nvalidated target.\n\n```python\nimport time\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass, field\n\n@dataclass(frozen=True)\nclass Manifest:\n    id: str; model: str; prompt: str; params: dict; tools: str; index: str; guardrails: str\n\n@dataclass\nclass Registry:\n    manifests: dict = field(default_factory=dict)\n    alias: dict = field(default_factory=dict)      # env -> manifest id\n    history: dict = field(default_factory=dict)    # env -> [manifest ids that served]\n    promoted_at: dict = field(default_factory=dict)\n\n    def promote(self, env: str, mid: str):\n        assert mid in self.manifests, \"unknown manifest\"\n        self.history.setdefault(env, []).append(mid)\n        self.alias[env] = mid                      # O(1): a pointer move, nothing rebuilt\n        self.promoted_at[env] = time.time()\n        cache.invalidate(namespace=env)            # cached answers belonged to the old version\n\n    def rollback(self, env: str) -> str:\n        served = self.history[env]\n        assert len(served) >= 2, \"nothing validated to roll back to\"\n        served.pop()                               # drop the bad one\n        self.alias[env] = served[-1]\n        cache.invalidate(namespace=env)\n        return served[-1]\n\nGUARDS = {\"schema_fail_rate\": (0.02, 200), \"p95_ms\": (6000, 200), \"refusal_rate\": (0.10, 200)}\nBAKE_S = 48 * 3600                                 # auto-rollback only while the release is fresh\n\ndef check_guards(reg: Registry, env: str, window: dict) -> str | None:\n    for metric, (limit, min_n) in GUARDS.items():\n        if window[\"n\"] >= min_n and window[metric] > limit:\n            if time.time() - reg.promoted_at[env] > BAKE_S:\n                alerts.page(f\"{env}: {metric} breach outside bake window; human decision\")\n                return None\n            target = reg.rollback(env)\n            alerts.page(f\"{env}: {metric}={window[metric]:.3f} > {limit}; rolled back to {target}\")\n            return target\n    return None\n```","tradeoffs":["Keeping the previous model warm makes rollback instant but costs GPU memory or a second serving pool; for infrequent releases a slower cold rollback may be the cheaper choice.","Automatic rollback shortens time-to-recover at night but, if thresholds lack minimum samples or metrics are noisy, it rolls back healthy releases and trains people to ignore it.","Bundle-level rollback is safe because the target was validated, but it is coarse: it reverts an unrelated good change that shipped in the same manifest, which argues for small manifests.","Blue/green indexes make retrieval changes reversible at the cost of double storage during the bake window; for very large corpora that cost can dominate and force a partial-rebuild strategy.","Immutable manifests and aliases impose registry discipline on every team; the overhead is real but it is what makes \"which version served this request\" answerable at all."],"pitfalls":["Re-embedding the corpus in place; the symptom is a retrieval regression after a model change with no way back except a multi-hour rebuild.","Caches not keyed by version; the symptom is \"we rolled back but users still see the bad answers\" for as long as the cache TTL.","Relying on the vendor to keep serving the previous model; the symptom is a rollback target that returns a model-not-found error during the incident.","Rolling back only the prompt because \"the model did not change\", when the model alias silently updated underneath; the symptom is a rollback that does not restore the old behaviour.","Never rehearsing; the symptom is a rollback runbook that fails on a permissions error at the exact moment it is needed.","Treating rollback as the whole fix; the symptom is memory entries, tickets or emails created by the bad version surfacing days later because nobody reconciled the audit log.","Auto-rolling back on every breach regardless of release age; the symptom is a good release reverted during a provider outage, with the outage still present on the old version."],"when":"Rollback is the first move whenever a release correlates with a guard breach or a credible quality\nregression, and diagnosis comes second; the cost of being wrong about a rollback is small if the\nprevious version was validated, while the cost of debugging live is paid by users. Prefer fixing\nforward only when the previous version is itself broken (a vendor retired it, an index was overwritten)\nor when the fault is clearly outside the release. A quick test for the latter: if the incumbent arm\nof a still-running canary, or a version that has not changed, shows the same degradation, the release\nis not the cause and a rollback will not help. Measure time-to-rollback in rehearsals, keep it in\nminutes, and make the trigger list part of every release plan. In day-to-day work this looks like\nmanifests in a registry, aliases in a control plane and a rollback button that engineers trust. In\ninterviews, the strong answer explains why an AI rollback is harder than a container rollback and what\nhas to be true beforehand for a pointer flip to actually restore behaviour.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds releases as immutable manifests, keys caches by manifest id, keeps the previous weights or index reachable, and wires guard metrics to the rollback action.","architect":"Defines the deployable unit, the retention policy for previous versions (how many, how warm), and which triggers are automatic versus human, balancing recovery time against cost.","fde":"Agrees rollback triggers and the rollback runbook with the customer before go-live, and verifies in the customer's environment that the previous version is actually still deployable."},"check":{"q":"A release changed the prompt and switched the embedding model, re-indexing the corpus in place.\nTwo days later answer quality drops. Walk through what rollback can and cannot do here and what you\nwould change for next time.","a":"The prompt can be rolled back instantly if it is versioned in a registry and the environment\nalias can be repointed. The index cannot: re-embedding in place destroyed the old vectors, and the\nquery side cannot go back to the old embedding model either, because old-model query vectors are\nmeaningless against new-model document vectors. So a prompt-only rollback runs the old prompt against\nretrieval from the new model, a combination never evaluated and possibly worse. The honest options are to rebuild the old index from source (hours),\nor to keep the new index and fix the prompt forward with a proper canary. For next time: treat the\nbundle as one manifest, build the new index blue/green beside the old and switch an alias, keep the old\nindex through a bake window, key any response cache by manifest id, and define the quality trigger\nand minimum sample before release so the decision happens on day one rather than day two."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why is \"roll back the prompt but keep the new model\" risky even when the model change looked harmless?","options":["Prompt registries cannot store more than one version per model","It produces a combination that was never evaluated, so it is a forward change rather than a return to a known-good state","Models cache the prompt, so the old prompt will not take effect","It doubles inference cost during the transition"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A rollback is only safe because its target previously passed gates and served traffic. Mixing components from different manifests creates a new, unvalidated version under incident pressure."},{"q":"After rolling back, users keep receiving the bad version's answers for an hour. What is the most likely cause?","options":["The rollback pointer takes an hour to propagate through DNS","The old model needs an hour to warm its KV cache","A response or semantic cache was keyed without the version id, so it still serves answers produced by the bad version","The guard metrics window is one hour long"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Rollback restores the code path, not the data it produced. A cache filled by the bad version keeps serving until its TTL unless caches are keyed by manifest or purged on rollback."},{"q":"Which practice most directly ensures that a retrieval-index change can be rolled back?","options":["Storing the embedding model name in the prompt","Lowering the index's HNSW parameters so rebuilds are faster","Building the new index beside the old one and switching an alias, keeping the old index during a bake window","Re-embedding only documents that changed since the last release"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Blue/green indexing preserves the old vectors, so rollback is an alias switch. In-place re-embedding overwrites them and turns rollback into a multi-hour rebuild."}]},{"key":"llmops-platform/model-monitoring","sources":["Sculley et al., 2015 — Hidden Technical Debt in Machine Learning Systems","Breck et al., 2017 — The ML Test Score","Beyer et al., 2016 — Site Reliability Engineering"],"why":"Service monitoring assumes failures are visible: a request errors, times out or is slow. An LLM\napplication fails differently. The model answers confidently with the wrong refund policy; the\nretriever returns last year's price list; the agent stops calling the search tool because the vendor\nquietly updated the model and it now prefers to answer from memory. All of these are HTTP 200, p95\nwithin budget, dashboards green.\n\nGround truth is also late or absent. A classifier in a bank gets labels when the loan defaults, months\nlater; a support assistant never learns whether the customer's problem was solved unless you build the\nloop. So you cannot monitor \"accuracy\" directly the way you monitor error rate.\n\nThe naive alternatives each cover one corner. \"We have evals in CI\" measures a fixed set at release\ntime; production is a different distribution and it changes after release, while the model itself can\nchange underneath a provider alias. \"We watch user complaints\" is sparse, biased toward the angriest\nusers and days late. \"We log everything\" produces terabytes nobody reads. Monitoring exists to make\nthe question \"is the system behaving as it did when we validated it?\" answerable continuously, cheaply\nand per segment, with a path from any alert to the concrete requests behind it.","how":"### Layer 1: operational signals\nThe ordinary SRE set, tagged with AI-specific dimensions: request rate, error rate by class (provider\n429s, 5xxs, timeouts, guardrail blocks), latency as time-to-first-token and total, input and output\ntokens, cost per request, tool-call failures and retries. Standard plumbing, but every series must\ncarry model id, prompt version, bundle id and tenant as labels, *because* the first question after any\nanomaly is \"which version, which customer\".\n\n### Layer 2: behavioural proxies (no labels needed)\nCheap functions of the output computed on 100% of traffic: refusal rate (a small classifier or pattern\nmatch), schema-validation failure rate for structured outputs, output length distribution, empty or\ntruncated responses (finish reason), repetition, language mismatch between input and output, tool-call\nrate and which tools, citation presence in RAG answers, retrieval score distribution, \"I don't know\"\nrate. None of these is quality. Each is a proxy whose *shift* is informative: if the refusal rate\ndoubles after a model update, something changed, and you know within minutes rather than after the\nweekly feedback review. Proxies are chosen *because* they are deterministic, free, and move early.\n\n### Layer 3: quality and outcome signals\n- Online judge: an LLM scores a sample of live responses against a rubric (groundedness, completeness,\n  policy adherence). Sampled *because* a judge call is another LLM inference over the full prompt and\n  response, often comparable to or more than the cost of the request it scores; 1–5% is a common order\n  of magnitude (illustrative), stratified so small tenants are still covered.\n- Human review queues: low-judge-score or flagged traces routed to reviewers; the labels feed both the\n  monitor's calibration and the golden set.\n- Explicit feedback: thumbs, ratings, \"report\". Sparse and selection-biased, so track the rate of\n  negative feedback per hundred responses rather than its raw count.\n- Implicit feedback: regenerate, copy, abandon, escalate to human, task completion. Higher volume, less\n  precise; correlate once with human labels to learn what each means.\n- Business outcomes: resolution rate, conversion, handle time. The slowest signal and the one leaders\n  believe.\n\nEach signal has a label latency: implicit feedback in seconds, explicit in minutes, judge in minutes to\nhours, business metrics in days. Monitoring design is largely about using fast proxies to decide where\nto spend slow, expensive labels.\n\n### Slicing, baselines and alerting\nAggregates hide regressions. A 2% drop in overall judge score can be a 40% drop for one tenant or one\nintent. Slice every metric by version, tenant, intent class, language and input length bucket. Compare\nagainst a baseline that controls for time: the same hour last week, or the incumbent version in a\nconcurrent canary. Alert on sudden shifts and on slow trends separately, and require a minimum sample\nper window *because* judged metrics on thirty requests are noise. Route operational breaches to the\npager and quality shifts to a ticket with the top disagreeing examples attached.\n\nSlicing has a statistical cost that is easy to miss: every slice is another hypothesis test. With 200\nslices each checked hourly at a threshold that fires falsely 5% of the time, you should expect about ten\nfalse alerts per run from noise alone. Two standard defences: make the threshold depend on the slice's\nown standard error (a drop must exceed roughly two standard errors of the difference, so small slices\nneed bigger drops), and require the breach to persist across consecutive windows before alerting.\n\n### From proxies to SLOs\nThe proxies that matter most can be written as service-level objectives in the SRE sense: \"99% of\nextraction responses pass schema validation over 28 days\" or \"refusal rate on the billing intent stays\nbelow 3%\". That gives each one an error budget, and you alert on how fast the budget is burning rather\nthan on single bad windows: a fast burn (a large fraction of the month's budget gone in an hour) pages,\na slow burn opens a ticket. This is the same machinery used for availability, applied to behaviour,\nand it is what stops quality alerting from becoming either silent or noisy.\n\n### Close the loop\nEvery metric should be derivable from traces so an alert can drill into the requests behind it, and\nevery reviewed failure should become a golden-set case. Monitoring that does not feed the eval suite\nfinds the same regression twice.","example":"A windowed monitor over logged events: proxies on all requests, judge scores on the sampled subset,\ncompared per version against a baseline window with a minimum-sample guard.\n\n```python\nimport math\nfrom collections import defaultdict\nfrom statistics import mean, stdev\n\nMIN_N = 200                       # no verdicts on tiny windows\nLIMITS = {\"refusal_rate\": 0.05, \"schema_fail_rate\": 0.02, \"judge_drop\": 0.3}\n\ndef summarise(events):            # events: dicts from the trace store, one per request\n    by = defaultdict(list)\n    for e in events:\n        by[(e[\"bundle\"], e[\"tenant\"])].append(e)\n    out = {}\n    for key, es in by.items():\n        judged = [e[\"judge\"] for e in es if e.get(\"judge\") is not None]   # sampled subset\n        out[key] = {\"n\": len(es),\n                    \"refusal_rate\": mean(e[\"refused\"] for e in es),\n                    \"schema_fail_rate\": mean(not e[\"schema_ok\"] for e in es),\n                    \"p95_ms\": sorted(e[\"ms\"] for e in es)[math.ceil(0.95 * len(es)) - 1],  # nearest rank\n                    \"cost_per_req\": mean(e[\"cost\"] for e in es),\n                    \"judge_mean\": mean(judged) if judged else None, \"judge_n\": len(judged),\n                    \"judge_sd\": stdev(judged) if len(judged) > 1 else None}\n    return out\n\ndef judge_dropped(c, b, z=2.0):   # drop must be large relative to its own noise\n    se = math.sqrt(c[\"judge_sd\"] ** 2 / c[\"judge_n\"] + b[\"judge_sd\"] ** 2 / b[\"judge_n\"])\n    return b[\"judge_mean\"] - c[\"judge_mean\"] > max(LIMITS[\"judge_drop\"], z * se)\n\ndef alerts(current, baseline):\n    found = []\n    for key, c in current.items():\n        if c[\"n\"] < MIN_N:\n            continue\n        b = baseline.get(key)\n        for m in (\"refusal_rate\", \"schema_fail_rate\"):\n            if c[m] > LIMITS[m] and (b is None or c[m] > 2 * b[m]):\n                found.append((key, m, c[m], \"page\"))\n        if b and c[\"judge_n\"] >= 50 and b[\"judge_n\"] >= 50 and judge_dropped(c, b):\n            found.append((key, \"judge_mean\", c[\"judge_mean\"], \"ticket+examples\"))\n    return found\n# Run hourly: alerts(summarise(last_hour), summarise(same_hour_last_week)).\n# With sd ≈ 0.8 and 50 judged per side, se ≈ 0.16, so only drops above ~0.32 fire: the\n# floor of 50 judged requests is what bounds the smallest regression this slice can see.\n```","tradeoffs":["Judge sampling at 1% is cheap but a small tenant may get five scored requests a day; stratified sampling fixes coverage at the cost of a more complex sampler.","Proxy metrics are free and fast but indirect; a refusal-rate drop can mean a better model or a jailbroken one, so proxies trigger investigation, not conclusions.","Fine slicing catches per-tenant regressions but multiplies alert series and shrinks samples per slice; past a point the right move is anomaly detection on the slice set rather than fixed thresholds.","Online judges give continuous quality signal but inherit judge bias and drift when the judge model itself is updated; pin the judge and re-calibrate it against human labels periodically.","Capturing full prompts and outputs makes every alert debuggable but creates a sensitive data store with retention and access obligations; hashing or redaction trades debuggability for safety."],"pitfalls":["Monitoring only HTTP health; the symptom is a week of green dashboards followed by a customer escalation about answers that have been wrong since the last model update.","Alerting on raw judge scores without a sample floor; the symptom is 3 a.m. pages caused by twelve unlucky requests, followed by the alert being muted.","Events not tagged with bundle, prompt and model version; the symptom is a regression you can see but cannot attribute to a release, so rollback becomes guesswork.","Treating thumbs-down counts as representative; the symptom is optimising for the loudest segment while the silent majority's task completion falls.","Dashboards without drill-down; the symptom is an alert that says \"quality down 8%\" and a two-hour hunt for a single example.","Letting the judge model auto-update; the symptom is a step change in every quality series on the day the vendor shipped, with no change in your system.","Fixed thresholds across hundreds of slices; the symptom is a daily stream of one-window alerts on small tenants that never reproduce, until the whole channel is muted."],"when":"From the first production deployment: operational metrics and the cheapest proxies (schema failures,\nrefusals, length, finish reason) sliced by version cost almost nothing and catch the loud failures.\nAdd sampled judging once you have a rubric that correlates with human labels, and add feedback\ncapture as soon as the product surface allows it. Invest in slicing when you have more than a handful\nof tenants or intents. What to measure is determined by what the system is for: a RAG assistant\nneeds groundedness and citation rate; an agent needs tool-failure and step-count distributions; a\nstructured extractor needs schema validity and field-level agreement on a sample. In interviews, the\nexpected answer distinguishes proxies from quality, explains label latency, and shows how an alert\nleads to examples and then to a new eval case.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Emits version-tagged events, implements the proxy checks and the sampler, and builds the drill-down from an alert to the traces that caused it.","architect":"Chooses the signal set per use case, the judge sampling and calibration policy, the slicing dimensions, and which alerts page versus ticket, within the data-retention constraints.","fde":"Agrees with the customer which outcome metric counts as success, wires their feedback channels into the monitor, and reviews the per-tenant slice with them during hypercare."},"check":{"q":"Your support assistant's provider model alias updated overnight. Nothing crashed. Describe how a\nwell-designed monitor would surface the change within hours and what it would show you.","a":"Operational series would show a cost-per-request or latency step change tagged to the same bundle\nid, since the bundle did not change but the model behind the alias did. Behavioural proxies would move\nfirst: output length distribution, refusal rate, tool-call rate or schema-failure rate shifting at the\nsame timestamp across all tenants, which is the signature of an upstream change rather than a user\nmix change. The sampled online judge would show a score shift within a few hours once the minimum\nsample accrues, with the lowest-scoring traces attached for review. Explicit feedback would lag by a\nday. Where the provider returns the resolved model snapshot in response metadata, logging it would show\nthat value changing at the same timestamp, which is the root cause; the fix is to pin snapshots and\ntreat any model change, alias update included, as a release that goes through shadow or canary."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why are behavioural proxies such as refusal rate and schema-failure rate monitored on every request while judge scores are sampled?","options":["Judge scores are only valid on requests with user feedback","Proxies are deterministic and nearly free to compute, whereas each judge call is another LLM inference, often as costly as the request it scores","Proxies are more accurate measures of answer quality than judges","Sampling is required by data-protection rules for judged content"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Proxies are cheap functions of the output, so full coverage is affordable; judging adds a second inference per scored request, so it is sampled and used to decide where expensive human labels go."},{"q":"A monitor reports overall judge score unchanged after a release, yet one enterprise customer complains loudly. What design gap most likely explains it?","options":["The judge model was not run at temperature zero","The release did not include a prompt change","Metrics were not sliced by tenant, so a large drop in one tenant was hidden by the aggregate","Explicit feedback was not collected from that customer"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Aggregates average across tenants and intents. A regression confined to one segment can be invisible in the overall mean, which is why every series is sliced by version, tenant and intent."},{"q":"Which alert design most reduces false pages from a noisy quality metric?","options":["Alert on the metric's raw value every five minutes","Alert only when a human reviewer confirms the drop","Require a minimum sample per window and compare against a time-matched baseline before alerting","Replace the judge with a keyword match"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Judged metrics vary widely on small samples and with time of day. A sample floor and a time-matched baseline separate real shifts from noise and from weekly seasonality."}]},{"key":"llmops-platform/drift","sources":["Gama et al., 2014 — A Survey on Concept Drift Adaptation","Quiñonero-Candela et al., 2009 — Dataset Shift in Machine Learning","Lopez-Paz & Oquab, 2017 — Revisiting Classifier Two-Sample Tests","Chen, Zaharia & Zou, 2023 — How Is ChatGPT's Behavior Changing over Time?"],"why":"Every evaluation is a snapshot. A golden set, a canary and an offline benchmark all say \"the system\nbehaved acceptably on *this* distribution at *this* time\". Nothing in that statement survives a change\nin either variable. Users start asking about a product launched after the corpus was indexed. A new\ncustomer segment writes in a language the prompt was never tuned on. A regulation changes, so the\ncorrect answer to \"can I return this?\" flips while the question text does not. The vendor retrains the\nmodel behind the alias you call. Each of these quietly moves production away from the thing you\nvalidated, and none of them raises an error.\n\nThe naive responses fail in predictable ways. \"We'll re-run the golden set weekly\" catches regressions\non old questions, not failures on new ones, because the golden set is frozen on the old distribution.\n\"We'll watch the quality metric\" is right in spirit but late in practice: judged quality needs enough\nsamples to be significant and labels arrive days later, so a drift that began on Monday shows up as a\nquality alert the following week. \"We'll retrain or re-index on a schedule\" is a cost with no\ndiagnosis: it fixes some drifts, cannot fix others (a prompt that is now wrong), and tells you nothing\nabout which one you had.\n\nDrift as a concept exists to separate three questions that get conflated: *what* moved (inputs,\noutcomes, or a component of the system), *how* it moved (abrupt, gradual, seasonal) and *whether it\nmatters* (did the shift change the error rate on the slice it affected). Only the third question\njustifies action, but you cannot answer it without the first two.","how":"### The statistical vocabulary\nTreat the system as a function from inputs **X** to outputs **Y**, validated on data drawn from a\njoint distribution P(X, Y). Drift is any change in that joint distribution, and it is useful to name\nwhich factor moved:\n\n- **Covariate shift (data / input drift):** P(X) changes, P(Y|X) does not. The questions are different,\n  but each question still has the same right answer. A support bot starts receiving shipping\n  questions instead of billing questions. The system may still be correct; it may also now be\n  operating in a region it was never validated on.\n- **Prior / label shift:** P(Y) changes while P(X|Y) stays put. The mix of correct outcomes moves. A fraud classifier sees\n  fraud go from 1% to 3% of traffic; thresholds tuned for the old base rate are now wrong even if the\n  model's scores are unchanged.\n- **Concept drift:** P(Y|X) changes. The same input now has a different correct output. Policy\n  changes, price changes, a new law, a renamed product. This is the dangerous one because it is\n  invisible in the inputs: the questions look identical, and only ground truth reveals the change.\n- **Prediction / output drift:** the distribution of what the system *emits* changes. Observable on\n  every request without labels, which makes it the cheapest early-warning signal, but ambiguous: it\n  happens under any of the three above and also when the system itself changes.\n\nFor a generative system Y is free text, so read it as \"the correct behaviour\": label shift then means,\nfor example, that the share of requests whose right response is \"escalate to a human\" or \"refuse\" has\nmoved, which matters because prompts and thresholds were tuned for the old mix.\n\nDrift also has a shape (the Gama et al. taxonomy): **sudden** (a release, a provider update),\n**gradual** (new segment ramping up), **incremental** (slow vocabulary change) and **recurring**\n(seasonality: tax season, Black Friday). The shape decides the detector: a sudden shift needs a short\nwindow and a change-point test; a gradual one needs a long window and a trend test; a recurring one\nneeds a baseline from the same season, not last week.\n\n### What drifts in an LLM application that did not drift in classic ML\nIn classic ML the model was yours and frozen; only the world moved. In LLM systems the components move\ntoo, and each is a separate drift source with its own detector and fix:\n\n- **Upstream model drift:** a provider updates the model behind an alias, or changes a default. Same\n  prompt, different behaviour across *all* tenants at one timestamp. Detected by proxy metrics\n  shifting globally at once and by the response's reported model id. Fixed by pinning snapshots and\n  treating upgrades as releases.\n- **Prompt and configuration drift:** someone edits the system prompt, a tool description, or a\n  temperature without going through the release path. Detected by hashing the resolved bundle on every\n  request and alerting when the hash changes without a deploy.\n- **Knowledge / corpus drift:** the RAG index falls behind the world (stale prices, retired policies).\n  Inputs and model are unchanged; answers are confidently out of date. Detected by document age at\n  retrieval time and by groundedness checks against the *current* source of truth, not the index.\n- **Embedding drift:** the embedder is upgraded but the index was built with the old one; query and\n  document vectors no longer live in the same space. Detected by a collapse in retrieval scores on a\n  fixed probe set. Fixed by versioning embeddings with the index and re-indexing together.\n- **Judge drift:** the LLM-as-a-judge is itself an alias that gets updated; every quality series steps\n  on the day the judge changed. Detected by re-scoring a fixed, human-labelled calibration set on a\n  schedule.\n- **Feedback-loop drift:** users adapt to the system (they learn which phrasings work) and the\n  system's outputs enter its own corpus or training data. Slow and self-reinforcing; detected only by\n  comparing against held-out, never-served data.\n\n### Detecting it\nDetection is always a comparison between a **reference window** (the traffic you validated on, or the\nsame period last cycle) and a **current window**, per feature and per slice:\n\n1. Choose the signal. For categorical features (intent, language, tenant, tool called, finish reason)\n   use the bucket distribution. For numeric ones (input length, retrieval score, latency) bucket them\n   or use the empirical CDF. For free text, embed it and compare the embedding clouds.\n2. Pick a statistic. **PSI** (population stability index) for bucketed distributions:\n   `PSI = Σ_b (cur_b − ref_b) · ln(cur_b / ref_b)`, a symmetric, interpretable number where 0 means\n   identical. The credit-risk rule of thumb treats under 0.1 as stable, 0.1–0.25 as worth a look, and\n   above 0.25 as a real shift; those cut-offs are convention, not theory, and should be re-derived from\n   your own history. **KS test** for continuous features. **Jensen–Shannon divergence** when you want\n   a bounded, symmetric measure. For embeddings, the **classifier two-sample test**: train a classifier\n   to tell reference from current; an AUC near 0.5 means indistinguishable, and the classifier's most\n   confident \"current\" examples show *what* moved, which a single divergence number never does.\n3. Control for noise. Require minimum window sizes, compare like with like (same weekday, same\n   season), and slice by tenant and intent, because a global PSI of 0.03 can hide one tenant at 0.6.\n4. Separate \"the world moved\" from \"the system moved\". Re-run a **frozen probe set** (a fixed slice of\n   the golden set with known-good outputs) on a schedule and whenever an alert fires. It is a control:\n   its inputs never change, so if its scores move, a component changed (model, prompt, embedder, judge);\n   if it is stable while production metrics move, the world changed. This one check halves most drift\n   investigations.\n5. Separate \"moved\" from \"broke\". A detected shift raises priority for the slow signals: route more of\n   that slice to judging and human review, and check whether error rate on it changed. Input drift\n   with stable outcomes is information, not an incident. Concept drift can only be confirmed by\n   labels, so the detector's job is to decide where to spend them.\n\n### Responding\nEach drift type has a different fix, which is why diagnosis comes first: new inputs → extend the\ngolden set and prompt coverage; concept drift → update the corpus, policy text or labels, then\nre-validate; upstream model change → pin, evaluate, canary; embedding mismatch → re-index; judge shift\n→ re-calibrate and pin. Retraining is the answer to one of these, not to all of them. Whatever the\nfix, the reference window moves forward afterwards, because the newly validated distribution is the\nnew baseline.","example":"Two detectors on one week of support-assistant traffic: PSI on the intent mix, and a classifier\ntwo-sample test on input embeddings. The intent shift is real but moderate; the embedding test on a\nsynthetic shift shows what a clearly separable week looks like (AUC ≈ 0.87–0.88) versus an unshifted\none (≈ 0.5 on the same code, give or take cross-validation noise).\n\n```python\nimport numpy as np\nfrom sklearn.linear_model import LogisticRegression\nfrom sklearn.model_selection import cross_val_score\n\ndef psi(expected, actual, eps=1e-6):\n    \"\"\"Population Stability Index between two bucket distributions (fractions summing to 1).\"\"\"\n    e, a = np.asarray(expected) + eps, np.asarray(actual) + eps\n    return float(np.sum((a - e) * np.log(a / e)))\n\n# Intent mix: reference week vs this week (fractions of requests)\nref = [0.30, 0.25, 0.20, 0.15, 0.10]      # billing, shipping, returns, account, other\ncur = [0.22, 0.20, 0.33, 0.15, 0.10]\nprint(f\"intent PSI = {psi(ref, cur):.3f}\")  # 0.101: 'returns' rose from 20% to 33%; look at that slice\n\ndef embedding_drift_auc(ref_emb, cur_emb):\n    \"\"\"Classifier two-sample test: can a model tell this week's inputs from the reference week's?\"\"\"\n    X = np.vstack([ref_emb, cur_emb])\n    y = np.r_[np.zeros(len(ref_emb)), np.ones(len(cur_emb))]\n    clf = LogisticRegression(max_iter=1000)\n    return cross_val_score(clf, X, y, cv=5, scoring=\"roc_auc\").mean()\n# AUC 0.5 = indistinguishable. Use a few thousand embeddings per window, then inspect the inputs the\n# classifier is most confident are 'current' to see what actually changed.\n\nrng = np.random.default_rng(0)\nref_emb = rng.normal(0, 1, (2000, 64))\ncur_emb = rng.normal(0, 1, (2000, 64)); cur_emb[:, :8] += 0.6   # shift along a few directions\nprint(f\"embedding drift AUC = {embedding_drift_auc(ref_emb, cur_emb):.2f}\")   # ~0.87-0.88\n# Ceiling for a linear separator: Phi(0.6*sqrt(8)/sqrt(2)) = Phi(1.2) ~ 0.885\n```\n\nReading the PSI by hand: the returns bucket contributes (0.33 − 0.20) · ln(1.65) ≈ 0.065, billing\n(0.22 − 0.30) · ln(0.733) ≈ 0.025, shipping ≈ 0.011, the two unchanged buckets 0. The total of ≈ 0.10\nsays the mix moved noticeably; whether it matters depends on whether the returns slice, now a third of\ntraffic, was well covered by the prompt and golden set.","tradeoffs":["Label-free detectors (PSI, embedding tests, output proxies) are cheap and immediate but cannot see concept drift; the moment ground truth lags by weeks, you need both, and the detectors decide where the scarce labels go.","Short windows catch sudden shifts fast but alert on noise; long windows are stable but late. Running both, with different thresholds, is usually cheaper than tuning one window to do both jobs.","Fine slicing exposes per-tenant drift that aggregates hide, yet each slice shrinks the sample and multiplies alert series; beyond a few dozen slices, rank slices by drift score rather than alerting on each.","Pinning every component (model snapshot, embedder, judge) makes drift attributable and removes a whole class of surprises, at the price of a scheduled upgrade burden and the risk of running on a model the provider is about to retire.","Moving the reference window forward after each validated change keeps detectors relevant, but if you move it without validating you quietly normalise the drift you were supposed to catch."],"pitfalls":["Treating a drift alert as a defect; the symptom is a team retraining or re-prompting in response to an input shift whose outcomes were fine, and a worse system afterwards.","Comparing this week against last week during seasonal traffic; the symptom is a recurring alert every Monday or every month-end that everyone has learned to ignore, which then masks a real shift.","Monitoring inputs and outputs but not the components; the symptom is a global step change in every metric with no deploy in the log, traced days later to a provider alias update or a judge upgrade.","Upgrading the embedding model without re-indexing; the symptom is retrieval scores collapsing on the fixed probe set and answers that cite irrelevant documents, while the model itself is unchanged.","Letting the golden set stay frozen while traffic moves; the symptom is a green eval suite alongside rising complaints from a segment that has no representation in it.","Running a PSI with empty buckets and no smoothing; the symptom is infinite or NaN scores that crash the job or get silently clipped, hiding exactly the new categories that signal drift."],"when":"Drift detection belongs in any system that will run for longer than its validation data stays\nrepresentative, which is every production system. Start with the cheap signals on day one: intent and\nlanguage mix, input length, output proxies, and a hash of the resolved bundle so configuration drift\nis caught instantly. Add embedding-based input drift once you have a few thousand requests per window,\nand judge re-calibration once you rely on an LLM judge. Measure drift per slice and feed each\nconfirmed shift back into the golden set, because the shift that moved production is exactly the\ndistribution your next evaluation must contain. In interviews, the expected answer distinguishes\ncovariate shift from concept drift, explains why the LLM-specific sources (model, prompt, corpus,\nembedder, judge) need pinning and hashing, and insists that a detected shift is a question about\noutcomes, not an answer.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements the window comparisons, bundle hashing and slice-level PSI jobs, and wires a drift score into the sampler so shifted slices get judged and reviewed first.","architect":"Decides which components are pinned, how reference windows are chosen and advanced, and the policy that turns a confirmed shift into a re-validation and a golden-set update.","fde":"Spots customer-side drift first: a new product line, a reorganised knowledge base or a changed policy, and gets it into the corpus and the regression suite before the complaints arrive."},"check":{"q":"A claims-extraction system's accuracy on its monthly human-reviewed sample fell from 94% to 87%.\nInput-length and field-mix PSIs are below 0.05, and no deploy happened. Walk through how you would find\nthe cause and what you would do for each possibility.","a":"First confirm the drop is real: on a review sample of a few hundred cases, seven points is\nwell outside sampling noise, but on fifty it may not be. Stable input statistics then rule out the\nobvious covariate shift, so look at the components and at concept drift. The fastest split is to\nre-run the frozen probe set: if it also dropped, the system changed even though nobody deployed; if it\nheld, the world changed. Check the model id reported in responses and the bundle hash per request: if the\nprovider alias moved, every tenant's proxies will have stepped at one timestamp, and the fix is to pin\nthe previous snapshot, evaluate the new one and canary it. Check the judge or review process: if the\nreviewers' guidelines changed, the \"drop\" is a measurement change. Then test for concept drift: pull\nthe failed cases and see whether the correct extraction changed, for example a new claim form layout\nor a renamed field; inputs can look statistically identical while the mapping changed. That fix is\nprompt and golden-set updates plus re-validation. Finally run the classifier two-sample test on\ndocument embeddings, because a layout change can be invisible to length and field-mix buckets while\nbeing obvious in embedding space."},"quiz":[{"q":"Which kind of drift cannot be detected by any statistic computed on inputs and outputs alone?","options":["Covariate shift, because input distributions are high-dimensional","Prediction drift, because outputs are free text","Concept drift, because the inputs and outputs can look identical while the correct output has changed","Upstream model drift, because the provider does not announce it"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Concept drift is a change in P(Y|X): the same question now has a different right answer. Only labels or ground truth can reveal it. Input, output and model-id signals all detect the other types."},{"q":"An intent-mix PSI of 0.10 is reported for one tenant. What is the correct next step?","options":["Roll back to the previous bundle immediately","Retrain or re-prompt, since the distribution has moved","Route that tenant's slice to more judging and review to learn whether outcomes changed","Raise the threshold, since 0.10 is within normal variation for all systems"],"answer":2,"explanation":"PSI says the inputs moved, not that the system is wrong. A shift of this size justifies spending labels on that slice; action follows only if the error rate on the shifted traffic has changed."},{"q":"Why does the classifier two-sample test on embeddings add something a divergence score does not?","options":["It is the only test that works on categorical data","The classifier's most confident \"current\" examples show what moved, not just that something moved","It needs no reference window","It detects concept drift without labels"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A single divergence number says how far apart two windows are. A trained discriminator can be inspected: the inputs it is surest belong to the new window are the new kind of traffic, which is what you need to extend the golden set and prompt."}]},{"key":"llmops-platform/model-lifecycle-and-deprecation-policy","sources":["Official docs: OpenAI model deprecations","Official docs: Anthropic model deprecations","Official docs: MLflow Model Registry","Sculley et al., 2015 — Hidden Technical Debt in Machine Learning Systems"],"why":"Models do not stay still. Providers retire snapshots on published schedules and ship replacements with\ndifferent behaviour. Self-hosted weights are superseded by a better fine-tune, a cheaper quantisation\nor a security fix in the serving stack. Embedding models are upgraded, which invalidates every index\nbuilt with the old one. Each of these is a change to something dozens of prompts, bundles and tenants\ndepend on, usually built by people who have since moved to another project.\n\nThe naive approach is to treat model versions like library versions: pin them and upgrade when\nconvenient. That fails because the supply side is not under your control. A pinned provider snapshot\nhas a shutdown date; on that date requests fail, or are silently redirected to a successor that\nbehaves differently. Teams discover this through an incident, then migrate a dozen bundles in a week\nwithout the evaluation they would normally require, and ship regressions to avoid an outage. The\nopposite naive approach, \"always use the latest alias\", converts every provider release into an\nunreviewed production change.\n\nA second failure is the internal one. A platform team that offers models to twenty product teams\nbecomes a provider itself. If it retires a fine-tuned model without a notice period, a freeze window\nand a migration path, it causes the same incident internally that the external provider would have\ncaused. Lifecycle and deprecation policy exist so that \"this version will stop existing\" is a planned,\nevaluated, dated event with an owner, in both directions of the supply chain.","how":"### States and transitions\nA version moves through a small state machine; the registry lesson describes the alias mechanics,\nthis one describes the policy around them. A typical set: **registered** (artifact and lineage\nrecorded, no claims made) → **evaluated** (offline gates passed, evidence attached) → **staged**\n(deployable to pre-production) → **canary** (serving a fraction of production) → **production**\n(the champion for one or more aliases) → **deprecated** (still served, no new consumers, sunset date\nset) → **retired** (not served; requests fail with a clear error) → **archived** (bytes kept for\nthe retention period, then deleted). Transitions are restricted *because* each state is a promise to\nconsumers: production → retired is forbidden, not merely discouraged, so that nobody can switch off a\nmodel that still has callers. Each transition records who, when and why, which makes the lifecycle\nitself an audit trail.\n\n### Evidence per gate\nEach forward move names its evidence: evaluated needs the eval report against the current golden set\nand slice results; staged needs integration tests against the real serving config; canary needs the\nrollback plan and the monitoring dashboard for the candidate; production needs the canary verdict and\nsign-off from the owning team. The policy fixes *what* is required, not *how good* the numbers must\nbe; thresholds belong to the use case's evaluation gates. This split exists *because* the lifecycle is\nplatform-wide while quality bars are domain-specific.\n\n### Provider models have a lifecycle too\nA hosted model registered as a virtual version carries the provider's snapshot id, its announced\nretirement date if any, and the recommended successor. Providers publish deprecation schedules and\ntypically give advance notice of weeks to months; the exact period varies by provider, by model tier\nand by whether the model was generally available or a preview, and providers revise these terms, so\nthe policy reads the provider's current page rather than assuming a number. The\nplatform mirrors each external deprecation as an internal one, *because* consumers should learn about\nretirements from one place with one format. Some clouds offer deployments that auto-upgrade to the\nsuccessor when a version retires; the policy should decide explicitly whether that is allowed, since\nit is a silent model change in production.\n\n### The deprecation workflow\n1. **Decide and date.** Triggers: provider sunset, a validated successor, a security or compliance\n   finding, cost, or a version with no owner. The sunset date is set at least the policy's notice\n   period ahead, except for security retirements, which have their own expedited path.\n2. **Announce.** The version is marked deprecated in the registry, and every consumer is notified\n   through the channel they registered with. Notification is targeted, not broadcast, *because* \"we\n   emailed everyone\" is how migrations get missed.\n3. **Inventory consumers.** The platform must be able to answer \"who still calls this version\". That\n   comes from the gateway's per-request telemetry and the bundle registry's pins, never from asking\n   around. Each consumer gets a tracked migration item with an owner.\n4. **Freeze.** From a fixed date before sunset, no new bundle may pin the deprecated version, and CI\n   fails on such pins. Existing traffic continues. This stops the consumer list growing while you are\n   trying to shrink it.\n5. **Migrate with evaluation.** Each consumer evaluates the successor against its own golden set,\n   fixes prompts where behaviour changed, and promotes through the normal canary path. The\n   scheduled model-upgrade job from the CI/CD lesson is the mechanism; the policy is what makes it\n   mandatory rather than optional. \"Same quality on the golden set\" is not the whole check, because a\n   successor usually changes more than answers: its tokenizer (so token counts, cost and effective\n   context length move), latency profile, rate limits and quota, refusal behaviour, structured-output\n   and tool-calling support, and default parameters. The migration checklist covers each, *because*\n   the regressions that slip through are typically a cost or a 429 surprise, not a quality drop.\n6. **Brownout.** In the last weeks, the deprecated version is made unavailable for short, announced\n   windows. Consumers who ignored every notice discover the dependency during a planned ten-minute\n   window instead of at sunset. Brownouts exist *because* an error in a test window costs less than\n   the same error on the retirement date.\n7. **Retire and archive.** Serving stops; requests return a specific error naming the successor.\n   Weights, config and evaluation evidence stay archived for the retention period the policy sets,\n   *because* audits, incident investigations and reproducibility all need to re-run the model that\n   was in production on a given date. Deletion is its own dated, recorded event. For a *provider*\n   model there are no weights to keep: once the vendor retires the snapshot, its behaviour can never\n   be re-run. What you can archive is the bundle (prompts, parameters, tool schemas), the evaluation\n   evidence and logged inputs and outputs on a representative sample, which is why the policy should\n   capture that record *before* the sunset, while the model still answers.\n\n### Compatibility promises\nThe policy states what a consumer may assume across versions: which fields of the response are\nstable, whether output format is guaranteed, and that behaviour is *not* guaranteed to be identical.\nMany platforms adopt an \"N−1\" or \"N−2\" rule: the current major version and the one or two before it\nare supported, older ones are deprecated automatically. That rule bounds the number of versions\nanyone must keep evaluated, monitored and secure at once.","example":"A lifecycle state machine with restricted transitions, and a deprecation planner that turns a sunset\ndate into dated obligations plus a list of consumers still pinned to the old version. Durations are\nillustrative policy values, not a standard.\n\n```python\nfrom datetime import date, timedelta\n\nTRANSITIONS = {                       # what may follow each state\n    \"registered\": {\"evaluated\", \"retired\"},\n    \"evaluated\":  {\"staged\", \"retired\"},\n    \"staged\":     {\"canary\", \"retired\"},\n    \"canary\":     {\"production\", \"staged\"},        # back to staged = candidate withdrawn\n    \"production\": {\"deprecated\"},                  # never straight to retired: consumers must move first\n    \"deprecated\": {\"retired\", \"production\"},       # un-deprecate allowed, with a recorded reason\n    \"retired\":    {\"archived\"},\n}\n\ndef move(version, new_state, actor, reason):\n    if new_state not in TRANSITIONS[version[\"state\"]]:\n        raise ValueError(f\"{version['id']}: {version['state']} -> {new_state} not allowed\")\n    version[\"history\"].append((date.today(), version[\"state\"], new_state, actor, reason))\n    version[\"state\"] = new_state\n\nPOLICY = {\"notice_days\": 90, \"freeze_days\": 60, \"brownout_days\": 14}   # illustrative\n\ndef deprecation_plan(version, sunset, consumers):\n    \"\"\"Sunset date -> dated obligations and the consumers still to chase.\"\"\"\n    todo = [c[\"bundle\"] for c in consumers\n            if c[\"pinned_model\"] == version[\"id\"] and not c.get(\"migration_pr\")]\n    return {\"announce_by\": sunset - timedelta(days=POLICY[\"notice_days\"]),\n            \"freeze_new_pins\": sunset - timedelta(days=POLICY[\"freeze_days\"]),\n            \"brownouts_from\": sunset - timedelta(days=POLICY[\"brownout_days\"]),\n            \"sunset\": sunset, \"unmigrated\": todo}\n\nv = {\"id\": \"chat-model@2025-03-01\", \"state\": \"production\", \"history\": []}\nmove(v, \"deprecated\", actor=\"platform-team\", reason=\"provider sunset announced\")\nplan = deprecation_plan(v, date(2026, 12, 15), consumers=[\n    {\"bundle\": \"support-assistant@41\", \"pinned_model\": v[\"id\"]},\n    {\"bundle\": \"claims-extractor@7\", \"pinned_model\": v[\"id\"], \"migration_pr\": 812},\n])\nprint(plan)   # announce_by 2026-09-16, freeze 2026-10-16, brownouts 2026-12-01, unmigrated: support-assistant@41\n```\n\nThe consumer list comes from the bundle registry's pins joined with gateway telemetry; each\nunmigrated entry becomes a ticket owned by the bundle's team, and the freeze date is enforced by a CI\ncheck on pinned model ids.","tradeoffs":["Long notice periods give consumers time to evaluate properly but keep more versions alive, each of which must stay monitored, patched and paid for; the balance shifts toward shorter notice when versions are cheap to keep and toward longer when migrations need re-prompting and re-indexing.","Mirroring provider deprecations internally gives consumers one calendar, but makes the platform the messenger for dates it does not control; when the provider shortens a window, the platform absorbs the pressure.","Freezing new pins early shrinks the migration list but blocks a team that legitimately needs the old behaviour for one more release; an exception path with an expiry date handles that without reopening the freeze.","Allowing auto-upgrade deployments removes the sunset incident but turns every retirement into an unreviewed behaviour change; acceptable for low-stakes internal tools, not for regulated or customer-facing flows.","Archiving weights and evidence for years satisfies audit and reproducibility, but costs storage and creates a sensitive artifact store; the retention period should follow the compliance requirement, not be set to \"forever\" by default."],"pitfalls":["Learning about a provider retirement from an error; the symptom is a week of emergency migrations, skipped evaluations and a regression that gets blamed on the new model rather than the process.","Deprecating by broadcast email; the symptom is a sunset day with several unknown consumers, found only because their error rate went to 100%.","Retiring without an inventory; the symptom is the platform team asking in chat \"does anyone still use model X?\" and being wrong.","No freeze window; the symptom is a migration list that grows during the migration because new bundles keep pinning the version everyone was told to leave.","Retiring the version and deleting the artifact in the same step; the symptom is an audit or an incident post-mortem that needs to reproduce last quarter's behaviour and cannot.","Treating an embedding model like a chat model in the lifecycle; the symptom is a successor promoted without re-indexing, and retrieval quality collapsing on the promotion day.","Validating the successor on quality alone; the symptom is a migration that passes every eval and then blows the monthly cost forecast or hits 429s, because tokenization, pricing or quota differ."],"when":"A written lifecycle and deprecation policy is worth having as soon as more than one team depends on a\nmodel you serve or pin, or as soon as you have a production dependency on a provider snapshot, which\nis almost immediately. Start small: name the states, forbid the dangerous transitions, require an\ninventory before any retirement, and set a notice and freeze period you can honour. Measure it by how\nmany retirements happened as planned, dated events versus incidents, and by how many consumers were\nstill unmigrated at the freeze date. Day to day, it shows up as the scheduled upgrade job opening PRs,\nCI rejecting pins to deprecated versions, and a dashboard of versions by state. In interviews, expect\nto be asked how you would handle a provider sunset across many teams; the strong answer has an\ninventory, a freeze, per-consumer evaluation, brownouts and archival, in that order.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Pins snapshot ids in bundles, runs the upgrade job's evaluation when a successor is announced, and adds the CI check that fails builds pinning a deprecated version.","architect":"Owns the state machine, the notice and freeze periods, the retention rule for retired artifacts, and the decision on whether auto-upgrading deployments are permitted anywhere.","fde":"Translates a provider's deprecation into a migration plan the customer can approve, runs the evaluation on the customer's golden set, and schedules the cutover inside their change window."},"check":{"q":"A provider announces that the snapshot behind your most-used model retires in four months. Twelve\nbundles across five teams pin it. Describe the plan, including what you would refuse to do.","a":"First register the deprecation internally with the sunset date and the recommended successor, so\nall five teams see one calendar. Build the inventory from bundle pins joined with gateway telemetry,\nthen open one tracked migration item per bundle with the owning team named. Set a freeze date, say\ntwo months out, after which CI rejects new pins to the old snapshot. Each team evaluates the successor\nagainst its own golden set and canaries it through the normal path, because the successor's behaviour\nis not identical; the platform provides the upgrade job but does not do the evaluation for them. In\nthe last weeks, run announced brownouts to flush unknown consumers. On sunset, retire with an error\nnaming the successor and archive evidence for the retention period. Refuse to enable silent\nauto-upgrade on customer-facing bundles and refuse to skip evaluation to meet the date; if a team\ncannot migrate in time, the fallback is a short, dated exception, not an unreviewed switch."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does a sound lifecycle forbid moving a version directly from production to retired?","options":["Retired versions cannot be archived without a deprecated step in the history","The deprecated state is where consumers are inventoried and migrated; skipping it switches off a model that still has callers","Provider APIs require a deprecation flag before a snapshot can be deleted","It prevents the registry from reusing the version number"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Deprecated is a serving state with a sunset date, during which the consumer list shrinks to zero under observation. Retiring straight from production skips that work and causes an outage."},{"q":"What is the purpose of a freeze window before a model's sunset?","options":["To stop all traffic to the version so consumers notice early","To give the provider time to prepare the successor","To stop new bundles pinning the version so the migration list cannot grow while it is being worked down","To lock the golden set so evaluations are comparable"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Freezing new pins, enforced in CI, bounds the migration; stopping traffic is what brownouts do later, briefly and by announcement, to find consumers the inventory missed."},{"q":"Why keep a retired self-hosted model's weights and evaluation evidence after it stops serving?","options":["So it can be promoted back if the successor fails","Because providers require it for billing reconciliation","Audits and incident investigations may need to reproduce the behaviour that was in production on a given date","To seed the training data for the next fine-tune"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Archival is about reproducibility and accountability for past decisions, for a defined retention period. Rolling back to a retired version is not a goal; the successor has its own rollback target."}]},{"key":"llmops-platform/observability-stack","sources":["Official docs: OpenTelemetry Semantic Conventions for Generative AI","Official docs: OpenTelemetry Collector","Sigelman et al., 2010 — Dapper, a Large-Scale Distributed Systems Tracing Infrastructure","Beyer et al., 2016 — Site Reliability Engineering"],"why":"A single team with one chatbot can get by with a vendor SDK that logs prompts and completions to a\nhosted dashboard. The moment there are several teams, several models and a compliance function, that\narrangement breaks in four places at once. Each team instruments differently, so there is no way to\nanswer \"what did we spend on model X across the company\" or \"which tenants were affected by the\nprovider's outage\". Prompts containing customer data flow into whichever SaaS the team picked, with\nretention nobody checked. A request that crosses the gateway, the application, a retriever and three\ntool calls appears as four unrelated records in four systems. And the evaluation loop, which needs\nto pull failed traces into a dataset and re-score them, has no join key between the trace store and\nthe eval tool.\n\nThe naive fix, \"pick one vendor and make everyone use it\", solves consistency and creates lock-in:\nevery span is emitted in that vendor's format, every dashboard is theirs, and migrating means\nre-instrumenting every service. The other naive fix, \"reuse the existing APM stack\", gives you\nlatency and errors but not tokens, cost, prompt versions, judge scores or the ability to open a span\nand read the completion, because classic APM has no notion of a generation.\n\nAn observability stack exists to separate the concerns: a neutral wire format at the edge so\ninstrumentation is written once; a collection layer where policy (redaction, sampling, routing) is\napplied centrally; stores chosen per signal type; and LLM-specific analysis tools that consume the\nsame data rather than owning it. The goal is that any alert, invoice line or quality score can be\ntraced back to the exact requests behind it, across teams, without the data leaving your control.","how":"### Layer 1: instrumentation\nApplications emit traces: a tree of spans per request, with one span per model call, retrieval,\ntool call and guardrail check. OpenTelemetry is the standard SDK and wire format *because* it is\nvendor-neutral and already carries W3C trace context across HTTP and queue boundaries, which is what\nmakes the gateway's span and the application's span part of one trace. The GenAI semantic\nconventions name the LLM-specific attributes: operation, provider, requested and actual model, input\nand output token counts, finish reasons, and optionally the prompt and completion content as events.\nThose conventions are still evolving and attribute names have changed between versions, so the\nplatform pins a version and keeps a mapping layer. On top of the standard attributes, every span\ncarries the platform's own keys: tenant, user (pseudonymous), bundle and prompt version, config\nversion, and request id. These are mandatory *because* every later question is a group-by on them.\n\n### Layer 2: collection\nServices send to an OpenTelemetry Collector rather than straight to a backend. The collector is where\nplatform policy runs: an attributes or redaction processor strips or hashes payload fields according\nto the tenant's data classification; a tail-sampling processor keeps 100% of traces with errors, low\nscores or high latency and a small fraction of the rest; a batch processor reduces write amplification;\nexporters fan the same stream out to several backends. Centralising this exists *because* each\napplication team getting redaction right independently is the thing that fails. Tail sampling, which\ndecides after the trace is complete, is chosen over head sampling *because* the traces you want are\nrare and only identifiable at the end.\n\nTwo consequences of tail sampling are easy to miss. First, \"complete\" means \"no new spans for a\nconfigured wait\", typically seconds, and all spans of a trace must reach the same collector instance\n(a trace-id-aware load balancer in front). An online judge score that arrives minutes later, or an\nagent session whose spans trickle in over an hour, is outside that window, so the sampler cannot use\nit; such signals need their own path, for example keeping all payloads in a short-retention hot store\nand promoting the ones a later score flags. Second, anything you *count* must be derived before the\nsampler drops spans. The collector's pipelines are ordered so that metrics (and token-usage series)\nare computed from the full stream, and only the trace export is sampled.\n\n### Layer 3: storage, by signal\nThree signals, three stores, chosen by cardinality and retention. **Traces** (high volume, structured,\nweeks of retention) go to a trace store. **Metrics** (low cardinality, long retention) go to a time\nseries database; a spanmetrics connector in the collector derives request rate, error rate and\nlatency histograms per model, bundle and tenant from the spans, so nobody hand-instruments counters.\nIt does not sum attribute values, so token and cost series come from a token-usage metric emitted by\nthe instrumentation (the GenAI conventions define one) or from a job over an *unsampled* copy of the\nmodel spans; a job over the tail-sampled trace store would undercount spend by roughly the fraction\nof normal traces discarded.\n**Payloads**, the prompts and completions, are the sensitive, bulky part and live in a store with\ntheir own access control and retention, referenced from spans by id. A full RAG trace with retrieved\nchunks can be tens of kilobytes; at a million requests a day that is tens of gigabytes a day\n(illustrative), which is why payload retention is a budget decision, not a default.\n\n### Layer 4: LLM-specific analysis and the evaluation loop\nLangfuse, LangSmith and Arize Phoenix sit here: a trace viewer that renders prompts and completions,\nsessions and users, prompt-version comparison, and, crucially, **scores** attached to traces: user\nfeedback, human annotation and LLM-as-a-judge results. Scores on traces are what close the loop:\nfilter traces by low score, add them to a dataset, run the candidate bundle against that dataset,\ncompare. These tools increasingly accept OpenTelemetry ingestion, so they consume the same stream\nthe collector exports rather than requiring their own instrumentation; whether to self-host or use\ntheir cloud is a data-residency decision.\n\n### Layer 5: metrics, SLOs and alerting\nDashboards and alerts consume the derived metrics: latency percentiles, error classes, tokens and\ncost per tenant and feature, and the behavioural proxies and judge aggregates from the monitoring\nlesson. Every alert links to a trace query *because* an alert without examples is a two-hour hunt.\n\n### What makes it a platform rather than a tool\nTrace context propagated through the gateway so cost attribution and debugging work end to end; one\nredaction policy; one set of mandatory attributes; one place where a judge score and a user's\nthumbs-down land on the same trace; and the ability to swap any backend by changing an exporter.","example":"Instrumenting one request with the OpenTelemetry SDK: a root span for the request, child spans for\nretrieval and the model call, GenAI attributes on the model span, and the platform's mandatory keys.\n`retrieve` and `call_model` are the application's existing functions; the exporter sends to a\ncollector whose address comes from the standard `OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT` variable. The\ncompletion preview is a custom event rather than a semantic-convention one, because the conventions'\ncontent-capture names have changed several times and content capture is opt-in.\n\n```python\nfrom opentelemetry import trace\nfrom opentelemetry.sdk.trace import TracerProvider\nfrom opentelemetry.sdk.trace.export import BatchSpanProcessor\nfrom opentelemetry.exporter.otlp.proto.http.trace_exporter import OTLPSpanExporter\n\nprovider = TracerProvider()\nprovider.add_span_processor(BatchSpanProcessor(OTLPSpanExporter()))   # -> collector, not a vendor\ntrace.set_tracer_provider(provider)\ntracer = trace.get_tracer(\"support-assistant\")\n\nMODEL = \"model-id-from-bundle\"          # pinned in the bundle, never hard-coded here\n\ndef answer(question, tenant, bundle=\"support-assistant@41\", config_version=118):\n    keys = {\"app.tenant\": tenant, \"app.bundle\": bundle,               # mandatory platform keys,\n            \"app.config_version\": config_version}                     # on every span: attributes\n    with tracer.start_as_current_span(\"answer\", attributes=keys):    # are not inherited by children\n        with tracer.start_as_current_span(\"retrieve\", attributes=keys) as s:\n            docs = retrieve(question)\n            s.set_attribute(\"retrieval.doc_count\", len(docs))\n        with tracer.start_as_current_span(f\"chat {MODEL}\", attributes=keys) as s:  # GenAI span name\n            s.set_attributes({\"gen_ai.operation.name\": \"chat\",\n                              \"gen_ai.provider.name\": \"anthropic\",     # 'gen_ai.system' in older versions\n                              \"gen_ai.request.model\": MODEL})\n            resp = call_model(MODEL, question, docs)\n            s.set_attributes({\"gen_ai.response.model\": resp.model,\n                              \"gen_ai.usage.input_tokens\": resp.usage.input_tokens,\n                              \"gen_ai.usage.output_tokens\": resp.usage.output_tokens})\n            ref = payloads.put(tenant, question, docs, resp.text)  # full payload: own store, own TTL\n            s.set_attribute(\"app.payload_ref\", ref)                 # span carries only the pointer\n            s.add_event(\"app.completion_preview\", {\"preview\": resp.text[:200]})  # redacted downstream\n        return resp.text\n```\n\nThe collector pipeline for this stream, in prose: an OTLP receiver; a redaction processor that drops\n`preview` for tenants classified as confidential; tail sampling keeping every trace with an error\nstatus or latency above the budget plus a 5% sample (illustrative) of the rest; a spanmetrics\nconnector producing per-model, per-bundle rate, error and latency series; and exporters to the trace\nstore, the metrics database and the LLM analysis tool.","tradeoffs":["OpenTelemetry keeps instrumentation vendor-neutral and composable, but vendor SDKs capture richer LLM detail with less code; the balance tips toward the SDK only for a single team with one tool and no residency constraints.","Tail sampling keeps every interesting trace at the cost of buffering whole traces in the collector, which needs memory and sticky routing; head sampling is cheap but throws away the errors you wanted.","Storing full prompts and completions makes any incident reproducible but creates a sensitive store with retention and access obligations; hashing or short previews trade debuggability for safety, and the right answer differs per tenant classification.","Deriving metrics from spans removes hand-written counters and guarantees consistency, but the cardinality of the labels you allow (tenant × bundle × model) sets the metrics bill.","Self-hosting the analysis tool keeps data in your boundary but adds an operational surface; the vendor cloud is simpler until the first customer asks where their prompts are stored."],"pitfalls":["Breaking trace context at the gateway or a queue; the symptom is cost attributed to \"unknown\" and model spans with no parent, so a slow request cannot be followed end to end.","Logging prompts as text lines in the application logger; the symptom is customer data in the log SaaS, discovered during a retention audit, with no per-tenant redaction possible.","Using user id or request id as a metric label; the symptom is the metrics database running out of memory or the vendor bill multiplying as series count explodes.","Head sampling at a flat percentage; the symptom is an error-rate alert with no example traces, because the failures were in the discarded 90%.","Mandatory attributes left optional; the symptom is a regression visible in aggregate but not attributable to a bundle or prompt version, so rollback becomes guesswork.","Judge scores stored in the eval tool without the trace id; the symptom is a quality dashboard that cannot be joined to the requests it describes.","Computing cost or volume from the sampled trace store; the symptom is attributed spend that is a small, unstable fraction of the invoice and moves whenever someone changes the sampling rate."],"when":"Build the stack, rather than adopting one tool, as soon as a second team, a second model provider or\na compliance requirement appears; before that, a single LLM-tracing tool with OpenTelemetry under it\nis enough and migrates cleanly later. Start with tracing through the gateway and the mandatory\nattributes, then the collector with redaction, then derived metrics and scores. Measure the stack by\nthe share of model spend that is attributed to a tenant and feature, the time from an alert to an\nexample trace, and whether a judge score can be joined to its request. In interviews, be ready to\ndraw the five layers and justify two choices: why the collector is where redaction and sampling\nlive, and why payloads are stored separately from spans.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Instruments model, retrieval and tool calls with the standard attributes, propagates trace context across services, and writes the collector configuration for sampling and redaction.","architect":"Chooses the wire format, the per-signal stores, the retention and redaction policy per data classification, and the mandatory attribute set every team must emit.","fde":"Connects the customer's existing APM and SIEM to the stack, agrees which payloads may be stored and for how long, and gives the customer a trace view for their own debugging."},"check":{"q":"Finance says the provider invoice is 30% higher than the sum of what each product team reports.\nCompliance separately asks whether any prompt containing a customer's account number was stored.\nWhich parts of a well-built observability stack answer each question, and what is missing if they\ncannot?","a":"The spend gap is a cost-attribution question answered by spans: every model span carries\ntokens, model and tenant and bundle attributes, and a cost series is computed from those token counts\n(a query or job over the trace store, or a token-usage metric emitted by the instrumentation, since\nspanmetrics only derives call counts and durations) priced per model and grouped by tenant and bundle. If the sum does not match the invoice, either some spans lack tenant or bundle, which means the\nmandatory attributes are not enforced, or some calls bypass the instrumented path, which means keys\nexist outside the gateway. Two quieter causes are worth checking: the cost job reads the tail-sampled\ntrace store rather than an unsampled metric, so it undercounts by design; or a client library retries\nbelow the instrumented span, so one span can stand for more than one billed call. The compliance question is answered by the collector's redaction policy\nand the payload store: if account numbers are redacted before export for the relevant classification,\nthe answer is a configuration document plus a query over the payload store for that tenant's\nretention window. If prompts were logged as plain log lines in a third-party log service, there is no\nreliable answer, which is the failure the stack exists to prevent."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does a platform route telemetry through an OpenTelemetry Collector instead of exporting directly from each service to the backend?","options":["The collector compresses traces so storage is cheaper","Redaction, sampling and fan-out are enforced in one place rather than re-implemented by every team","Backends cannot accept connections from many services at once","It is the only way to derive metrics from spans"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Each application getting redaction and sampling right independently is the thing that fails. A central collector applies policy once and can export the same stream to several backends."},{"q":"Why is tail-based sampling preferred over head-based sampling for LLM traces?","options":["It uses less memory in the collector","It samples a fixed fraction, which keeps costs predictable","The traces worth keeping (errors, low scores, slow requests) are only identifiable once the trace is complete","Head sampling is unsupported by the GenAI semantic conventions"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A decision at the start of a request cannot know it will fail or score badly. Tail sampling waits for the whole trace, keeps all of the interesting ones and a small fraction of the rest, at the price of buffering."},{"q":"Why are prompts and completions stored separately from spans and metrics?","options":["Span attributes have a hard size limit of a few bytes","They are bulky and sensitive, so they need their own retention and access control, referenced from spans by id","Metrics databases cannot store strings","Analysis tools require a dedicated payload API"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Payloads dominate volume and carry customer data; keeping them in a dedicated store lets the platform apply per-tenant retention and access rules without touching the trace or metrics stores."}]},{"key":"llmops-platform/ai-control-plane-vs-data-plane","sources":["Official docs: Kubernetes components","Official docs: Envoy xDS protocol","Official docs: Istio architecture"],"why":"The two halves of an AI platform have opposite requirements. The request path handles thousands of\ncalls a second, where every millisecond the platform adds before the model starts lands directly in\ntime-to-first-token, and must keep working through partial failures. The management side handles a few changes an\nhour: promote a bundle, change a canary percentage, add a tenant's model allow-list, rotate a key. It\nmust be consistent, audited and permissioned, and nobody cares if a change takes two seconds.\n\nMixing them fails in both directions. If the request path looks up the alias, the policy and the\nbudget in the management database on every call, the database's latency becomes part of every\nrequest's p99 and its availability becomes the platform's availability; one maintenance window on\nthe config store is a total outage. If, to avoid that, the policy is baked into the deployed service,\nevery alias move or rollout change becomes a redeploy, so rollback takes as long as a build and\nnobody can tell which configuration was live at 14:07 yesterday.\n\nThe split has a long history outside AI. Routers separate the forwarding path from the routing\nprotocols that compute the tables it uses. Kubernetes separates the API server, store and\ncontrollers from the kubelets that run pods; a cluster keeps running workloads while the API server\nis down, you just cannot change anything. Service meshes push configuration from a control plane to\nsidecar proxies over a streaming protocol and the proxies keep their last configuration if the\ncontrol plane disappears. AI platforms inherit this *because* an AI gateway is a proxy with policy,\nand model registries, prompt registries and rollout state are exactly the configuration such a\nproxy needs. The split exists so that the thing that must be reliable is simple and reads local\nstate, and the thing that must be auditable is small and changes slowly.","how":"### What lives where\nThe **control plane** is the system of record: model registry and aliases, prompt registry and\nbundle definitions, rollout state (which bundle is stable, which is the candidate, what percentage,\nwhether shadow mode is on), per-tenant and per-use-case policy (allowed models, data classification\nrouting such as \"confidential tenants only to in-region models\", budgets, rate limits), credential\nmanagement, evaluation gates and release approvals, the deprecation calendar, cost accounting and\nthe audit log of every change. It is low-QPS, strongly consistent, permissioned and boring.\n\nThe **data plane** is the request path: authenticate the caller against cached credentials, resolve\ntenant and use case, pick the bundle according to rollout state, render the prompt, enforce policy\nlocally, route to the model with fallbacks, retrieve, execute tools, run guardrail checks, cache,\nand emit telemetry. It is stateless with respect to configuration, horizontally scaled, and makes\nno synchronous call to the control plane per request. In policy terms, the control plane is the\ndecision point and the data plane is the enforcement point, with the decisions precomputed into\nconfiguration *because* a per-request decision call would put the control plane back on the hot path.\n\n### Configuration as versioned snapshots\nThe control plane publishes its state as an immutable, monotonically numbered snapshot that contains\neverything the data plane needs: aliases, bundles, rollouts, policies. Data-plane replicas pull it on\na short interval or receive it by push, verify its signature, and swap it in atomically. Whole\nsnapshots rather than per-key updates *because* a bundle and its model pin must change together; a\nreplica that applied the new prompt but not the new model for three seconds is serving a combination\nnobody evaluated. Monotonic versions *because* two replicas that fetch from two control-plane nodes\nmust never regress. Every span the data plane emits carries the snapshot version, which is what makes\n\"what was live at 14:07\" a query and makes rollout and rollback attributable.\n\n### Fail static\nIf the control plane is unreachable, the data plane keeps serving its last snapshot. Replicas also\npersist the last snapshot locally so a cold start during a control-plane outage still comes up. The\nconsequence is that during such an outage you cannot change anything, including rolling back, so the\ndesign needs a break-glass path: an operator-supplied local override snapshot that replicas accept\nwith a logged, time-limited exception. This is the same trade the meshes make: availability of\nserving over freshness of configuration.\n\nFail static is not unconditional. Most configuration (aliases, rollout percentages, prompts) is safe\nto serve stale for hours. A few entries are security decisions whose staleness is itself a breach: a\nrevoked API key, an offboarded tenant, a newly blocked model or region. Give the snapshot a\n**staleness budget**, alert when a replica's config age exceeds it, and decide per entry type what\nhappens past it: keep serving (most of the snapshot), or deny (revocations, which should also travel\non a separate, faster push channel so they do not wait for the next full snapshot).\n\n### Telemetry flows up\nThe data plane emits spans, usage and cost tagged with tenant, bundle, model and snapshot version.\nThe control plane consumes the aggregates: budgets are enforced against them, canary analysis reads\nthe candidate-versus-stable comparison from them, and a failed canary results in the control plane\npublishing a snapshot with the rollout reverted. The feedback loop is asynchronous by design;\nper-request decisions use local counters, and precise global quotas use a shared fast store that is\npart of the data plane, not the control plane.\n\n### Where the gateway fits\nAn AI gateway is a data-plane component. Its admin API, configuration database and UI are control\nplane. Many gateway products ship both in one process, which is fine for one team; at platform scale\nthe gateway runs as stateless replicas consuming snapshots, and the admin side is a separate service\nwith its own availability target, access control and audit.","example":"A minimal data-plane handler: it starts from the snapshot persisted on local disk, refreshes it in the\nbackground, keeps serving the old one when the control plane is unreachable, picks the bundle with a\nsticky canary bucket, enforces tenant policy locally, and stamps the snapshot version on telemetry.\nSignature verification is left out for brevity. `call_model` and `emit_span`\nare the application's existing functions.\n\n```python\nimport hashlib, json, os, threading, time, urllib.request\n\nLOCAL = \"/var/lib/gateway/last_snapshot.json\"\nCONF = json.load(open(LOCAL))      # last-known-good from disk, so a cold start survives an outage\n\ndef refresh(url=\"http://control-plane/config/current\", every=30):\n    \"\"\"Pull the control plane's snapshot; keep serving the old one if it is unreachable.\"\"\"\n    global CONF\n    while True:\n        try:\n            with urllib.request.urlopen(url, timeout=2) as r:\n                snap = json.load(r)\n            if snap[\"version\"] > CONF[\"version\"]:        # monotonic: a stale node never wins\n                CONF = snap                               # whole-snapshot atomic swap\n                with open(LOCAL + \".tmp\", \"w\") as f:      # write-then-rename: a crash mid-write\n                    json.dump(snap, f)                    # must not corrupt last-known-good\n                os.replace(LOCAL + \".tmp\", LOCAL)\n        except Exception as e:\n            print(f\"control plane unreachable ({e}); still serving config v{CONF['version']}\")\n        time.sleep(every)\n\nthreading.Thread(target=refresh, daemon=True).start()\n\ndef bucket(key):                                           # stable across replicas, unlike hash()\n    return int(hashlib.sha256(key.encode()).hexdigest(), 16) % 100\n\ndef resolve(c, use_case, tenant):\n    r = c[\"rollouts\"].get(use_case)      # {\"stable\": \"support@41\", \"candidate\": \"support@42\", \"percent\": 5}\n    if r and bucket(f\"{r['candidate']}:{tenant}\") < r[\"percent\"]:  # salted by candidate: each\n        return r[\"candidate\"]               # rollout picks different tenants; percent counts tenants\n    return r[\"stable\"] if r else c[\"aliases\"][use_case]\n\ndef handle(tenant, use_case, messages):\n    \"\"\"Data-plane path: every decision is a local lookup; no control-plane call per request.\"\"\"\n    c = CONF                                               # read the pointer once per request\n    bundle = resolve(c, use_case, tenant)\n    model, prompt = c[\"bundles\"][bundle][\"model\"], c[\"bundles\"][bundle][\"prompt\"]\n    allowed = c[\"policy\"].get(tenant, {}).get(\"allowed_models\")\n    if allowed is not None and model not in allowed:\n        return {\"error\": \"model not allowed for tenant\", \"config_version\": c[\"version\"]}\n    resp = call_model(model, prompt, messages)\n    emit_span(tenant=tenant, bundle=bundle, model=model, config_version=c[\"version\"], usage=resp.usage)\n    return resp\n# A canary is snapshot v+1 with percent=5; promotion sets stable=support@42, percent=0; rollback sets\n# stable=support@41. None of them redeploys this process.\n```","tradeoffs":["Snapshot-based configuration removes any per-request dependency on the control plane, but changes propagate within the refresh interval; a kill switch that must act in seconds needs a push channel or a shorter interval for a small emergency subset of the config.","Local policy enforcement is fast, but per-replica counters make budgets and rate limits approximate; precise global quotas need a shared counter in the data plane, which reintroduces a dependency you must size and protect.","Separate control and data services scale and fail independently, at the cost of two deployables and two on-call surfaces; for one team with one application, a gateway with an embedded admin store is the right size until the second team arrives.","Fail static keeps serving through control-plane outages but freezes rollbacks too; a break-glass override restores agility and must itself be audited, or it becomes the way around every policy.","Immutable, versioned snapshots give full attribution and reproducibility, but a busy platform publishes thousands of them; retention and compaction become real operational tasks."],"pitfalls":["Calling the registry or policy database on every request; the symptom is request p99 tracking the config database's latency, and a config-store maintenance window turning into a serving outage.","Hot-reloading configuration key by key; the symptom is a burst of errors or odd answers for a few seconds after each change, when a new prompt ran against the old model or tool set.","Bucketing the canary per request instead of per tenant or user; the symptom is a user flipping between bundle versions mid-conversation and support tickets nobody can reproduce.","Bucketing per tenant and reading the percentage as a traffic share; the symptom is a \"5%\" canary carrying a third of all requests because one very large tenant hashed into the canary bucket.","Using Python's built-in `hash()` for buckets; the symptom is \"sticky\" assignments that differ per replica, because string hashing is randomised per process.","Omitting the snapshot version from telemetry; the symptom is a canary analysis that cannot tell which traffic ran under which rollout percentage, and a rollback whose effect cannot be dated.","No break-glass path; the symptom is a bad bundle live during a control-plane outage with every engineer able to see the problem and nobody able to change the alias.","Treating key revocation as ordinary snapshot config; the symptom is a revoked key that keeps working on some replicas for as long as they cannot fetch a fresh snapshot."],"when":"The split is worth making explicit as soon as configuration changes more often than code deploys, or\nas soon as more than one application shares the gateway, registries and policies. Below that, a\nsingle gateway process with its own small database is fine, provided it already reads config into\nmemory rather than per request. Measure it by two numbers: the number of control-plane calls per\nrequest (target zero) and the time from an alias move to the last replica serving it. In daily work\nthe split shows up as \"promote\", \"canary to 5%\" and \"roll back\" being control-plane operations that\ntake seconds and leave an audit entry, while the data plane is only ever redeployed for code changes.\nIn interviews, expect to be asked what still works when the control plane is down and how a rollback\nhappens without a deploy.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements snapshot refresh, atomic swap, sticky bucketing and local policy checks in the gateway or application, and stamps the config version on every span.","architect":"Draws the boundary: which decisions are precomputed into snapshots, the refresh and push strategy, the fail-static and break-glass rules, and the availability target of each plane.","fde":"Deploys data-plane replicas inside the customer's network or region while the control plane stays central, and explains why their rollback is an alias move rather than a redeploy."},"check":{"q":"The control plane's database is unavailable for two hours in the middle of the day. In a\nwell-designed AI platform, what keeps working, what stops, and what does the answer tell you about\nthe design?","a":"Serving keeps working: every data-plane replica holds the last snapshot in memory and on local\ndisk, so requests resolve bundles, enforce policy and route to models as before, and new replicas can\nstill start from the persisted copy. Telemetry keeps flowing to the collector, since that is a\ndata-plane concern. What stops is change: no promotions, no canary adjustments, no new tenants, no key\nrotations, and, importantly, no rollback through the normal path. Budget enforcement degrades to\nlocal counters. If any of the serving path stopped, the design had a synchronous dependency on the\ncontrol plane that should not exist. If rollback was impossible for two hours, the design lacked a\nbreak-glass override. If nobody could later say which snapshot was live during the window, spans\nwere not stamped with the version. The outage is a test of exactly those three properties."},"quiz":[{"q":"Which of these belongs in the control plane rather than the data plane?","options":["Rendering the prompt template for a request","Running the guardrail classifier on a completion","Changing the canary percentage for a use case's candidate bundle","Choosing the fallback model after a provider timeout"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Rollout state is configuration the control plane publishes; the data plane reads it. Rendering, guardrail inference and fallback selection all happen per request on the serving path."},{"q":"Why does the data plane consume whole versioned snapshots instead of hot-reloading individual keys?","options":["Snapshots are smaller on the wire than key-by-key updates","Related settings such as a bundle's prompt and model pin must change together, and a version number makes behaviour attributable","Control-plane databases cannot stream individual changes","Hot reloading is unsupported by gateway products"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Applying keys independently creates combinations nobody evaluated for a few seconds after each change. An atomic snapshot with a monotonic version avoids that and gives every span a config id."},{"q":"What should happen when the data plane cannot reach the control plane?","options":["Reject requests, since policy cannot be verified","Fall back to a default model with no policy","Keep serving the last known snapshot and log that configuration is stale","Redeploy the data plane with configuration baked in"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Fail static is the design goal: availability of serving outranks freshness of configuration. The trade is that changes, including rollbacks, wait for the control plane or a break-glass override."}]},{"key":"llmops-platform/centralized-vs-federated-ai-platform","sources":["Skelton & Pais, 2019 — Team Topologies","Dehghani, 2022 — Data Mesh","Conway, 1968 — How Do Committees Invent?"],"why":"LLM adoption spreads faster than any previous infrastructure. A product team can be in production\nwith a provider API key and a credit card in an afternoon, so by the time anyone asks \"what AI do we\nrun\", the honest answer is a survey. That state has concrete costs: spend spread over many accounts\nthat never reach volume pricing; three home-grown gateways and four tracing setups; guardrails\nconfigured differently per team; and no way to answer a regulator's or customer's question about\nwhich models process which data. The federated default is not a decision anyone made; it is what\nhappens without one.\n\nThe reflex response is a central AI team, sometimes called a centre of excellence, that owns all\nmodel access and reviews every use case. That fails differently. The central team cannot know the\nclaims domain, the support domain and the legal domain well enough to own their prompts and quality\nbars, so quality disputes replace quality. Every request to use a model or change a prompt waits in\na queue, and the teams with the most urgent needs route around it, recreating the shadow usage the\ncentralization was meant to end. The central team, measured by what it ships, starts building\napplications for the loudest business unit and stops being a platform.\n\nThe decision exists because the two failure modes are real and opposite. Conway's law predicts the\nplatform's shape will tend to follow the organisation's communication structure, so the question is which responsibilities must be\nowned once, for the whole company, and which must stay with the people who understand the domain.\nTeam Topologies' answer is a platform team that treats the platform as a product consumed by\nstream-aligned teams; Data Mesh's answer for analytics, domain ownership with federated\ncomputational governance, translates directly to AI: domains own their prompts and evals, the\nplatform owns the rails and the rules are enforced by code rather than by review.","how":"### The two poles\nUnder **centralization** the platform team runs the gateway with all provider keys, the approved\nmodel list, the registries, the evaluation harness and judge services, the trace pipeline, baseline\nguardrails and governance artefacts such as risk classification and model cards. It often also\nbuilds the applications. Product teams request access and capacity. Under **federation** each\nproduct team or business unit picks providers, holds keys, builds or buys its own tracing and\nevaluation, sets its own quality bar, and the central function is at most a policy document and a\nquarterly review.\n\n### What must be owned once\nSome responsibilities only work if there is exactly one of them, *because* their value comes from\ncompleteness: identity and model access through a single gateway, so every call is authenticated,\nattributed and rate-limited; budgets and chargeback, so finance sees one account and the volume\ndiscount applies; the inventory and audit log, so \"which models touched this tenant's data\" is a\nquery; the telemetry pipeline with its redaction policy, so compliance holds for every team; the\ndeprecation calendar, so a provider sunset is handled once; and the approved-model list with risk\ntiers. These form the mandatory core. It is kept thin *because* every mandatory item is friction,\nand friction breeds workarounds.\n\nCentralizing keys has a consequence specific to LLM providers: rate limits and quotas are typically\nset per account or per project, so pooling everyone onto shared keys pools their quota too. One\nteam's overnight batch summarisation job can then consume the tokens-per-minute budget that a\ncustomer-facing assistant needs, and the assistant sees 429s it did nothing to cause. A central\ngateway is therefore only safe if it also owns per-team quotas and priority classes (interactive\nbefore batch), so that the shared pool is partitioned rather than first-come-first-served.\n\n### What must stay with the domain\nQuality is domain knowledge. The prompts, the golden sets, the rubrics, the retrieval corpora, the\nagent designs and the acceptance thresholds for a claims extractor can only be owned by the claims\nteam, *because* only they can judge an output. Federating these is not a concession; it is the only\nway the quality bar is meaningful. The platform supports them with a shared eval harness, judge\nservices, templates and training, offered rather than imposed.\n\n### The paved road\nBetween mandatory and federated sits the shared-but-optional layer: a reference application\ntemplate with tracing, evals and guardrails pre-wired; a managed judge; dataset tooling; golden-path\ndocumentation. The paved road works when it is easier to use than to bypass, which is a product\nrequirement on the platform team: self-service onboarding, a published SLA for requests, and a\nroadmap driven by adoption metrics rather than feature counts. Money is part of \"easier\": if the\nplatform charges teams a markup over provider prices, bypassing it becomes the cheaper path, so\ncharge back at cost (or start with showback, reporting spend without billing it) until the pooled\ndiscount makes the gateway visibly cheaper than a private key. Governance in a federated model is\n**computational**: conformance checks in CI (spans carry mandatory attributes, model pins are on the\napproved list, bundles are registered) replace review meetings, *because* review scales with\nheadcount and checks scale with code.\n\n### Mapping to control plane and data plane\nThe split often lands on the control-plane boundary: centralize the control plane (policy, keys,\ninventory, rollout state, deprecations) and allow federated data-plane deployments, so a business\nunit with residency or isolation requirements runs its own gateway replicas in its own region or\nnetwork while consuming central configuration. The reverse, federated policy with a shared gateway,\nis rarely stable.\n\n### Moving the line as the organisation grows\nWith one to three teams there is no platform, only the most mature team's stack, and the right move\nis to make that stack reusable. From a handful of teams, a shared gateway and telemetry pipeline\nbecome worth a part-time owner. Past roughly ten teams, or at the first regulated use case, a\ndedicated platform team with the mandatory core pays for itself. The signals that the line is in the\nwrong place are measurable: the share of model spend flowing through the gateway, the median time\nfrom \"we want to use a model\" to the first production call, the age of the platform's request queue,\nand the number of unregistered use cases found per audit.","example":"An insurer has 14 product teams building on LLMs (all figures illustrative). Three teams bought their\nown provider keys, two built gateways, one has an evaluation harness, and the rest copy prompts\nbetween repositories. Monthly provider spend is around $180k across seven accounts, so no account\nreaches the provider's volume tier and finance cannot attribute spend to products. Compliance asks\nwhich models process policyholder data and receives an estimate.\n\nOption A, full centralization: a six-person platform team takes over every key, builds gateway,\nregistries, harness and tracing, and reviews each team's prompts. At roughly 25 requests a month and\ntwo engineer-days each, review alone consumes about 50 of the team's 120 engineer-days, leaving\nlittle for the platform itself; the queue grows, and the two teams with deadlines keep their keys.\n\nOption B, leave it federated: each of 14 teams builds its own gateway, tracing and harness at about\nsix engineer-weeks each, roughly 84 engineer-weeks of duplicated work, with 14 guardrail\nconfigurations and still no inventory.\n\nOption C, hub and spoke: the platform team spends a quarter on the mandatory core only: gateway with\nkeys, budgets and audit; trace pipeline with redaction; approved-model list; deprecation calendar;\nand a paved-road template. Keys outside the gateway are revoked on a dated schedule. Spend\nconsolidates into one account, so even a 10% volume discount is worth about $18k a month; the\ninventory becomes a query; teams keep their prompts, evals and corpora and inherit tracing from the\ntemplate. The platform's own metrics: share of spend through the gateway (target near 100%), median\nonboarding under two days via self-service, and zero unregistered use cases at the next audit.","tradeoffs":["Centralizing model access gives pooled pricing, a complete inventory and one enforcement point, at the cost of a single point of failure and a team that can become a bottleneck; the balance tips back toward federation only when the gateway's outages or queue cost more than the duplication did.","Federated ownership of prompts and evals keeps quality with the domain but duplicates harnesses and standards; offering the harness as a paved road recovers most of the consistency without taking ownership away.","A long mandatory list guarantees compliance but breeds workarounds; every item added to the core should be justified by something that only works when it is complete.","Central chargeback is precise when all traffic flows through one gateway and meaningless when 40% bypasses it; attribution quality is a direct function of how mandatory the gateway really is.","Federated data-plane deployments satisfy residency and isolation, but each one is an operational surface the platform must support; the cost is justified by regulation, not by preference."],"pitfalls":["The platform team building applications for one business unit; the symptom is a roadmap full of that unit's features while other teams wait, and the platform itself stagnating.","Mandating the gateway without self-service onboarding; the symptom is shadow keys reappearing within a quarter, because the sanctioned path was slower than a credit card.","Federation without an inventory; the symptom is a compliance questionnaire answered by survey, and a provider sunset discovered separately by each team.","Measuring the platform by features shipped rather than adoption; the symptom is a rich platform with a third of spend bypassing it.","Centralizing prompts and golden sets; the symptom is a central team defending a quality bar for a domain it does not understand, and the domain team disowning the result.","Governance by review meeting; the symptom is a monthly committee that cannot keep up and approvals that become rubber stamps.","Pooling provider keys without per-team quotas; the symptom is a customer-facing assistant throwing 429s every night when another team's batch job runs, followed by that team asking for its own key back."],"when":"Decide explicitly as soon as the second team starts an LLM project; the cheapest moment to make the\ngateway and telemetry mandatory is before anyone has to migrate. Centralize what must be complete to\nbe useful, federate what needs domain judgement, and put everything else on the paved road. Revisit\nthe line when the number of teams, the regulatory load or the spend concentration changes. Measure\nit by spend through the gateway, time to first production call, queue age and audit findings. In\ndaily work it appears as the argument over who owns a prompt, who pays for a judge, and whether a\nteam may hold its own key. In interviews, the strong answer names the mandatory core, explains why\nquality ownership cannot be centralized, and describes computational governance instead of\ncommittees.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds on the paved-road template, registers bundles and pins approved models, and adds the CI conformance checks that make federated governance enforceable.","architect":"Draws the line between the mandatory core, the paved road and domain ownership, and defines the adoption metrics that tell the organisation when to move it.","fde":"Fits a customer deployment into their platform model, running data-plane components in the customer's boundary while respecting their central policy, keys and inventory."},"check":{"q":"After a compliance scare, a CTO proposes moving all AI work into one central team. What would\nyou centralize, what would you refuse to centralize, and how would you stop the central team from\nbecoming a bottleneck?","a":"Centralize what the scare actually needs: model access through one gateway with keys, budgets\nand audit, the telemetry pipeline with redaction, the inventory and risk classification of use cases,\nthe approved-model list and the deprecation calendar. Those answer the compliance question completely\nonly if they are complete, which is the argument for making them mandatory. Refuse to centralize\nprompts, golden sets, rubrics, corpora and agent designs, because quality judgement lives with the\ndomain and a central team owning them would produce either a queue or an unowned quality bar. To\navoid the bottleneck, run the platform as a product: self-service onboarding with a published lead\ntime, a paved-road template that is faster than building, conformance checks in CI instead of review\nmeetings, and adoption metrics such as spend through the gateway and time to first production call\nas the team's success criteria. Offer enabling engineers to embed with teams temporarily rather than\ntaking their work over."},"quiz":[{"q":"Which responsibility is strongest as a candidate for the mandatory central core?","options":["The golden datasets for each product's evaluation","Model access, keys and budgets through a single gateway","The agent design for the support assistant","The retrieval corpus for the legal team"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Access and budgets only deliver inventory, attribution and pooled pricing when they are complete, which requires a single enforcement point. Golden sets, agents and corpora need domain judgement."},{"q":"Which symptom most clearly indicates over-centralization rather than under-centralization?","options":["Compliance cannot list which models process customer data","Three teams run three different gateways","Teams with deadlines keep their own provider keys because the sanctioned path takes weeks","Provider spend is spread across many accounts"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Shadow keys reappearing despite a mandate means the central path is slower than bypassing it. The other three symptoms are what federation without a core looks like."},{"q":"What distinguishes a paved road from a mandate in a federated platform?","options":["A paved road is enforced by CI, a mandate by policy documents","A paved road applies only to new teams","A paved road is adopted because it is easier than the alternative, while a mandate is required regardless","A paved road covers infrastructure, a mandate covers prompts"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The paved road competes with building it yourself and wins on convenience; mandates are reserved for the few things that must be complete to work. Both can be checked in CI."}]}]});
